WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.754
At the end of last year, we're like,
all right, we are quite

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.881
We haven't seen the models yet.

00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:08.925
You know, we're going to write this check
for like, I don't know,

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:11.511
like $100 billion, $200 billion.

00:00:11.594 --> 00:00:14.639
I'm like, Alex, are you
sure this is going to work?

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:16.641
It's like—Cheers.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.019
Cheers.
Yeah.

00:00:19.102 --> 00:00:20.729
Cheers.
Cheers.

00:00:21.146 --> 00:00:21.813
Cheers.

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:27.360
So, give me the update on Muse.

00:00:27.485 --> 00:00:31.698
If you were looking at the third-party reports,

00:00:32.073 --> 00:00:38.580
you would see that this might be
the fastest-growing agent or AI app

00:00:39.289 --> 00:00:40.790
that I think has existed yet.

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:46.212
What are your guys' personal favorite Muse
use cases, either yours or other people's?

00:00:47.505 --> 00:00:49.257
I was actually talking about
this at dinner yesterday.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:52.719
My favorite thing is I have my Muse,
whose name is Oiler,

00:00:53.762 --> 00:00:57.265
constantly source
increasingly obscure teas

00:00:57.766 --> 00:01:01.978
from increasingly obscure vendors of tea.

00:01:02.103 --> 00:01:06.983
So at this point he's, on a weekly basis,
my Muse is buying

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:14.074
some random tea from some
farm in Taiwan or some farm in Japan

00:01:14.365 --> 00:01:16.201
and sourcing it over and bringing it back.

00:01:16.659 --> 00:01:18.328
What's your favorite Muse use case?

00:01:18.828 --> 00:01:21.331
I don't know.
I mean, I use it for a ton of stuff.

00:01:21.498 --> 00:01:22.290
I still like...

00:01:22.999 --> 00:01:26.169
My favorite both use case and probably
eval for the product

00:01:26.586 --> 00:01:28.338
is having it coach me in MMA.

00:01:28.713 --> 00:01:34.344
So I put cameras in my gym,
and I basically...

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:38.723
It watches the cameras when I'm in there,
and it gives me feedback in between

00:01:38.807 --> 00:01:42.143
the rounds, which is a very hard problem,
it turns out, because not

00:01:42.227 --> 00:01:43.603
only does it have to...

00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:50.819
It basically has to process all the video
quickly enough because you only

00:01:50.902 --> 00:01:52.570
get 1 minute in between the rounds.

00:01:52.695 --> 00:01:54.197
Oh, it coaches you between the rounds?
Yeah.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.491
I want feedback.
I want feedback on how to win.

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:57.617
You know what I mean?
It's like, come on.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:58.868
Did you have to instruct it...

00:01:58.993 --> 00:02:02.122
I've wondered about this too, because
looking at videos is

00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:03.748
a really good AI use case.

00:02:04.582 --> 00:02:07.836
It feels like you can't just give
it a multi-gigabyte video file.

00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:10.296
So do you tell it how
to slice stills out of it?

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:12.715
No, it basically...
Yeah, it pulls stills.

00:02:12.924 --> 00:02:14.968
I think it kind of figured
that out by itself.

00:02:15.343 --> 00:02:20.849
Um, it turns out that Muse
has pretty good native understanding

00:02:21.099 --> 00:02:22.600
of MMA, so that's good.

00:02:22.892 --> 00:02:23.935
So like the multi-modal—

00:02:24.018 --> 00:02:25.228
The pre-training.

00:02:25.603 --> 00:02:30.859
Yeah, someone on the team knew.
Now...

00:02:30.942 --> 00:02:32.527
Just kind of out there in the corpus.

00:02:32.735 --> 00:02:38.741
I think it's a good test of the kind
of coding ability that...

00:02:39.075 --> 00:02:41.744
Because really basically what it's doing,
it's like, you know,

00:02:41.953 --> 00:02:45.707
within the agent, I'm not seeing any code,
but it basically is writing this whole

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.917
video processing,
image processing pipeline.

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:48.334
Yes.

00:02:48.418 --> 00:02:50.420
And I'm constantly telling
it like, all right...

00:02:50.628 --> 00:02:53.423
Like, look you took 30 seconds
to give me that feedback.

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:55.592
I only have a 60-second break.
Like, you gotta go faster.

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:57.552
Just optimize yourself, optimize yourself.

00:02:58.094 --> 00:03:02.056
And it's,
I think, a good example of how stuff is

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:05.268
going to go in the future where
basically everyone is actually

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:06.603
going to be writing tons of code.

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:08.730
They're just not going to know
that they're writing code.

00:03:09.063 --> 00:03:12.025
What kind of MMA steers
does a model give you?

00:03:12.901 --> 00:03:14.944
Um, you want to see?
Yeah.

00:03:15.028 --> 00:03:16.571
Basically, let me see if
I can pull some stuff out.

00:03:16.654 --> 00:03:22.619
But basically it gives me a lot of stuff
about balance and, like, hip position.

00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:26.331
Or it's like
when you're kind of in side control here.

00:03:26.414 --> 00:03:29.000
It's just like
you didn't get this underhook

00:03:29.083 --> 00:03:30.210
in this place so you're going to...

00:03:30.293 --> 00:03:33.254
You're vulnerable to this getting
rolled over here.

00:03:33.338 --> 00:03:35.673
I'll pull up some stuff.
You guys should try this.

00:03:35.757 --> 00:03:38.593
It was definitely one of his first use
cases actually of even

00:03:38.676 --> 00:03:40.303
the initial prototypes.
Yeah.

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:40.428
Yeah.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:43.723
I mean, I did this with OpenClaw
before we even had Muse working.

00:03:43.806 --> 00:03:45.141
It was just like...

00:03:45.308 --> 00:03:49.771
Then that's why I was saying it's kind
of like become an eval for this too.

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:50.396
It's good.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:52.523
I actually didn't know that it
was an eval for the product.

00:03:52.607 --> 00:03:54.400
So noted.
Yeah.

00:03:54.484 --> 00:03:55.568
There you go.

00:03:57.278 --> 00:03:58.112
How is all this...

00:03:59.113 --> 00:04:02.867
In the model's training data,
like the fine-grained,

00:04:03.368 --> 00:04:07.705
this is what an MMA fighter
looks like when they're appropriately

00:04:07.789 --> 00:04:10.416
balanced, and this is when they're
like too much on the back foot.

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:13.753
Just, I'm wondering,
how has it consumed so many images of

00:04:14.462 --> 00:04:17.215
appropriate versus
inappropriate MMA posture?

00:04:17.382 --> 00:04:17.840
Here you can see it.

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:22.095
It just pulls out frames from it and it's
like too much on my back leg there.

00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:24.806
Yeah, but is this
the emergent intelligence?

00:04:24.931 --> 00:04:26.683
Like it does actually...

00:04:27.475 --> 00:04:30.144
Again, does it know
from seeing lots of images of MMA

00:04:30.561 --> 00:04:33.022
or does it know because it understands
the concept of where you're

00:04:33.106 --> 00:04:33.815
meant to be balancing?

00:04:33.898 --> 00:04:36.317
It's read books about MMA,
and then it sees that you're

00:04:36.401 --> 00:04:37.402
too much on the back foot, or—

00:04:37.485 --> 00:04:43.866
Well, we did train the models
with a lot of visual perception data.

00:04:44.284 --> 00:04:44.450
Yeah.

00:04:44.534 --> 00:04:47.954
So, I mean, I think that that's like
kind of making good multimodal models.

00:04:48.037 --> 00:04:52.292
I mean, this gets into like, I think,
a core approach thing that we have,

00:04:52.375 --> 00:04:55.128
which is we're training the models
end-to-end to be good

00:04:55.586 --> 00:04:56.796
personal agents, right?

00:04:56.879 --> 00:05:00.758
So like the question is why would Meta
want to build these models?

00:05:01.134 --> 00:05:04.262
First of all, I think
just superintelligence overall is going

00:05:04.345 --> 00:05:07.307
to allow us to run the company better
and build all kinds of different things.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:11.769
But for the products that we're building,
there's certain capabilities and certain

00:05:11.853 --> 00:05:17.400
mixes of skills that basically are just
much more applicable to these cases.

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:20.945
So I mean, this is one of the things
that Alex focused on from the beginning

00:05:21.029 --> 00:05:23.823
was like, all right,
we really want to make sure that our

00:05:23.906 --> 00:05:27.410
multimodal program is good because that's
disproportionately important for the types

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:29.662
of things that people use
all these services for.

00:05:29.746 --> 00:05:31.706
How do you train a good multimodal model?

00:05:32.415 --> 00:05:34.709
Is this where Meta just has an advantage
because you guys have so

00:05:34.792 --> 00:05:36.794
much image and video data?
It's very helpful.

00:05:36.794 --> 00:05:37.170
Yeah.

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:42.675
And I think ultimately, a lot of this,
it comes down to how are

00:05:42.759 --> 00:05:43.593
you focused on it, right?

00:05:43.676 --> 00:05:47.930
I think because of the fact that we wanted
to build personal superintelligence.

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:51.267
And fundamentally,
I think most people in the world really

00:05:51.601 --> 00:05:53.686
experience the world
in a highly multimodal way.

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:58.941
They like talking to other people
and listening to things and seeing videos

00:05:59.484 --> 00:06:01.110
and filming things of their own.

00:06:01.194 --> 00:06:04.781
I think most consumers have this
highly multimodal experience.

00:06:04.906 --> 00:06:09.911
And especially earlier this year,
there was actually a point of tension

00:06:09.994 --> 00:06:14.832
almost in the industry because I think so
much of the consensus view within the AI

00:06:14.916 --> 00:06:19.128
industry was that it was just all about
coding and coding was all that mattered.

00:06:19.796 --> 00:06:23.132
And I think
that for the success of a product like

00:06:23.216 --> 00:06:28.012
Muse and the success of a lot of these
consumer use cases, it really depends on

00:06:28.179 --> 00:06:30.431
the ability to be strong and multimodal.
Yeah.

00:06:30.973 --> 00:06:33.017
I think we're just kind
of scratching the surface of that.

00:06:33.101 --> 00:06:35.728
I mean, a lot of the initial theory
that we had was that,

00:06:35.853 --> 00:06:39.649
I mean, most people don't actually
like reading that much, right?

00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:43.903
So even the text-based interaction model,
I think it's something that people do.

00:06:43.986 --> 00:06:45.279
I mean, everyone texts.

00:06:46.322 --> 00:06:49.534
But when you think about stuff like
the glasses that we're working on,

00:06:49.617 --> 00:06:51.786
or even our other products that are very
visual,

00:06:52.745 --> 00:06:56.416
the glasses basically will eventually
just be a pure multimodal product, right?

00:06:56.499 --> 00:07:01.421
It's like you can let it hear what you
are listening to, and it can talk to you,

00:07:01.629 --> 00:07:03.589
and it can see what you see if you let it.

00:07:03.881 --> 00:07:06.592
And then eventually,
I don't even know if we're going to

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:08.344
have an app store on the glasses.

00:07:08.428 --> 00:07:12.306
It'll just be like a multimodal generation
that kind of creates whatever

00:07:13.266 --> 00:07:14.809
you need right in your vision.

00:07:15.476 --> 00:07:17.728
And I imagine something like that will
probably end up powering a lot

00:07:17.812 --> 00:07:20.356
of the apps that we build too.

00:07:20.982 --> 00:07:23.484
But yeah, I mean, we're still
in the early days of that.

00:07:24.902 --> 00:07:27.447
But for now, the perception
work has been really impressive.

00:07:28.698 --> 00:07:31.701
I want to ask you guys about the product
space here in AI

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:33.703
and try something out on you.

00:07:33.828 --> 00:07:37.707
If I was to describe to you, say,
with Stripe, what I think a winning

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:41.210
product looks like, it would take me pages
and pages to talk about because we have

00:07:41.294 --> 00:07:43.379
the developer experience and we have
to talk about all the international

00:07:43.463 --> 00:07:46.257
heterogeneity of payment methods.
Then we have to talk about stablecoins.

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:47.633
Like, it would take me a long time.

00:07:47.967 --> 00:07:51.804
Whereas I notice a phenomenon in AI
where it feels like the winning products

00:07:52.054 --> 00:07:56.058
collapse very quickly
onto an agreed-upon product space

00:07:56.142 --> 00:07:59.520
that everyone is pursuing,
and so transformers worked, and then

00:07:59.604 --> 00:08:02.773
like the chat agent UI really worked.

00:08:03.232 --> 00:08:04.775
And now we are seeing...

00:08:04.984 --> 00:08:09.864
You mentioned OpenClaw earlier,
the kind of OpenClaw persistent agent,

00:08:09.947 --> 00:08:11.991
has a computer, able to do things.

00:08:12.116 --> 00:08:13.868
You know, computer use out
there in the world view.

00:08:13.951 --> 00:08:15.578
We're collapsing on that product space.

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.498
But there's something about AI
where the winning products

00:08:18.998 --> 00:08:22.251
tend to be very simple,
pursued by all the companies

00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:23.878
that are taking it seriously.

00:08:24.295 --> 00:08:26.297
You could describe
on the back of a postcard...

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:27.924
Like you could describe very concisely.

00:08:28.007 --> 00:08:30.927
And obviously, implementing
them is massively complicated.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:32.803
Like, we'll get into the new model.

00:08:32.887 --> 00:08:35.932
There's, you know,
huge amounts of frontier work

00:08:36.015 --> 00:08:37.475
that goes into making a good version.

00:08:37.558 --> 00:08:42.647
But the winning brand—now you're talking
about the glasses or the future versions.

00:08:42.939 --> 00:08:48.194
But somehow there's a very small number of
clearly correct products in AI

00:08:48.778 --> 00:08:53.574
as opposed to it being this, like,
very broad and complicated product space.

00:08:54.492 --> 00:08:55.743
I kind of disagree.

00:08:55.993 --> 00:08:56.285
Sure.

00:08:57.870 --> 00:08:59.956
Well, first of all,
I think part of the theory

00:09:00.039 --> 00:09:03.751
from the beginning was that this kind
of agent or whatever form it took was

00:09:03.876 --> 00:09:09.632
going to be quite a different thing from
this text-based assistant, right?

00:09:09.715 --> 00:09:12.343
Every company has one of those.
I mean, ours is Meta AI.

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:14.804
It reaches a ton of people, but I mean,
it's like the same thing

00:09:14.887 --> 00:09:16.347
that all these companies have.

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:18.599
And
we, I think,

00:09:18.683 --> 00:09:23.229
specifically think that that is
used for very informational purposes,

00:09:23.354 --> 00:09:26.816
but people don't think about it as like a
thing that gets stuff done for them.

00:09:26.899 --> 00:09:28.651
It's retrieval,
not delegation or something.

00:09:28.734 --> 00:09:31.696
Yeah, and even like when
you say it has a computer...

00:09:31.946 --> 00:09:33.948
Yeah, but there's lots of different
ways to do that, right?

00:09:34.031 --> 00:09:39.370
You know, I think that it's only
kind of this most recent wave that

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:44.041
gives the agent a dedicated VM,
which I think is actually incredibly

00:09:44.125 --> 00:09:49.672
important for making you feel confident
that the security and privacy of your

00:09:49.755 --> 00:09:53.968
information is—it's not just like, okay,
it's not connecting to some elastic

00:09:54.051 --> 00:09:56.679
compute that just everyone uses.

00:09:57.054 --> 00:10:00.725
But my claim isn't that it's easy
to implement, nor that it's obvious—

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:02.018
I think there are going
to be a lot of things.

00:10:02.101 --> 00:10:03.102
Yeah.
Okay.

00:10:03.185 --> 00:10:04.437
You think there's going to be
a lot of different things?

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:07.148
Because again, my claim is that now
that people see Muse,

00:10:07.273 --> 00:10:12.570
everyone looks at that and says, "Oh yeah,
that's clearly how you want the AI agent

00:10:12.653 --> 00:10:15.990
product to work, and that the future AI
agent products that we'll see

00:10:16.365 --> 00:10:17.658
will be fairly Muse-shaped."

00:10:18.075 --> 00:10:19.076
So I think...

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:23.164
My take on this is,
I think we're just so early in the realm

00:10:23.289 --> 00:10:27.084
of what AI products will look like
that I think a lot of the interesting

00:10:27.209 --> 00:10:30.546
differentiation about the products kind
of gets collapsed in how

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:31.756
we view the products today.

00:10:32.006 --> 00:10:35.509
I think the traditional way in which we
wrote down and think about

00:10:35.593 --> 00:10:36.677
product specs, that is...

00:10:38.012 --> 00:10:41.724
Those aren't the relevant dimensions
and levers and axes

00:10:42.141 --> 00:10:46.562
to consider the important design
decisions on AI-native products.

00:10:47.104 --> 00:10:52.109
And so, Muse, for example, I think
so much attention was put in the design

00:10:52.318 --> 00:10:54.070
of the model for the product.

00:10:54.362 --> 00:10:57.865
And there were a lot of very intentional
decisions around, in all these situations,

00:10:57.948 --> 00:11:00.201
how exactly should the model behave?

00:11:00.368 --> 00:11:02.787
And what's the exact personality
of the model in these situations?

00:11:02.870 --> 00:11:05.539
And how should it handle these
sorts of tool-use scenarios?

00:11:05.873 --> 00:11:07.708
And these are very intentional decisions.

00:11:08.417 --> 00:11:14.298
And probably in the same way that the full
spec of how we wanted the model to behave

00:11:14.382 --> 00:11:17.176
in all these scenarios would
be an extremely long document.

00:11:17.343 --> 00:11:23.057
But because of just how we think
about products today, it's just AI.

00:11:23.808 --> 00:11:26.102
It's kind of like how
when mobile first started happening,

00:11:26.185 --> 00:11:28.354
it was like, "Oh,
it's just an app and everything is just

00:11:28.437 --> 00:11:32.525
an app." Well, it turns out there's a lot
of nuance in what exactly an app is.

00:11:33.359 --> 00:11:36.821
And we're kind of in this phase where, oh,
it's just AI, or it's just an agent,

00:11:37.196 --> 00:11:39.490
or it's just an agent with a VM
or something like that.

00:11:39.573 --> 00:11:44.412
But the interesting differentiations
we have to tease out.

00:11:44.704 --> 00:11:44.870
Okay.

00:11:44.954 --> 00:11:46.914
So you guys think I'm making the classic
mistake, which is like,

00:11:46.997 --> 00:11:49.333
my domain is complex and this domain is,
again, from a product

00:11:49.417 --> 00:11:51.335
point-of-view, it's simple.
All payment processors seem the same.

00:11:51.419 --> 00:11:53.879
Yeah, no, I really agree
with what you're saying.

00:11:54.046 --> 00:11:57.258
One thing that we talked about from early
on, is that basically

00:11:58.008 --> 00:12:00.970
there is this pattern where I think
the products are getting thinner

00:12:01.053 --> 00:12:03.305
and the model is getting to be
more of the product, right?

00:12:03.389 --> 00:12:04.682
So one of the things that we say
internally is like,

00:12:04.765 --> 00:12:05.891
the model is the product.
Yes.

00:12:05.975 --> 00:12:06.100
Right?

00:12:06.183 --> 00:12:08.185
So, and when you're talking about
an agent,

00:12:08.269 --> 00:12:10.730
a lot of the complex product work
that went into Muse

00:12:11.313 --> 00:12:14.525
is basically around the privacy
and security model for handling your data

00:12:14.608 --> 00:12:15.860
and the things that get connected to it.

00:12:15.943 --> 00:12:18.738
And that, I think, is something that we're
just very proud of that we

00:12:18.821 --> 00:12:19.613
really focused on.

00:12:19.739 --> 00:12:22.783
But that's a lot of the product
surface that we need to innovate on.

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:26.579
But the capabilities, the personality,
a lot of the stuff is like

00:12:26.954 --> 00:12:28.247
you're training into the model.

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:32.293
And whether they're the people in the lab
who are working on that or the people

00:12:32.543 --> 00:12:34.545
on Nat's team who are working
on the product,

00:12:34.962 --> 00:12:38.716
I think when Nat is thinking about what is
the key stuff that we need to make

00:12:38.799 --> 00:12:42.303
the product feel good, he's talking
about post-training the model, right?

00:12:42.386 --> 00:12:42.928
Not like...

00:12:43.262 --> 00:12:46.140
I mean, of course the UI of the product
matters too, but the most important

00:12:46.223 --> 00:12:48.809
part of the UI is the product.
Yeah, exactly.

00:12:48.934 --> 00:12:53.606
I think there's like, there's still
very viscerally, I think from a user,

00:12:53.856 --> 00:12:56.484
there's a feeling of using Muse over time.

00:12:57.067 --> 00:13:00.571
And maybe some meaningful portion
of that is just the reliability

00:13:00.654 --> 00:13:02.031
of Muse to handle certain tasks.

00:13:02.239 --> 00:13:05.326
But then another meaningful portion is
how is it proactive?

00:13:05.493 --> 00:13:07.119
And in what context is it proactive?

00:13:07.369 --> 00:13:10.372
And when it talks to you,
how do you feel about how it talks to you?

00:13:10.623 --> 00:13:13.083
And so there's clearly a lot of...

00:13:14.251 --> 00:13:17.713
And I'm starting to see more and more
of this actually show up on the X feed,

00:13:17.797 --> 00:13:20.841
which are people are starting
to tease out these, like...

00:13:20.925 --> 00:13:24.261
They're all proactive agents,
but what are the actual meaningful

00:13:24.345 --> 00:13:25.805
differences between them?

00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:28.682
And these, I think,
are the interesting product decisions,

00:13:28.766 --> 00:13:32.478
which, you know, the funny thing is how
do you specify these product decisions?

00:13:32.561 --> 00:13:36.357
Well, it's ultimately in model design
decisions, which ultimately boil down to

00:13:36.816 --> 00:13:39.944
training methods or data or evals or,
you know, all these things.

00:13:40.152 --> 00:13:40.778
Yes.

00:13:41.111 --> 00:13:44.281
As we talk about product space,
one notable Muse detail is

00:13:44.365 --> 00:13:45.658
that there is no model selector.

00:13:46.033 --> 00:13:48.410
and no effort selector.

00:13:49.119 --> 00:13:52.414
Do you guys think that's just obviously
correct in a consumer AI product, or—

00:13:52.665 --> 00:13:53.874
I just want to make it simple.

00:13:54.250 --> 00:13:55.751
Yeah.
I think that we've been...

00:13:57.962 --> 00:14:00.881
I think somewhat infamously,
a lot of the AI products have been

00:14:01.632 --> 00:14:07.930
designed by a highly technical group
that do not have these sort of randomly

00:14:08.013 --> 00:14:10.975
sampled American in their mental model.

00:14:11.517 --> 00:14:12.643
And so the products...

00:14:13.060 --> 00:14:18.899
I mean, the naming of some of the models
is like so egregious.

00:14:19.024 --> 00:14:19.817
The numbers go backwards?

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:22.570
Some of the times the numbers go backwards
and sometimes it's just like a totally

00:14:22.653 --> 00:14:24.655
different word and you
have another set of words.

00:14:24.738 --> 00:14:27.032
And like, I just like,
how is somebody who's not

00:14:27.741 --> 00:14:32.830
carefully reading
API design specs supposed to know

00:14:32.913 --> 00:14:35.499
what they're supposed to pick?
Like, it's just far too confusing.

00:14:35.583 --> 00:14:37.918
Okay, we're talking about models a lot.
Tell me about the new model.

00:14:38.002 --> 00:14:38.961
It sounds awesome.

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:41.589
Yeah, I mean, I think
we're quite proud of this.

00:14:41.672 --> 00:14:45.718
Um, yeah, the new model—Muse Wonder—it's
a new class of model

00:14:45.926 --> 00:14:47.011
that is at the frontier.

00:14:47.094 --> 00:14:49.096
So we've basically been working
on this since the beginning.

00:14:49.179 --> 00:14:53.601
Basically, when you're doing these
pre-trains, you don't just go to whatever

00:14:53.684 --> 00:14:55.477
the biggest cluster is that you have.

00:14:55.561 --> 00:14:59.106
You want to work your way up the ladder
training models that are

00:14:59.648 --> 00:15:04.612
maybe 4 or 5 times bigger, and then you
just keep on repeating that process.

00:15:05.446 --> 00:15:11.368
So the first set of models that we've had
Muse Spark was all based on this initial

00:15:11.452 --> 00:15:13.454
pre-training that we did in,
what was it, December?

00:15:14.121 --> 00:15:15.497
Yeah, the end of last year.
Yeah.

00:15:15.581 --> 00:15:16.916
And, and it's actually...

00:15:16.999 --> 00:15:21.462
I think we've all just been
quite surprised and impressed with how

00:15:21.545 --> 00:15:25.966
much intelligence and capability
the team has been able to build

00:15:26.050 --> 00:15:30.054
into a model which is like
frankly not that big, right?

00:15:30.137 --> 00:15:33.682
I mean, and then after that we were
working on this next model,

00:15:33.974 --> 00:15:36.894
and the pre-training has been done
for a bit, and we've been working

00:15:36.977 --> 00:15:39.396
on the post-training, and now it is here.

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:42.358
So it's the next class of model.

00:15:42.483 --> 00:15:44.276
It is significantly bigger.

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:48.739
So I think you you will feel that in terms
of the nuance in which it can think.

00:15:50.032 --> 00:15:50.658
And it really.

00:15:50.741 --> 00:15:55.079
A lot of the areas that we focused on were
making it the best personal

00:15:55.162 --> 00:15:57.081
and professional agentic model.

00:15:57.206 --> 00:16:01.710
Some of the different things that you can
optimize for that's it for us, and then

00:16:02.378 --> 00:16:06.757
yeah, a lot of the basic capabilities
that we focused on building in.

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:11.804
Very good memory, very good ability to
persist through complicated tasks.

00:16:11.887 --> 00:16:15.557
But a ton of work went into this across
the lab and the product

00:16:15.641 --> 00:16:16.976
teams and all of this.

00:16:17.309 --> 00:16:21.313
And I think it's also just sort
of a testament to the the team being able

00:16:21.397 --> 00:16:24.858
to methodically figure out
the process to scale these models.

00:16:24.942 --> 00:16:28.320
So I think this is also just, you know,

00:16:30.155 --> 00:16:33.158
a milestone on the way towards the time

00:16:33.242 --> 00:16:36.370
between each subsequently bigger class
of models, I think will

00:16:36.453 --> 00:16:37.955
now start to compress.

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:40.499
And that's very exciting for the type
of stuff that we can build.

00:16:40.582 --> 00:16:42.126
At Meta or in the industry generally?

00:16:42.418 --> 00:16:43.127
At Meta.

00:16:43.252 --> 00:16:46.714
I mean, I think you're seeing
something like this everywhere else, but—

00:16:46.797 --> 00:16:48.424
Is this an RSI effect or something else?

00:16:49.049 --> 00:16:51.593
Well, I think at Meta,
part of this is just...

00:16:52.219 --> 00:16:53.053
We needed to...

00:16:53.512 --> 00:16:56.432
Well, I think quite famously,
we were not happy with the trajectory

00:16:56.515 --> 00:16:57.891
that we were on with Llama 4.

00:16:58.058 --> 00:16:59.852
We kind of like rebuilt the whole team.

00:16:59.935 --> 00:17:02.980
That's been this kind of crazy thing over
the last year and a half that we should

00:17:03.063 --> 00:17:04.481
probably spend a bunch of time on.

00:17:04.565 --> 00:17:04.815
Oh, we'll get to that.
Yeah, yeah.

00:17:04.898 --> 00:17:06.025
Some war stories.

00:17:06.608 --> 00:17:11.071
But the team basically had to go build up
like different tiers

00:17:11.155 --> 00:17:13.699
of models to get to this one.

00:17:14.158 --> 00:17:16.910
And
now that we are kind of at the frontier,

00:17:17.494 --> 00:17:21.498
now everything that we do going forward
will just be pushing the frontier,

00:17:21.582 --> 00:17:25.085
as opposed to there's a lot of background
work that we needed to do before that.

00:17:25.169 --> 00:17:27.504
I don't know, what would you say?
Yeah.

00:17:27.588 --> 00:17:29.548
Well, I think we're
really excited about it.

00:17:29.631 --> 00:17:35.471
We will have 2 models in this series
that are going to be available.

00:17:35.554 --> 00:17:39.141
So there's Muse Wonder
and Muse Wonder Full.

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:41.310
Laugh?
Yeah, all right, there you go.

00:17:41.393 --> 00:17:43.979
Yeah, very good.
Uh, which is basically a much higher—

00:17:44.063 --> 00:17:45.314
Studio band version.

00:17:45.397 --> 00:17:47.441
Yeah, no, a much higher-end model.

00:17:47.524 --> 00:17:49.777
It's the full version of Muse 1.

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:51.320
I know, I got it.
Yeah, yeah, it's not—

00:17:51.403 --> 00:17:53.739
It's 2 words.
Wonder full version.

00:17:56.408 --> 00:17:57.409
You're clearly a father.

00:17:57.576 --> 00:17:58.577
What's up?

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:00.788
This is an excellent dad joke.
Yeah, there you go.

00:18:00.871 --> 00:18:01.830
Um, and, and the performance...

00:18:01.914 --> 00:18:05.834
I mean, we've seen like incredible
performance across a slate of evaluations

00:18:05.918 --> 00:18:08.212
on personal, professional agent tasks.

00:18:08.295 --> 00:18:10.339
It's state-of-the-art
based on our measurement.

00:18:10.547 --> 00:18:14.218
And then there's a number of, I think,
specific highlights which we're

00:18:14.301 --> 00:18:15.260
really excited about.

00:18:15.344 --> 00:18:17.638
Which we've designed
into the model over time.

00:18:17.888 --> 00:18:21.892
So active memory, like Mark mentioned,
is one area where

00:18:22.601 --> 00:18:26.688
we think that to build personal agents,
personal professional agents,

00:18:26.772 --> 00:18:31.485
like memory is just one of the clear
requirements to build reliable agents

00:18:31.693 --> 00:18:33.654
and is one of the areas where we've
invested, I think,

00:18:33.737 --> 00:18:36.990
disproportionately actually compared
to many of the other efforts.

00:18:37.074 --> 00:18:40.410
And we have our own internal evals
of Memory Bench which demonstrate

00:18:40.494 --> 00:18:41.995
a lot of the capabilities there.

00:18:42.913 --> 00:18:44.998
What does memory mean
in a model to a layperson?

00:18:46.250 --> 00:18:47.501
Is it just how much stuff it can...

00:18:47.584 --> 00:18:50.337
Like the working memory of if you're
working on a task, how much stuff

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:52.172
the model can kind of keep in its—

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:54.383
Yeah.
How's it different from context?

00:18:54.466 --> 00:18:57.094
I think it's in the experience
of using the model.

00:18:57.386 --> 00:19:01.598
How well does it remember things that you
mentioned to it over days, weeks,

00:19:01.682 --> 00:19:05.018
or months, and then leverage those
in the process of doing future tasks?

00:19:05.102 --> 00:19:05.352
I see.

00:19:05.519 --> 00:19:07.855
This is the stuff that's
buggy AF in OpenClaw.

00:19:08.147 --> 00:19:10.607
Like, I think we're all
extremely fond of OpenClaw.

00:19:11.150 --> 00:19:15.070
Very happy OpenClaw user,
but it ultimately does this compaction

00:19:15.154 --> 00:19:18.323
where it has like the long-term memory and
the working short-term memory or whatever.

00:19:18.407 --> 00:19:21.994
And I think once people use OpenClaw for a
while, they find the memory pretty buggy.

00:19:22.286 --> 00:19:22.411
Yeah.

00:19:22.494 --> 00:19:27.541
So we've designed basically both the model
and a system for the model to use

00:19:27.708 --> 00:19:30.961
to develop a much stronger form of memory.
We call it active memory.

00:19:31.253 --> 00:19:31.628
Yeah.

00:19:32.045 --> 00:19:34.882
In addition,
as Mark mentioned on the persistence—

00:19:35.007 --> 00:19:37.509
But so this is outside the model,
like the model exists and it's—

00:19:37.593 --> 00:19:39.928
No, no, but some of the capabilities
that you're talking about,

00:19:40.012 --> 00:19:43.140
like the compaction,
are things that the model does, right?

00:19:43.223 --> 00:19:45.684
So basically being able
to do this well, both with—

00:19:45.767 --> 00:19:47.561
It's not a harness,
it's in the model itself.

00:19:47.895 --> 00:19:50.230
Yes, it's in the model itself.
Exactly, yeah.

00:19:50.814 --> 00:19:55.485
So it's aware of its memory
as a core system.

00:19:55.819 --> 00:19:59.865
And it's about how it's able to leverage
various sources to be able to

00:20:00.949 --> 00:20:03.952
conduct tasks in the future,
as well as understand its ability to do

00:20:04.036 --> 00:20:06.997
compaction, how it should do that
compaction most effectively, et cetera.

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:07.164
Okay.

00:20:07.247 --> 00:20:09.958
But this is a big deal because I think
a lot of people's early experience

00:20:10.042 --> 00:20:12.836
of AI agents has been buggy memory.

00:20:13.128 --> 00:20:14.755
And so, yeah, improving that is—

00:20:14.922 --> 00:20:17.257
And to be clear, there's still
plenty of room to improve.

00:20:17.341 --> 00:20:20.219
Our evaluations show this,
but this is something that we're quite

00:20:20.302 --> 00:20:22.554
proud of, and we think this
is a meaningful contribution.

00:20:22.638 --> 00:20:23.847
Okay.
So active memory is one thing.

00:20:23.931 --> 00:20:24.640
Yeah.
Active memory.

00:20:24.723 --> 00:20:28.810
Active focus is another, which is
effectively just the ability for the model

00:20:28.894 --> 00:20:31.521
to,
when conducting tasks,

00:20:31.605 --> 00:20:34.274
be able to carry them through,
especially as we've seen

00:20:34.733 --> 00:20:39.029
Muse users as well as Muse Code users,
give  higher and higher complexity

00:20:39.238 --> 00:20:41.782
and more and more ambiguous and more
and more complex tasks,

00:20:42.032 --> 00:20:45.118
we want the model to be able to conduct
those and do those very effectively.

00:20:45.202 --> 00:20:48.413
And this is one of the things actually
in early dogfooding and testing

00:20:48.789 --> 00:20:52.251
that a lot of people have noticed about
the model that is sort of special

00:20:52.376 --> 00:20:55.837
and stands out with respect to other
models that they've tried or used.

00:20:57.172 --> 00:20:59.633
The third one is domain expertise.

00:20:59.716 --> 00:21:01.635
What is focus in AI terms?

00:21:01.718 --> 00:21:04.513
Is it like putting a lot
of compute on the task?

00:21:04.596 --> 00:21:07.307
Is it not getting distracted with other—

00:21:08.183 --> 00:21:09.893
Kind of, persistence?
Yeah, like persistence.

00:21:10.018 --> 00:21:14.022
It's basically like the ability to conduct
complete tasks over very long horizons.

00:21:14.106 --> 00:21:14.439
Okay.

00:21:14.523 --> 00:21:19.820
And then when faced with various setbacks
along the way,

00:21:20.237 --> 00:21:24.074
the ability to identify other solutions
to those problems in an aligned way.

00:21:24.825 --> 00:21:25.742
Okay.

00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:27.119
Then there's...

00:21:27.452 --> 00:21:31.248
It's actually
a big step up in coding capabilities,

00:21:31.331 --> 00:21:32.374
so frontier coding capabilities.

00:21:32.457 --> 00:21:37.421
So it is actually state-of-the-art on
DeepSWE and, SWE-Marathon

00:21:38.422 --> 00:21:41.842
and does very well on other benchmarks
like Terminal-Bench 4 and many others.

00:21:41.967 --> 00:21:45.429
So we think that users who
use it for coding,

00:21:45.637 --> 00:21:49.558
which will be a minority of all of our
users, but the ones who do will see

00:21:49.641 --> 00:21:51.310
a big improvement and a big step up.

00:21:51.685 --> 00:21:55.939
Okay, so we should also just be using
Muse Wonder Full for coding at Stripe?

00:21:56.440 --> 00:21:57.524
Potentially, yeah.

00:21:57.607 --> 00:21:59.276
I mean, let's go out there and—

00:21:59.359 --> 00:22:01.570
But like if it's state
of the art on SWE-bench, yeah.

00:22:01.653 --> 00:22:02.279
We'd be missing out.

00:22:02.362 --> 00:22:04.239
Yeah, no, it's a big
improvement on coding.

00:22:04.364 --> 00:22:07.909
I mean, obviously,
we want people to try it and see how they

00:22:07.993 --> 00:22:11.330
experience it, but we've been very
pleased in our own internal testing.

00:22:12.164 --> 00:22:12.998
Okay, so this is—

00:22:13.081 --> 00:22:14.624
Yeah, I think people will
like it for a lot of things.

00:22:14.708 --> 00:22:19.421
Okay, so this is both just your latest
frontier model, like people can come,

00:22:19.504 --> 00:22:22.215
you know, developers can come directly
to you, they can use it on OpenRouter,

00:22:22.299 --> 00:22:26.845
like they can just consume it as a model,
and it's also a big upgrade

00:22:27.679 --> 00:22:33.018
inside of Muse that people will
just feel as active users of Muse.

00:22:33.560 --> 00:22:33.727
Yeah.

00:22:33.810 --> 00:22:36.646
Well, what we're going to do in Muse,
because it's a lot bigger and it's more

00:22:36.730 --> 00:22:43.528
expensive to serve, we're going to start
off primarily in paid subscriptions.

00:22:43.737 --> 00:22:45.822
So what we want to do with Muse as
a product, we want it to be

00:22:45.906 --> 00:22:47.240
free for everyone to use.

00:22:47.741 --> 00:22:50.619
So we have this huge amount of free tokens
that people can use with

00:22:51.286 --> 00:22:52.537
with Muse Spark as the model.

00:22:53.205 --> 00:22:58.502
But if you want to use Wonder
for most of the hard tasks, then

00:23:00.253 --> 00:23:02.714
at least for now,
you'll use the paid version.

00:23:03.173 --> 00:23:06.760
But, you know, I mean, eventually we'll,
you know how quickly the field moves.

00:23:06.843 --> 00:23:06.927
Yes.

00:23:07.010 --> 00:23:09.763
And we hope to, you know,
at some point not too far from now,

00:23:09.888 --> 00:23:13.767
have a model that is as good as Wonder,
but in the Spark form factor.

00:23:14.267 --> 00:23:17.604
And that will be a great thing to be able
to serve hundreds of millions

00:23:17.687 --> 00:23:18.772
of people around the world.

00:23:18.855 --> 00:23:20.690
Okay, so you go to Muse,
you subscribe to the—what's it

00:23:20.774 --> 00:23:22.734
called—like the premium or pro plan, or...

00:23:22.818 --> 00:23:24.361
Pay money.
Yeah.

00:23:24.444 --> 00:23:26.446
And just don't stop until you've
taken out your credit card.

00:23:26.530 --> 00:23:28.490
Yeah.
And then you'll be able to...

00:23:28.573 --> 00:23:30.826
You'll select it, or it'll just magically—
Oh, it just gets better.

00:23:30.909 --> 00:23:32.536
It just gets better for you.
Yeah.

00:23:32.619 --> 00:23:39.543
And then it'll also be available via API,
on the Meta Model API and OpenRouter.

00:23:40.210 --> 00:23:45.006
And then it'll also be
available for Muse Code users.

00:23:46.174 --> 00:23:50.429
And then Muse Wonder Full will be
available to subscribers of Muse Code.

00:23:51.555 --> 00:23:53.723
So it's pretty exciting.

00:23:53.807 --> 00:23:56.893
I think we want to, as Mark mentioned,
it is a much larger model.

00:23:56.977 --> 00:23:59.604
And so we're working through
how to serve that most effectively

00:23:59.688 --> 00:24:00.981
to as many people as possible.

00:24:01.064 --> 00:24:03.859
But we're excited to get it in the hands
of as many people as possible.

00:24:03.984 --> 00:24:05.026
It's a big step forward.

00:24:05.235 --> 00:24:07.571
I mean, this is like,
and we really with Muse Spark,

00:24:07.779 --> 00:24:10.907
we were very happy with the performance
that we were able to deliver as

00:24:11.032 --> 00:24:11.950
we were getting up to this point.

00:24:12.033 --> 00:24:16.997
But like, this is, the beginning of, like,
MSL kind of being at the frontier.

00:24:18.081 --> 00:24:20.709
We feel like the models are quite
competitive with the best

00:24:20.792 --> 00:24:24.087
models that are out there.
And I think that this is—

00:24:24.629 --> 00:24:30.719
Okay, so you think it's like Astra, Fable,
and Wonder altogether at the frontier?

00:24:31.219 --> 00:24:33.388
I mean, you can look at it
empirically by the benchmarks.

00:24:33.472 --> 00:24:35.765
Empirically by the benchmarks,
one would conclude that.

00:24:35.891 --> 00:24:36.266
Yes.
I see.

00:24:36.433 --> 00:24:36.683
Yes.

00:24:37.142 --> 00:24:41.313
And I think actually one of the
things that we're especially proud of is

00:24:41.771 --> 00:24:47.027
we have taken the time,
as we've mentioned, to optimize models

00:24:47.194 --> 00:24:49.154
for the product form factor of Muse.

00:24:49.237 --> 00:24:51.698
So this is the state-of-the-art model.

00:24:51.781 --> 00:24:55.076
We think it really is the best model
in the world for the use case

00:24:55.160 --> 00:24:56.745
of personal professional agents.

00:24:56.995 --> 00:24:59.748
Yeah, people inside companies like
complaining about Goodhart's Law.

00:24:59.956 --> 00:25:02.792
You know, a metric that becomes
a target ceases to become a good metric.

00:25:03.376 --> 00:25:07.297
You hear complaints that models are
benchmaxed and that at a certain point,

00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:11.635
kind of overfitting to the benchmarks
actually makes the models slightly worse.

00:25:12.552 --> 00:25:15.222
As people who actually have
to train AI models, is that true?

00:25:15.514 --> 00:25:17.891
Are the benchmarks pretty
good at grading models?

00:25:17.974 --> 00:25:20.936
Do you start to see weird
edge failure cases from the—

00:25:21.019 --> 00:25:22.687
The benchmarks have to keep moving, right?

00:25:22.771 --> 00:25:26.983
Because it happens very quickly,
but they just get saturated so quickly.

00:25:27.067 --> 00:25:28.443
So, I mean, I do think that—

00:25:28.527 --> 00:25:31.279
But you think non-saturated
benchmarks are fine?

00:25:32.113 --> 00:25:37.661
I mean, as we've gone on, we steer the
models more towards our internal

00:25:37.869 --> 00:25:39.955
benchmarks for the use
cases that we're focused on.

00:25:40.372 --> 00:25:42.749
So,
I mean, early on when we were kind of just

00:25:43.375 --> 00:25:45.627
doing the science of trying to climb
the ladder,

00:25:46.086 --> 00:25:48.964
we cared more about what the external
benchmarks were just to make

00:25:49.047 --> 00:25:50.465
sure we were on the right track.

00:25:50.549 --> 00:25:53.385
And then I'd say,
you know, each release that goes by,

00:25:54.094 --> 00:25:57.847
the larger share of what we're
looking at are internal ones.

00:25:58.431 --> 00:26:02.602
And one of the things that we actually
quantified in our research blog post as

00:26:02.686 --> 00:26:07.148
part of Muse Wonder is
this metric for big model smell,

00:26:07.232 --> 00:26:10.193
which is kind of, you know,
big model smell and feeling of benchmaxing

00:26:10.277 --> 00:26:11.194
are kind of like inverses of one another.

00:26:11.278 --> 00:26:13.572
This idea that you could just like tell
when you're using a really

00:26:13.655 --> 00:26:15.156
big model, it just...
You can tell.

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:16.950
It just has nuance.
It's got that je ne sais quoi.

00:26:17.033 --> 00:26:17.284
Yeah.

00:26:17.367 --> 00:26:20.370
And so we, the way we quantify that is
actually almost explicitly what you're

00:26:20.453 --> 00:26:23.498
mentioning, which is the gap between
in-distribution performance

00:26:23.582 --> 00:26:26.084
and out-of-distribution performance.
So what is the...

00:26:26.167 --> 00:26:29.713
We kind of measure
how well does the model perform on tasks

00:26:29.796 --> 00:26:33.675
that are in distribution of our RL
and post-training versus tasks

00:26:33.758 --> 00:26:35.218
that are out of distribution.

00:26:35.343 --> 00:26:37.137
And what does that gap look like for...

00:26:37.846 --> 00:26:40.890
What does that gap look like between the
Muse Spark line and the Muse Wonder line?

00:26:40.974 --> 00:26:44.477
And we actually see this huge step up
from the Muse Spark to the Muse Wonder

00:26:44.561 --> 00:26:47.731
line because of, as Mark mentioned, like,
it's just a much better base

00:26:47.814 --> 00:26:49.441
model powering this new line.

00:26:49.608 --> 00:26:52.277
And there's
an entire je ne sais quoi to that,

00:26:52.360 --> 00:26:55.614
but I think we've been able
to actually quantify this effect.

00:26:56.031 --> 00:26:59.826
And it's an obviously dimension that we're
excited to continue improving on.

00:27:00.201 --> 00:27:03.872
But if you're saying that
external benchmarks have ceased to become

00:27:03.955 --> 00:27:07.000
as important, and you care much
more about your internal benchmarks.

00:27:07.459 --> 00:27:10.837
Is that kind of an argument against
benchmaxing, that you think that

00:27:11.421 --> 00:27:14.090
just trying to get the top score
on the external benchmarks that everyone

00:27:14.174 --> 00:27:17.344
talks about is maybe not going
to lead you to develop a great model?

00:27:17.844 --> 00:27:20.221
Well, yeah, but also we're
focused on specific things.

00:27:20.680 --> 00:27:24.643
I mean, part of the whole strategy is
personal superintelligence and just trying

00:27:24.726 --> 00:27:27.729
to build different types of products
than other people are trying to build.

00:27:27.854 --> 00:27:30.982
So even if we did have the exact
same evals as everyone else.

00:27:31.358 --> 00:27:32.192
It just—

00:27:32.817 --> 00:27:32.984
Yes.

00:27:33.068 --> 00:27:35.654
And though I would say a personal
superintelligence is not like a super

00:27:35.779 --> 00:27:38.573
niche interest,
like it's probably something that other

00:27:38.657 --> 00:27:40.116
models want to be able to do.

00:27:40.992 --> 00:27:43.703
I think that there are things
that are very different about it.

00:27:44.120 --> 00:27:47.123
I mean, I think like the personality
probably matters more.

00:27:47.248 --> 00:27:51.211
I mean, one of the things that we talk
about is this idea of discretion, right?

00:27:51.294 --> 00:27:54.214
So if you want to like
use the model to get something done,

00:27:55.048 --> 00:27:59.010
It needs to be able to know a lot about
you and get things done in the world

00:27:59.511 --> 00:28:01.888
while disclosing as little as possible.

00:28:01.971 --> 00:28:04.849
So, you know,
if you're— you have an allergy or you're

00:28:04.933 --> 00:28:06.851
pregnant or something, you're trying to,
like, get a,

00:28:06.935 --> 00:28:09.521
you know, like, a table at a restaurant
and you don't want to, like,

00:28:09.604 --> 00:28:11.856
have to tell them your medical condition.
Yes, yeah.

00:28:12.023 --> 00:28:14.901
You know, to make sure that it's
going to be a good place for you.

00:28:14.984 --> 00:28:16.152
Like, okay, how do you handle that?

00:28:16.236 --> 00:28:20.323
And I mean, humans have common sense and
the models so far

00:28:20.740 --> 00:28:24.744
If you're building a coding agent that
works in an environment where everyone has

00:28:24.828 --> 00:28:28.581
access to the same context,
discretion isn't particularly important.

00:28:28.665 --> 00:28:32.919
So there are all these things that I think
are just kind of more important for us.

00:28:33.294 --> 00:28:33.545
Yes.

00:28:34.546 --> 00:28:37.465
Aspects of coding where
the code needs to work,

00:28:37.549 --> 00:28:41.636
but you don't— the consumers who are
using it may not even ever see the code.

00:28:41.720 --> 00:28:45.265
I mean, that probably matters more
in a personal superintelligence context

00:28:45.473 --> 00:28:47.517
rather than serving software developers.

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:50.061
So I think these things
are just a bit different.

00:28:50.478 --> 00:28:50.645
Yeah.

00:28:50.729 --> 00:28:52.814
Ultimately, we care about
making useful models.

00:28:52.939 --> 00:28:56.067
So we want models when
you put them into people's hands,

00:28:56.443 --> 00:28:58.403
they have a great experience using.

00:28:58.653 --> 00:29:02.657
And I think that is the most important
metric for any model that we

00:29:02.741 --> 00:29:03.700
or other people produce.

00:29:04.117 --> 00:29:08.913
I think that really focusing
on the external benchmarks,

00:29:09.414 --> 00:29:12.333
if you really dig into the supply chain
a little bit, it's some

00:29:12.876 --> 00:29:17.797
PhD student who made these benchmarks,
a benchmark as part of their,

00:29:18.465 --> 00:29:20.133
as the course of them doing their PhD.

00:29:20.216 --> 00:29:24.429
And so you're kind of outsourcing all
of your product thinking to that PhD

00:29:24.512 --> 00:29:26.431
student who happened
to make a viral benchmark.

00:29:26.514 --> 00:29:28.057
Right.
So clearly that's wrong.

00:29:28.183 --> 00:29:30.018
Yeah.
And so that's why we care a lot.

00:29:30.351 --> 00:29:34.063
A huge part of what a lot of people
at Meta and MSL are focused

00:29:34.147 --> 00:29:36.691
on is crafting amazing evals.

00:29:36.775 --> 00:29:38.693
And now that we have them,
we can work on our own.

00:29:38.818 --> 00:29:41.905
But if you're building a you're
ramping up your lab, right?

00:29:41.988 --> 00:29:45.450
It's like those public ones are not
a crazy place to start, but it's

00:29:45.533 --> 00:29:46.618
just not where you want to end up.

00:29:46.701 --> 00:29:48.828
You're just at that point,
you're just looking for signs of life.

00:29:48.912 --> 00:29:52.707
But then when you're like really in this
mode, as we are now, where it's like,

00:29:53.082 --> 00:29:55.627
you know,
we want to be very intentional about how

00:29:55.710 --> 00:29:59.798
we're improving our model over time to
build better and better, better products.

00:29:59.881 --> 00:30:02.550
Like a lot of the cases that Mark just
mentioned, like these are

00:30:03.134 --> 00:30:07.472
actually detailed very rigorously in like
the spreadsheet of all the little

00:30:07.555 --> 00:30:11.810
capabilities and nuances of the product
that we think really

00:30:11.893 --> 00:30:13.102
matter to the end user.

00:30:13.186 --> 00:30:16.231
And maybe each of them matters like 2%
to the user, but in aggregate,

00:30:16.523 --> 00:30:21.069
that's the obsession that will ultimately
result in these models that feel magical.

00:30:21.945 --> 00:30:24.739
Can you guys describe for me just
what goes into training an RL model?

00:30:24.823 --> 00:30:27.075
Or sorry, a leading-edge frontier model?

00:30:27.325 --> 00:30:30.370
I'm just curious
in that I think a lot of people listening

00:30:30.453 --> 00:30:34.040
to this have a rough idea where there's
there's a pre-training

00:30:34.123 --> 00:30:34.916
and a post-training step.

00:30:34.999 --> 00:30:38.127
So pre-training is you kind
of ingest this huge amount of data.

00:30:38.253 --> 00:30:40.380
You look at a lot of pictures
and these are cats and these are dogs.

00:30:40.463 --> 00:30:44.509
And after a while you start to develop
a sense for what is catness and what is

00:30:44.592 --> 00:30:47.303
dogness and what is fur
and everything like that.

00:30:47.720 --> 00:30:50.473
And so that's kind of the pre-training
step where you just hoover up data

00:30:50.557 --> 00:30:52.684
and maybe you can talk about the data
acquisition and just kind

00:30:52.767 --> 00:30:53.935
of what goes into that.

00:30:54.102 --> 00:31:00.942
And then there's this RL
stage, which is the newish advance

00:31:01.025 --> 00:31:02.861
over kind of the past few years of AI.

00:31:03.319 --> 00:31:07.740
And again, it sounds like that is where
a lot of the specific nuance of this model

00:31:07.824 --> 00:31:12.912
has come in, where you guys have chosen
better domains,

00:31:15.123 --> 00:31:17.959
better hills to climb for the model.

00:31:18.334 --> 00:31:21.754
But just at a high level,
describe how many people,

00:31:21.838 --> 00:31:25.174
how long it takes, what the steps are,
how you think about those in—

00:31:26.301 --> 00:31:27.886
How much time do you have?
Yeah, at a high level.

00:31:27.969 --> 00:31:32.056
This happens to be
What we spend all of our time on.

00:31:32.140 --> 00:31:34.017
Exactly.
And so summarizing—

00:31:34.517 --> 00:31:35.685
YouTube courses.

00:31:36.269 --> 00:31:39.981
Yeah, I think at a very high level,
these are the stages.

00:31:40.064 --> 00:31:41.774
It's pre-training and post-training.

00:31:42.525 --> 00:31:46.404
Ironically, training is the
superset of all of it.

00:31:46.779 --> 00:31:47.113
But that's—

00:31:47.196 --> 00:31:49.490
so how many people are on this whole
effort, like when you

00:31:49.574 --> 00:31:50.241
develop these models?

00:31:50.325 --> 00:31:54.329
So this, I think you're getting into
what is interesting about MSL

00:31:54.829 --> 00:31:58.625
and the approach that we've taken,
which is, I mean, From the very beginning,

00:31:58.708 --> 00:32:01.294
I think when I first reached out to you,
I think we were just looking at this

00:32:01.377 --> 00:32:04.297
because we were like, just like,
wow, okay, how far we've come.

00:32:04.380 --> 00:32:06.174
And I was just like,
I pulled up the screenshot of like

00:32:06.257 --> 00:32:07.926
when I first reached out to Alex.

00:32:08.009 --> 00:32:10.762
My first thing was like, hey,
do you have time to catch up and talk?

00:32:10.845 --> 00:32:12.055
And then it's like,
all right, we talked for—

00:32:12.138 --> 00:32:14.474
That's how they get you.
44 minutes or whatever.

00:32:14.557 --> 00:32:19.354
And then after that call, 2 days later,
Alex sends me this long thing,

00:32:19.437 --> 00:32:23.942
which is like, well, here's what I think
I would do if I were in your position.

00:32:24.525 --> 00:32:29.447
And the very first thing is
that you don't need a lot of people, and

00:32:29.530 --> 00:32:31.324
talent density is the thing that matters.

00:32:31.407 --> 00:32:34.994
So I'd say, like,
from the beginning of the plan, this was,

00:32:35.078 --> 00:32:37.288
like, the core of your strategy, right?

00:32:37.372 --> 00:32:39.582
It's basically, like,
you actually don't need

00:32:40.124 --> 00:32:44.212
a massive number of people,
but what you do need is for,

00:32:44.879 --> 00:32:48.633
like, each of the people to be as good as
possible, which kind of informed this

00:32:49.133 --> 00:32:51.302
kind of crazy recruiting push and, like,
very

00:32:52.720 --> 00:32:56.224
intense filtering that we had to take,
you know, some of the best people

00:32:56.307 --> 00:33:00.770
from inside Meta and a very intense
process to get people from outside Meta.

00:33:01.354 --> 00:33:05.024
But I actually think like a lot of what
has made MSL special

00:33:05.483 --> 00:33:09.320
and why a lot of people have wanted
to join is because I think it's in some

00:33:09.404 --> 00:33:12.532
ways it's just like more fun to work
on a talent and smaller effort.

00:33:12.615 --> 00:33:14.075
It feels like the startup.
Yes.

00:33:14.158 --> 00:33:14.909
That's operating.

00:33:16.327 --> 00:33:18.955
In some ways it has
all the benefits of a startup

00:33:19.038 --> 00:33:21.457
with the stability and resources
of a larger company.

00:33:21.833 --> 00:33:25.420
And this was like this environment that I
think this was like point one in your kind

00:33:25.503 --> 00:33:29.549
of plan for what, um, for what Alex was
like, this is what we need to go do.

00:33:29.757 --> 00:33:33.011
So I don't know the answer of like how big
is a pre-training team, you know,

00:33:33.094 --> 00:33:34.679
it's, it doesn't need to be that big.

00:33:34.762 --> 00:33:38.474
But I think if you look at, you know,
some of the other labs, um, it's

00:33:38.558 --> 00:33:39.934
probably quite big in some other places.

00:33:40.018 --> 00:33:44.439
So, um, I think part of the reason why,
uh, these models are doing well is because

00:33:44.522 --> 00:33:48.776
you You have people who we're betting
on and they have a lot of resources

00:33:48.860 --> 00:33:50.486
because we're taking our compute.

00:33:50.570 --> 00:33:53.614
We're not peanut buttering it
across a lot of competing efforts.

00:33:53.740 --> 00:33:59.454
We're basically just investing in the key
people, a lot of compute per researcher,

00:33:59.871 --> 00:34:01.748
and then they can do their work.

00:34:02.248 --> 00:34:04.751
Was Wonder the one big training effort?

00:34:04.959 --> 00:34:06.586
That was where most of the training was?

00:34:06.711 --> 00:34:07.837
It was the next pre-train.

00:34:07.920 --> 00:34:09.213
It was climbing up this ladder.

00:34:09.297 --> 00:34:11.966
So, I mean, we have this tiering
system and then kind of Spark.

00:34:12.050 --> 00:34:14.510
I mean, internally, like,
Avocado was the code name

00:34:14.594 --> 00:34:18.473
for the Muse Spark line, and then
Watermelon was the code name for this one.

00:34:18.639 --> 00:34:20.433
And we're, like, out of fruits now.
We're out of fruits.

00:34:20.516 --> 00:34:24.395
Yeah, so we're going to have
to switch to a, to a new convention.

00:34:24.729 --> 00:34:28.441
But, um, I think the only fruit that is
technically larger is the durian,

00:34:28.858 --> 00:34:30.359
and you don't want to go to the durian.
No, no, no, no.

00:34:30.443 --> 00:34:31.986
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
No, you want to stay far away.

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:34.322
A durian is bigger than a watermelon?

00:34:34.447 --> 00:34:36.115
I think it's definitely heavier.
That's horrifying.

00:34:36.199 --> 00:34:37.450
Definitely heavier.
Spikier.

00:34:37.533 --> 00:34:38.701
Spikier, yeah.

00:34:39.368 --> 00:34:44.415
But no, yeah, I think as Mark mentioned,
like, you know, each of the— I mean,

00:34:44.499 --> 00:34:48.419
each of the stages I'll describe, like,
it's tens of people working on it

00:34:48.503 --> 00:34:50.588
at Meta versus, you know, hundreds at—

00:34:50.713 --> 00:34:54.133
So Meta is a huge company, but this is
like a really tiny effort inside of Meta.

00:34:54.217 --> 00:34:54.842
Yeah.
Oh yeah, I know.

00:34:54.926 --> 00:34:56.385
The research team is less than 200 people.

00:34:56.469 --> 00:34:58.346
Yeah, it's very small.

00:34:58.429 --> 00:35:00.890
It's intentionally very, very tight.
I think that the—

00:35:00.973 --> 00:35:02.141
It's closer to 100, right?

00:35:02.225 --> 00:35:04.268
And then there's a bunch of InfoFolks.
Yeah.

00:35:04.852 --> 00:35:06.020
It's a lot of infra work.

00:35:06.104 --> 00:35:11.275
So I think at a very high level,
to go back to the, to the, to the what is

00:35:11.400 --> 00:35:13.236
it question, you know, how does it happen?

00:35:13.319 --> 00:35:15.655
How does model happen?
That's basically what I'm asking.

00:35:15.738 --> 00:35:17.115
Where do models come from?

00:35:19.575 --> 00:35:24.163
You take a huge amount of data
for pre-training, and here you're—

00:35:24.747 --> 00:35:28.501
And it's here, like, roughly,
it's like all the,

00:35:28.960 --> 00:35:31.796
you know, public internet and like all
the books you can get your hands

00:35:31.879 --> 00:35:34.173
on and just like as much data as possible.
But different types.

00:35:34.257 --> 00:35:37.802
Plus the proprietary
But also different modalities, right?

00:35:37.885 --> 00:35:40.263
So if you want it to be good
at perception, then you want to make sure

00:35:40.346 --> 00:35:44.308
it has a lot of images and audio
and video and this kind of stuff.

00:35:44.851 --> 00:35:47.103
Um,
you know, if you want it to

00:35:47.770 --> 00:35:50.982
be good at certain domains, you don't
just want to RL that in at the end.

00:35:51.107 --> 00:35:53.734
You want to have it have exposure
to that from the beginning.

00:35:53.818 --> 00:35:54.485
Yeah.
Yep.

00:35:54.652 --> 00:35:54.735
Yeah.

00:35:54.819 --> 00:35:57.864
So, so, and in this stage, you're kind of,
you're much less,

00:35:58.281 --> 00:36:01.450
you care a lot less exactly what the data
looks like or how it's formatted or

00:36:01.701 --> 00:36:03.411
You know, all of everything about it.

00:36:03.786 --> 00:36:05.788
But it's just lots and lots of data.

00:36:05.872 --> 00:36:08.749
When you talk about this being the best
personal agent model,

00:36:09.208 --> 00:36:12.336
was there data you had to get
for the pre-train where it's like huge

00:36:12.420 --> 00:36:15.423
amounts of restaurant
reservation, call booking?

00:36:15.506 --> 00:36:16.716
I don't know what it is, but like—

00:36:16.799 --> 00:36:20.136
You just kind of want to dump in large
volumes of the modalities

00:36:20.261 --> 00:36:21.470
that people are going to care about.

00:36:21.554 --> 00:36:22.763
I mean, there were a bunch of things.

00:36:22.847 --> 00:36:25.183
I mean, I don't actually think we talked
about this when we were talking

00:36:25.266 --> 00:36:27.602
about where Muse Wonder excels.

00:36:27.894 --> 00:36:31.230
But some of the areas that it's like, all
right, what do people care about, right?

00:36:31.314 --> 00:36:34.358
It's, I mean, there are things like
shopping that people care about a lot,

00:36:34.442 --> 00:36:36.569
personal advice that people
care about a lot.

00:36:36.903 --> 00:36:39.780
People have personal legal questions,
personal health questions,

00:36:39.864 --> 00:36:41.574
personal tax questions.

00:36:41.949 --> 00:36:44.493
Muse Wonder, I mean,
is like quite good at those.

00:36:44.577 --> 00:36:47.079
I don't know if there's any quantification
that we want to give,

00:36:47.163 --> 00:36:49.749
but it's, let's say it's—

00:36:49.832 --> 00:36:51.834
It's very good on those
personal advice questions.

00:36:51.918 --> 00:36:55.338
Yeah, and so those types of things,
I mean, you can RL them to some degree,

00:36:55.421 --> 00:36:58.382
but you're like, just want
to put in a large amount of data.

00:36:58.799 --> 00:37:01.260
Like, you can imagine health is a very—
there's a lot of data

00:37:01.344 --> 00:37:02.220
out there about health.

00:37:02.303 --> 00:37:05.348
And, um, in, in pre-training,
you just wanted to like see

00:37:05.431 --> 00:37:06.599
all these different things.

00:37:06.682 --> 00:37:11.020
And, um, so you, you kind of just
put these different corpora in.

00:37:11.646 --> 00:37:11.854
Yeah.

00:37:12.188 --> 00:37:17.193
So, so that— so as, um,
as Mark was mentioning, like, the, uh, we,

00:37:18.361 --> 00:37:22.490
uh, one of the highlights of Muse Wonder
actually is its ability on domain

00:37:22.573 --> 00:37:24.116
various domain expertises.
Yes.

00:37:24.200 --> 00:37:29.121
And I think our philosophy here is that,
you know, the best personal agent should

00:37:29.205 --> 00:37:31.123
feel like a team
of experts in your corner.

00:37:31.207 --> 00:37:33.960
Like, it should feel like
it is your legal team.

00:37:34.168 --> 00:37:34.293
Yes.

00:37:34.377 --> 00:37:38.047
And your finance team, and your
medical team, and, and, and, and, and.

00:37:38.422 --> 00:37:42.051
And so, um, on a bunch of these domains,
we, you know, Muse Wonder is actually

00:37:42.134 --> 00:37:45.554
state of the art on, I think, um,
a lot of the finance evals, as well

00:37:45.638 --> 00:37:48.099
as a lot of the, um, uh, legal evals.

00:37:48.641 --> 00:37:50.017
the state-of-the-art model.

00:37:50.142 --> 00:37:53.562
And that, you know, these, these domain
areas, that starts in pre-training.

00:37:53.646 --> 00:37:57.692
Those, you know, we look for a lot of
domain data to put into pre-training.

00:37:57.817 --> 00:37:58.234
Yes.

00:37:58.317 --> 00:38:01.362
But a lot of the,
a lot of the art of pre-training— I mean,

00:38:01.654 --> 00:38:05.449
pre-training is, um, there's this one Rune
tweet where it's like pre-training is full

00:38:05.574 --> 00:38:08.869
of the researchers who like chew glass
and stare out into the distance

00:38:08.953 --> 00:38:10.913
and into the, into the abyss or whatnot.

00:38:10.997 --> 00:38:13.916
But, but I think there's a truth
to that because it is like, um,

00:38:14.375 --> 00:38:17.962
It is both the highest stakes
of the research areas because you,

00:38:18.462 --> 00:38:22.800
you know, we invested a huge percentage
of our compute into training Muse Wonder,

00:38:22.883 --> 00:38:24.302
into pre-training Muse Wonder.

00:38:24.385 --> 00:38:26.929
And it was very high stakes.

00:38:27.054 --> 00:38:30.683
I think probably both of us at various
points in that process, you know—

00:38:30.766 --> 00:38:32.184
You went through troughs of sadness.

00:38:32.268 --> 00:38:36.147
But yeah, troughs of uncertainties,
you know, all the 7 stages of grief,

00:38:36.355 --> 00:38:38.983
you know, happen
while you're pre-training a model.

00:38:39.066 --> 00:38:44.322
And it is the most sort of like
fundamentally scientific in an interesting

00:38:44.405 --> 00:38:47.491
way, because, you know,
you just don't have very many samples.

00:38:47.908 --> 00:38:52.330
So, like, your ability to infer exactly
what will happen scientifically

00:38:52.705 --> 00:38:54.749
really depends on your ability to sort
of like,

00:38:54.832 --> 00:38:56.834
you know, big brain reason about
everything that's

00:38:56.917 --> 00:38:58.294
happening in this process.
Okay.

00:38:58.377 --> 00:39:01.756
So in the pre-training stage,
everyone who's training frontier models

00:39:02.256 --> 00:39:05.301
basically is trying to get as much data as
possible for the pre-train

00:39:05.384 --> 00:39:09.638
with maybe a bent towards
If you want Muse Wonder to excel at legal

00:39:09.722 --> 00:39:11.807
and finance and tax questions and things
like this,

00:39:12.183 --> 00:39:15.936
then you want to have as much
of that specific corpus as

00:39:16.062 --> 00:39:17.271
possible in the pre-train.

00:39:17.355 --> 00:39:20.066
Yeah, you still want as much
intentionality about where ultimately you

00:39:20.149 --> 00:39:23.277
want your model to be, but again, it just,
you know, benefits from a lot more data.

00:39:23.361 --> 00:39:23.611
Yeah.

00:39:23.694 --> 00:39:28.657
And this is sort of the massive data,
massive compute stage.

00:39:29.033 --> 00:39:32.578
Do you hit some saturation in the data you
put in the pre-train, or ultimately

00:39:32.661 --> 00:39:34.288
is it just more data is better?

00:39:35.289 --> 00:39:37.708
Well,
there's a relationship between if you want

00:39:37.792 --> 00:39:39.877
the model to be bigger,
you need more data.

00:39:40.336 --> 00:39:41.170
Otherwise, it overfits.

00:39:41.253 --> 00:39:44.131
Yes, but my point is, like,
is there data where it's like,

00:39:44.340 --> 00:39:47.343
we can't distract the model with that,
we can't clutter up its brain attic

00:39:47.426 --> 00:39:50.429
with knowing that stuff,
or is it just more data is better?

00:39:50.805 --> 00:39:52.973
There are some of these effects, I think.

00:39:53.057 --> 00:39:54.725
Yeah, because this is part of the science.

00:39:54.809 --> 00:39:56.143
Okay, we're getting into
the secret sauce, sorry.

00:39:56.227 --> 00:39:58.813
No, it's not even that, it's just like,
I mean, I think that there are all these

00:39:58.896 --> 00:40:01.107
questions about, like, okay, well,
we put a lot of these tokens in,

00:40:01.190 --> 00:40:03.275
is there any zero-sumness in a bunch
of places there isn't,

00:40:03.359 --> 00:40:08.406
or it's actually positive transfer,
but then like depending on how it's set

00:40:08.489 --> 00:40:10.533
up, like you can have
some negative transfer.

00:40:10.699 --> 00:40:10.866
Yes.

00:40:10.950 --> 00:40:12.993
And so I don't know, there are
all these interesting questions.

00:40:13.077 --> 00:40:18.249
But in general, one of the
bigger challenges of scaling bigger models

00:40:18.791 --> 00:40:21.544
is it's not just about how many
flops and how much compute.

00:40:22.294 --> 00:40:24.755
You want to be able
to scale data with that.

00:40:24.839 --> 00:40:28.592
I mean, this is like part of what,
when the original kind of scaling laws

00:40:28.676 --> 00:40:31.554
for pre-training were written, I mean,
this is sort of what came

00:40:31.637 --> 00:40:33.180
out of that is is that.

00:40:33.264 --> 00:40:35.558
So if the internet isn't growing
that quickly

00:40:36.308 --> 00:40:39.311
and you want to scale bigger models
that can be more intelligent,

00:40:39.395 --> 00:40:41.939
I think one of the interesting problems
is, all right, where's

00:40:42.022 --> 00:40:42.982
the data coming from?
Yes.

00:40:43.065 --> 00:40:48.112
So I think that probably gets to the end
of what we're going to talk about on that.

00:40:48.279 --> 00:40:54.702
But we could go to the other—
Excellent CEO energy over here.

00:40:55.202 --> 00:41:00.291
But so post-training, so
then I think Post-training is kind of— is

00:41:00.374 --> 00:41:04.044
a very exciting field now, I think,
and is an area where

00:41:05.254 --> 00:41:08.048
I think actually, I think
almost on a continual basis,

00:41:08.132 --> 00:41:10.926
the entire field is learning about
the optimal recipes to be able

00:41:11.010 --> 00:41:12.553
to post-train these models.

00:41:12.887 --> 00:41:16.307
And again, for people, like,
post-training is where you give the model

00:41:16.390 --> 00:41:19.059
a task that you expect it to do,
grade it on how it does.

00:41:19.268 --> 00:41:21.979
And so if it's a chess-playing model,
it plays chess games, learns the— Well,

00:41:22.062 --> 00:41:23.272
there's actually 2 stages.

00:41:23.439 --> 00:41:25.483
There's the SFT,
So there's supervised learning.

00:41:25.733 --> 00:41:30.404
So that's like a lot of when you take this
kind of raw thing and you give it both

00:41:31.447 --> 00:41:33.699
the question and the answer that you
would want it to have, right?

00:41:33.782 --> 00:41:35.701
If like, if you're having a chat
conversation, how would you

00:41:35.784 --> 00:41:37.036
want it to respond in this case?

00:41:37.119 --> 00:41:41.123
So when you're training things like
personality, the SFT is actually like

00:41:41.207 --> 00:41:43.209
a very important part for molding it.

00:41:43.667 --> 00:41:47.213
But then I think most of the advances
in the recent years have been in RL.

00:41:47.755 --> 00:41:48.506
Yeah.
Yeah.

00:41:48.714 --> 00:41:52.635
So I think one mental model that can be
helpful is like, And SFT is where

00:41:52.718 --> 00:41:55.179
you kind of initialize a lot of—
SFT is supervised fine-tuning.

00:41:55.304 --> 00:41:56.514
Supervised fine-tuning, yeah.

00:41:56.597 --> 00:41:59.558
Where you initialize a lot
of the behaviors that you want the model

00:41:59.642 --> 00:42:01.393
to be able to exhibit later on.

00:42:01.727 --> 00:42:04.647
And then,
you know, maybe at the end of SFT,

00:42:05.105 --> 00:42:08.692
it has those behaviors kind of baked in,
but it'll only do it like 10% of the time.

00:42:08.776 --> 00:42:13.864
And then in reinforcement learning,
you really sort of give the model

00:42:14.323 --> 00:42:18.494
reinforcement in various situations
to bring the elicitation of those

00:42:18.577 --> 00:42:19.787
behaviors dramatically up.

00:42:21.121 --> 00:42:24.583
And I think that the—
yeah, so I think there's a bunch

00:42:24.667 --> 00:42:28.921
of interesting things that are
happening in RL and post-training.

00:42:29.004 --> 00:42:33.133
I think one is that just the scaling of RL
and post-training has been

00:42:34.468 --> 00:42:37.763
one of the clear axes, just spending
more compute and gathering more data.

00:42:37.972 --> 00:42:40.766
Just doing much more of it
before you release a model.

00:42:40.975 --> 00:42:44.562
Yes, doing much more of it, especially as
the pre-trains scale up, doing much more.

00:42:44.853 --> 00:42:47.982
It's like anything in scaling laws,
like it has to go with the rest

00:42:48.065 --> 00:42:50.859
of the factors or it doesn't work.
Yeah, in general.

00:42:51.277 --> 00:42:53.946
I mean, if I were to give, say,
like one broad statement about AI,

00:42:54.029 --> 00:42:55.781
it's just scale everything, you know?

00:42:55.864 --> 00:42:58.909
But, um, uh, but in particular,
you have to scale everything.

00:42:58.993 --> 00:43:01.662
Like scaling one thing
in isolation does not work.

00:43:02.246 --> 00:43:05.958
You, you technically can,
you can scale one dimension.

00:43:06.417 --> 00:43:08.627
You just will get
much more optimal performance if

00:43:08.711 --> 00:43:10.170
you scale many things altogether.

00:43:10.254 --> 00:43:13.173
Well, I mean, so one way to think about
like Muse Wonder, right?

00:43:13.257 --> 00:43:14.633
It's like, all right,
so we did the pre-train.

00:43:14.717 --> 00:43:16.760
Yep.
we've done some RL, right?

00:43:16.844 --> 00:43:18.178
When you look at it
like Muse Spark, right?

00:43:18.262 --> 00:43:22.975
So version 1 to 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, we didn't
change anything about pre-training, right?

00:43:23.225 --> 00:43:25.644
We just continued doing
more RL on top of it.

00:43:25.811 --> 00:43:29.773
So, um, so you,
when you think about the early versions

00:43:30.024 --> 00:43:33.902
of a model of a new class,
they're actually relatively less,

00:43:34.361 --> 00:43:37.656
like the RL to pre-training ratio is going
to be lower for RL

00:43:38.324 --> 00:43:39.283
than the later versions.

00:43:39.366 --> 00:43:42.077
That is kind of what the point
releases in models are.

00:43:42.286 --> 00:43:44.413
Well, I think every company
has its own convention.

00:43:44.496 --> 00:43:45.581
For us, that's what it is meant to be.

00:43:45.664 --> 00:43:49.168
But for a lot of companies, like the big
number is the pre-train and then the—

00:43:49.251 --> 00:43:51.378
I think it kind of lost
that semantic value.

00:43:53.088 --> 00:43:54.214
Yeah, I think now you
actually can't do that.

00:43:54.298 --> 00:43:56.050
Okay, but that is true at Meta at least.

00:43:56.133 --> 00:43:57.217
But we haven't even done it.

00:43:57.301 --> 00:43:59.678
We kind of decided to not even do it.
It's true at Muse Spark at least.

00:43:59.762 --> 00:44:01.347
It is true at Muse Spark and Muse Wonder.

00:44:01.430 --> 00:44:03.432
It's true that Muse Spark
and Muse Wonder are different pre-trains.

00:44:03.515 --> 00:44:06.644
And we decided to kick the can down
the road on this question for Muse Wonder

00:44:06.727 --> 00:44:08.604
by not giving it a number yet.

00:44:08.771 --> 00:44:11.940
But when we have our first dot release,
we will have to decide that.

00:44:13.901 --> 00:44:14.151
Yeah.

00:44:14.234 --> 00:44:17.279
But yeah, no, I think the RL stage,
part of what has been—

00:44:18.614 --> 00:44:23.118
part of what's very interesting is
first is just producing the environments

00:44:23.202 --> 00:44:24.119
and producing the tasks.

00:44:24.203 --> 00:44:27.122
And I think
that is one of the areas where there's

00:44:27.206 --> 00:44:29.208
a lot of intentionality
in training these models.

00:44:29.416 --> 00:44:33.003
It's also one of the areas where
you notice very funny things.

00:44:33.087 --> 00:44:38.801
Like if you— in a lot of cases, less is
actually more when it comes to data in RL.

00:44:39.385 --> 00:44:44.181
And so I think I think the sort of, like,
the feel and the science of it, like,

00:44:44.765 --> 00:44:48.477
is pretty flipped, generally
speaking, from pre-training.

00:44:48.560 --> 00:44:49.061
Yeah.

00:44:49.186 --> 00:44:54.191
The other is that I think it can give very
magnified kind of instruction

00:44:54.274 --> 00:44:55.150
to the model, if that makes sense.

00:44:55.234 --> 00:44:58.529
So I think that, like,
a lot of safety cases or alignment cases

00:44:58.612 --> 00:45:01.031
that we care a lot about are, like,
you know, and, you know,

00:45:01.115 --> 00:45:04.952
where you would see when you do large
high-compute RL runs on frontier models,

00:45:05.285 --> 00:45:11.041
are where you apply a lot of RL or
optimization pressure in scenarios where

00:45:11.125 --> 00:45:15.254
the only way for the model to succeed is
to do something misaligned

00:45:15.379 --> 00:45:16.296
or do something unsafe.

00:45:16.755 --> 00:45:19.842
And so that's another thing that we had
to be very careful about and thoughtful

00:45:19.925 --> 00:45:24.012
about in training Muse Wonder is ensuring
that we curated the RL environments very

00:45:24.680 --> 00:45:28.350
thoughtfully to ensure that
all of them were eliciting and driving

00:45:28.434 --> 00:45:30.519
towards more aligned
behavior from the model.

00:45:30.644 --> 00:45:32.604
So the point that you're saying about
quality, I think,

00:45:32.688 --> 00:45:36.150
is an important one because I think a lot
of the previous questions back and forth

00:45:36.233 --> 00:45:38.569
were about the quantity and have
the flavor of like, oh,

00:45:38.652 --> 00:45:40.070
you need to scale the quantity.
Yes.

00:45:40.154 --> 00:45:42.698
But I mean, I do think like a huge part
of this is just, is,

00:45:43.532 --> 00:45:47.661
is the point that you just made of like
the even a smaller amount of high quality.

00:45:48.245 --> 00:45:52.082
Yeah, I think, I think in RL, like we,
you know, as we've seen

00:45:52.666 --> 00:45:57.713
pretty big jumps in our new Spark line,
I think these have been like accompanied

00:45:57.796 --> 00:46:02.301
with, you know, real meaningful
improvements to the underlying recipe.

00:46:02.509 --> 00:46:05.095
And each of these improvements
in the recipe have been like,

00:46:06.346 --> 00:46:11.477
I think the improvement between Muse Spark
1 to Muse Spark 1.3 feels night and day.

00:46:11.560 --> 00:46:14.897
I think probably to most people,
if you ask them,

00:46:16.690 --> 00:46:18.192
do these feel like they came
from different pretrains?

00:46:18.275 --> 00:46:20.986
I think they probably would say yes,
because I think you can—

00:46:21.111 --> 00:46:21.487
Mm-hmm.

00:46:21.695 --> 00:46:24.198
I think it's happening
industry-wide for what it's worth.

00:46:24.281 --> 00:46:29.703
It's just like we are learning to
squeeze so much capability and so much

00:46:30.496 --> 00:46:35.209
and elicit so much agentic capability
from these existing base models.

00:46:35.334 --> 00:46:38.837
Now, that's not to say, like,
we believe scaling is very important.

00:46:38.921 --> 00:46:41.089
That's why we have to use
Wonderland in the first place.

00:46:41.215 --> 00:46:41.632
Yes.

00:46:41.924 --> 00:46:44.551
But, you know, this sort of like,
these two things are happening at the same

00:46:44.635 --> 00:46:48.847
time, which is also, by the way, for us,
this is why we're seeing so

00:46:48.931 --> 00:46:49.973
much progress in general.

00:46:50.057 --> 00:46:53.519
Like, I think it's the fact that we're
scaling both pre-training and scaling our

00:46:53.602 --> 00:46:56.855
recipes in RL kind of like concurrently.
At a nice clip.

00:46:57.356 --> 00:46:57.523
Yeah.

00:46:57.606 --> 00:46:59.733
And then there's these
other scaling axes too.

00:46:59.817 --> 00:47:03.028
I mean, like making sure that your agent
harness is good and that you can

00:47:03.111 --> 00:47:06.198
co-optimize these things is probably just
another aspect of,

00:47:06.657 --> 00:47:08.116
or dimension for scaling.

00:47:08.742 --> 00:47:11.745
But if I'm understanding you guys
correctly, are you saying that alignment

00:47:12.204 --> 00:47:16.250
is not about
coming up with some alignment-specific RL

00:47:16.375 --> 00:47:20.379
steps and instead about making sure
the entire RL process

00:47:20.963 --> 00:47:23.382
is kind of steering The model—

00:47:23.966 --> 00:47:26.468
You have to be thoughtful about
it in the entire process of RL.

00:47:26.552 --> 00:47:26.802
Yeah.

00:47:27.427 --> 00:47:30.055
So I think this is
a holistic effort, I think.

00:47:30.138 --> 00:47:30.556
Yeah.

00:47:30.722 --> 00:47:33.517
But also, I think different labs
have very different theories.

00:47:33.934 --> 00:47:36.770
I mean, alignment is a very
philosophical concept, right?

00:47:36.854 --> 00:47:38.730
Of like, who do you want
it to be aligned to?

00:47:38.814 --> 00:47:42.985
I mean, a lot of our theory is like, okay,
we care more about it being aligned to,

00:47:43.068 --> 00:47:46.196
you know, we're trying
to give people agents, right?

00:47:46.280 --> 00:47:48.407
And it needs to,
if you ask it to do something,

00:47:49.032 --> 00:47:52.744
you need to have a high confidence that
it's going to do what you ask it to do.

00:47:53.036 --> 00:47:53.120
Yes.

00:47:53.203 --> 00:47:55.747
You know, like, you want it
to do that with high reliability.

00:47:55.914 --> 00:47:58.917
You don't want a moralizing lecture
for why you're wrong

00:47:59.126 --> 00:48:00.335
for asking it to do the thing.

00:48:00.544 --> 00:48:02.880
Obviously, if you're trying to do
something that's catastrophic,

00:48:02.963 --> 00:48:06.758
it needs to gracefully,
without pissing you off, like, not do it.

00:48:08.093 --> 00:48:10.596
But in general, you know,
we view alignment as more

00:48:11.179 --> 00:48:15.893
of a question of instruction and values
alignment with the person

00:48:15.976 --> 00:48:17.102
who it's intending to serve.

00:48:18.979 --> 00:48:22.983
And I think that that's kind
of a different mode of thinking about it.

00:48:23.066 --> 00:48:25.861
It's very different from, I think,
how some of the others do.

00:48:26.028 --> 00:48:30.908
But one of the more interesting things I
think that is maybe a question related

00:48:30.991 --> 00:48:34.578
to your original question is also just how
long the process of training

00:48:34.745 --> 00:48:35.579
one of these models takes.

00:48:35.662 --> 00:48:41.335
Like Muse Wonder, I mean, in general,
we've planned Muse Wonder since

00:48:42.169 --> 00:48:43.795
last You know, August.

00:48:44.379 --> 00:48:48.300
Yeah, so yeah, I remember the offsite when
we were— so this is like when

00:48:48.383 --> 00:48:49.384
they first presented this.

00:48:49.468 --> 00:48:54.097
Yeah, so I think all of the stages that we
just talked about are like, you know, you

00:48:54.181 --> 00:48:55.891
really work on them for quite a long time.

00:48:55.974 --> 00:48:59.770
And, and, um, it's not that we,
we didn't start working on Muse Wonder

00:48:59.853 --> 00:49:01.188
when we finished Muse Spark.

00:49:01.271 --> 00:49:03.941
It's that we've actually just been
planning and working on this

00:49:04.024 --> 00:49:05.651
for quite a long time.

00:49:06.151 --> 00:49:09.446
And we really view Muse Spark,
if anything, as like I think we mentioned

00:49:09.529 --> 00:49:11.740
this in the first blog post
where we wrote about it.

00:49:12.240 --> 00:49:15.577
That was a stepping stone
to get to Muse Wonder.

00:49:16.370 --> 00:49:17.371
And that really was how
we thought about it.

00:49:17.454 --> 00:49:18.664
Even at the time you thought about it.

00:49:18.747 --> 00:49:22.209
Yeah, and in terms of how long it takes,
I mean, we're also even just

00:49:22.292 --> 00:49:23.502
talking about the research part.

00:49:23.585 --> 00:49:27.839
I mean, there's also the compute part
where we've been investing in building up,

00:49:28.090 --> 00:49:29.758
first of all, a huge
amount of compute overall.

00:49:29.841 --> 00:49:32.886
But a lot of, you know,
part of our philosophy on this is that

00:49:33.720 --> 00:49:38.600
you can kind of measure
the compute strength of a lab by how big

00:49:38.684 --> 00:49:43.730
its largest single computer is,
supercomputer, for being able to do these

00:49:44.356 --> 00:49:45.065
large training runs.

00:49:45.148 --> 00:49:48.777
So, I mean, we built this Prometheus
cluster, we call it, in Ohio.

00:49:49.027 --> 00:49:52.614
Mm-hmm.
That's scaling to larger than a gigawatt.

00:49:53.115 --> 00:49:56.243
And now we're building this Hyperion
cluster in Louisiana that's

00:49:56.326 --> 00:49:57.911
going to scale to 5 gigawatts.

00:49:58.870 --> 00:50:03.542
And those actually, predate MSL.

00:50:05.002 --> 00:50:08.547
Because like building a whole
like data center site from scratch,

00:50:09.214 --> 00:50:11.383
at least the plans for those,
they're coming online now.

00:50:11.466 --> 00:50:13.093
But that was—

00:50:13.385 --> 00:50:15.137
You guys thought it
might be the feed, right?

00:50:15.220 --> 00:50:17.472
That you just have used
for a lot of compute generally?

00:50:17.556 --> 00:50:18.306
No, no, no.

00:50:18.849 --> 00:50:23.103
This is kind of predating, even going
back to the, to kind of the LLaMA program.

00:50:23.186 --> 00:50:23.854
I see.

00:50:23.937 --> 00:50:27.441
I mean, it was probably in 2023.

00:50:29.484 --> 00:50:35.407
or yeah, either 2023, 2024 was when we
basically started making

00:50:35.490 --> 00:50:36.700
the plans to build those clusters.

00:50:36.783 --> 00:50:39.494
They're now kind of fully coming online.

00:50:39.786 --> 00:50:43.373
Well, I guess the Louisiana one will
come online over a couple years.

00:50:43.999 --> 00:50:44.249
Yeah.

00:50:45.625 --> 00:50:49.546
But if you want to go really end-to-end
on undoing this stuff, then, I mean,

00:50:49.629 --> 00:50:54.051
obviously the kind of full stack, and I
mean, Meta is a full-stack tech company.

00:50:54.176 --> 00:50:58.430
I mean, we build our own
data centers, we build our own chips,

00:50:58.513 --> 00:51:00.182
we build our own low-level infrastructure.

00:51:00.265 --> 00:51:02.893
And now this is kind of the next
component, which, I mean,

00:51:03.101 --> 00:51:06.146
arguably is probably the most important
component in all of this for the next wave

00:51:06.229 --> 00:51:09.608
of technology is kind of building really
good models and then

00:51:09.691 --> 00:51:10.567
delivering them to people.

00:51:10.650 --> 00:51:13.403
But I mean, yeah, if you want to go fully
end-to-end, I mean,

00:51:13.862 --> 00:51:16.531
this is a long process.

00:51:16.865 --> 00:51:20.952
I do think this is like a— I mean,
maybe it's well appreciated by folks

00:51:21.036 --> 00:51:24.998
in the tech industry, but It really is,
you know, it feels like models

00:51:25.082 --> 00:51:25.874
are coming out constantly.

00:51:25.957 --> 00:51:28.752
So maybe you'd have the mental model
that like, oh, a company like launches

00:51:28.835 --> 00:51:31.630
a model and then they figure out how
they're going to make the next model and,

00:51:31.713 --> 00:51:32.339
or something like that.

00:51:32.422 --> 00:51:35.801
But it's actually these like
very long planning processes and these

00:51:36.218 --> 00:51:39.971
sort of like very long work streams
that kind of all come together at the very

00:51:40.055 --> 00:51:41.973
end and then result
in these beautiful models.

00:51:42.057 --> 00:51:42.390
Yeah.

00:51:42.891 --> 00:51:46.853
How do you build good products when
the feedback cycle is so long?

00:51:47.062 --> 00:51:49.231
Like ultimately you start planning
the model in August and you don't

00:51:49.314 --> 00:51:51.441
know until more than a year later.
if it's good.

00:51:51.942 --> 00:51:55.445
And, you know, Silicon Valley famously
likes to run on metrics, but, like,

00:51:55.654 --> 00:51:58.573
ultimately,
do you just need someone who

00:51:59.574 --> 00:52:02.327
is the aesthete and says,
I'm sorry, I don't like it?

00:52:02.410 --> 00:52:04.371
It's nice when you have metrics,
but I think the, like,

00:52:04.454 --> 00:52:08.667
state of being an entrepreneur is, like,
how well do you make decisions

00:52:08.750 --> 00:52:11.294
in an information-poor environment, right?
So—

00:52:11.628 --> 00:52:13.255
But this is, like,
that on steroids, right?

00:52:13.338 --> 00:52:14.297
That on mega steroids.

00:52:14.422 --> 00:52:17.592
Yeah, I think that this is—
and I think Marc gets a huge amount

00:52:17.676 --> 00:52:21.638
of credit for this, like,
Because of how long everything takes,

00:52:21.721 --> 00:52:27.102
like, there were many points along over
the past year since I've been here,

00:52:27.185 --> 00:52:29.896
but also, you know, as you mentioned,
like, long before that, where

00:52:30.564 --> 00:52:34.234
you have to make
very large investments or make very big

00:52:34.317 --> 00:52:38.530
decisions about, you know,
how you're going to invest or develop

00:52:38.613 --> 00:52:41.491
the technology or, you know,
what decisions you're going to make

00:52:41.950 --> 00:52:47.247
long before there's proof points around
whether or not even the thing is working.

00:52:47.330 --> 00:52:51.042
And so I think this is like,
in some sense, it's like

00:52:51.126 --> 00:52:52.210
entrepreneurship on hard mode.

00:52:52.294 --> 00:52:53.587
It's like the opposite of Lean Startup.

00:52:53.670 --> 00:52:56.173
Like, Lean Startup is like, yeah,
you like see if you get something working

00:52:56.256 --> 00:52:58.592
and then you double down and then you
see if that works, then you double down.

00:52:58.675 --> 00:53:00.260
Like, you cannot do that in AI.

00:53:00.719 --> 00:53:05.307
You have to like weave— I mean,
we've been investing into these data

00:53:05.390 --> 00:53:07.601
centers since, you know,
for years and years and years.

00:53:07.684 --> 00:53:08.476
We've had to—

00:53:08.560 --> 00:53:11.688
And you'll be radically behind if you
wait to see if things are working.

00:53:11.855 --> 00:53:15.859
So we have these conversations where it's
like, At the end of last year, we're like,

00:53:15.942 --> 00:53:19.237
all right, we are quite
excited about the progress.

00:53:19.321 --> 00:53:21.031
Yeah, we haven't seen the models yet.

00:53:21.114 --> 00:53:23.950
Like, it's like, I'm going to, like,
you know, we're going to write this check

00:53:24.034 --> 00:53:27.412
for, like, I don't know,
like $100 billion, $200 billion.

00:53:27.495 --> 00:53:30.332
I'm like, Alex, are you
sure this is going to work?

00:53:30.999 --> 00:53:35.670
It's like, um, yeah, so it was,
it was so extreme that, you know,

00:53:35.754 --> 00:53:37.505
we had, we had like the whole exec team.

00:53:37.589 --> 00:53:38.924
I think Susan scared you a bit.

00:53:39.049 --> 00:53:43.678
Yeah, well, yeah, no, well,
it was like The joke every time we talked

00:53:43.762 --> 00:53:47.891
about it would just be like, yeah, and we
hope it's all going to work and be good.

00:53:47.974 --> 00:53:50.810
But like, there were literally, like,
at the end of last year, other than

00:53:50.894 --> 00:53:54.397
me giving a vibe report on how our
research was going and, you know,

00:53:54.481 --> 00:53:58.818
I could sort of show some plots
and whatnot, but it was like, I mean,

00:53:59.319 --> 00:54:01.613
I was feeling good about it,
but it was certainly not clear

00:54:01.696 --> 00:54:03.240
at all that it was going to work.

00:54:03.323 --> 00:54:03.573
Yeah.

00:54:03.907 --> 00:54:08.495
You guys are saying that the essence of
training a frontier model is you have

00:54:08.578 --> 00:54:12.123
to push your chips all in before
you've been dealt the cards.

00:54:12.290 --> 00:54:16.378
You just have to decide that you're going
to develop a frontier model

00:54:16.544 --> 00:54:19.339
and you don't get to meter the inputs.
At this scale.

00:54:19.839 --> 00:54:22.550
I mean, if we, like, back a few years ago,
the clusters didn't have to be

00:54:22.634 --> 00:54:24.094
this big, but now they do.

00:54:24.177 --> 00:54:28.139
So I think this is part of the excitement

00:54:28.974 --> 00:54:32.519
of booting up a frontier lab now, right?

00:54:33.061 --> 00:54:35.689
Is basically like the ante
is extremely high.

00:54:36.106 --> 00:54:40.360
And I think this is— and like,
I kind of like viscerally remember this.

00:54:40.443 --> 00:54:42.153
We were in Hawaii at the end of last year.

00:54:42.237 --> 00:54:44.489
It was kind of like the whole
exec team got together.

00:54:44.906 --> 00:54:48.159
And yeah, Susan pulled me aside many times
and was like,

00:54:48.618 --> 00:54:52.080
you know, I just want to tell you,
if this doesn't work, like, you know,

00:54:52.247 --> 00:54:53.873
this is going to be really, really bad.

00:54:54.082 --> 00:54:57.377
It's going to be very,
very bad in financial terms.

00:54:58.420 --> 00:55:01.214
I really need to impress on you
that this model has to work.

00:55:01.715 --> 00:55:02.799
it has to be good.

00:55:02.882 --> 00:55:04.926
If it's good, it's all good.
We're great.

00:55:05.010 --> 00:55:07.971
But if it's bad, it's really,
really, really bad.

00:55:08.054 --> 00:55:10.015
And Susan, who you know—
And that was useful motivation?

00:55:10.098 --> 00:55:10.682
It was.

00:55:10.765 --> 00:55:14.060
Well, it was like,
it was one of these very funny moments

00:55:14.144 --> 00:55:16.813
where I was like, you know,
obviously the amounts of money

00:55:16.896 --> 00:55:20.066
are like so obscenely large.

00:55:20.525 --> 00:55:21.860
What are the amounts of money in training?

00:55:21.943 --> 00:55:23.528
Order of magnitude,
whatever you can share, but just

00:55:23.611 --> 00:55:25.280
like how much are they costing?
Well, you can do the classic math.

00:55:25.363 --> 00:55:26.990
Yeah, no, I mean, a gigawatt cluster.

00:55:27.073 --> 00:55:31.202
I mean, it's Like, I don't know,
I think what you'd see out there would be,

00:55:31.494 --> 00:55:35.373
you know, to operate a gigawatt a year,
call it on the order of $15 billion.

00:55:36.166 --> 00:55:40.086
Okay, so it's roughly a $15 billion
effort to train a bunch of models.

00:55:40.295 --> 00:55:42.130
To operate a gigawatt cluster for a year.

00:55:42.672 --> 00:55:45.425
If you're talking about building
the CapEx, it's probably closer to,

00:55:45.508 --> 00:55:48.136
you know, I mean, you pay for it upfront,
so it's probably, you know,

00:55:48.219 --> 00:55:50.555
$50 or $60 billion.

00:55:51.139 --> 00:55:57.896
So, but then that's just kind of,
one cluster for one part of training.

00:55:58.188 --> 00:56:00.732
Yeah.
Um, then you have all the other parts.

00:56:01.399 --> 00:56:04.652
Um, research isn't just
doing the run, right?

00:56:04.736 --> 00:56:07.113
It's like, that's like probably an equal
amount of it

00:56:07.864 --> 00:56:11.076
is because for each change that you make,
you're testing all the ladders all

00:56:11.159 --> 00:56:13.328
the way up to the big, the big scale.

00:56:13.703 --> 00:56:15.789
Um, and then they're serving the stuff.

00:56:16.164 --> 00:56:18.708
So, I mean, part of the
stuff that in some ways—

00:56:18.792 --> 00:56:21.419
So tens of billions of dollars
to train a Frontier model now.

00:56:23.213 --> 00:56:26.132
Yeah, definitely the whole,
I mean, I've seen the financial rollups,

00:56:26.216 --> 00:56:28.259
tens of billions of dollars
is a good estimate for—

00:56:28.385 --> 00:56:28.551
Yeah.

00:56:28.635 --> 00:56:30.595
And then, but then, but then you
want to do something with it, right?

00:56:30.678 --> 00:56:34.182
So I actually think like
some of the more complicated questions

00:56:34.265 --> 00:56:39.771
that we had were like, all right,
so we're training this model and we know,

00:56:39.854 --> 00:56:42.774
we know we want to train the model cuz we,
this is like key, but like

00:56:43.233 --> 00:56:44.859
how much product demand do
we think we're going to have?

00:56:44.943 --> 00:56:45.026
Right.

00:56:45.110 --> 00:56:48.154
Because so now it's like, all right,
well the product doesn't exist.

00:56:48.488 --> 00:56:51.866
Um, like, and as in you're trying to build
data centers to serve Muse

00:56:51.950 --> 00:56:53.410
for a product that doesn't exist yet.
Yeah.

00:56:53.493 --> 00:56:55.662
And we have to make it
years in advance, right?

00:56:55.745 --> 00:56:58.706
So this is probably where, like,
when we were talking about it last year,

00:56:58.790 --> 00:57:01.793
we were just like,
huh, there was a moment where I think

00:57:01.876 --> 00:57:05.630
a bunch of people were like,
yeah, maybe, maybe we should just

00:57:05.713 --> 00:57:07.298
like be a little more conservative.

00:57:07.424 --> 00:57:09.259
And it's like, no, we're doing this.

00:57:09.342 --> 00:57:11.094
It's like, it's like,
we're going to make it work.

00:57:11.177 --> 00:57:12.262
We're going to make it work.
We're going to burn the boats.

00:57:12.345 --> 00:57:13.471
Yeah, this is going to work.

00:57:13.555 --> 00:57:19.102
And But it was like, I mean, even,
I mean, I too am an entrepreneur

00:57:19.185 --> 00:57:22.564
and founder and have, you know,
at various points in my life taken risk.

00:57:22.897 --> 00:57:26.943
I remember the visceral feeling at the
time was like, this is a fuck ton of risk.

00:57:27.485 --> 00:57:30.155
Like, I don't know if I really—
Oh, it's only riskier.

00:57:30.488 --> 00:57:31.364
Yeah, yeah, it's only—

00:57:31.614 --> 00:57:37.454
well, I do think this is like one
of the like things that I think, Mark,

00:57:37.745 --> 00:57:40.081
is very impressive about you,
which is I think you have really

00:57:40.165 --> 00:57:45.086
internalized this concept of You know,
it's about how big it is when you win,

00:57:45.253 --> 00:57:46.921
not like, you know, failure is okay.

00:57:47.005 --> 00:57:50.925
It's just like how big is
the win when you do win?

00:57:51.301 --> 00:57:52.135
And this was, I think—

00:57:52.218 --> 00:57:55.346
Mark has fully internalized power laws
in a way that many people have not.

00:57:55.430 --> 00:57:56.389
I think quite deeply, yeah.

00:57:56.473 --> 00:57:58.766
Well, I think the other piece is,
I don't actually think it's

00:57:58.850 --> 00:58:00.059
quite as risky as this.

00:58:00.143 --> 00:58:02.395
I mean, the numbers are big, but I think—

00:58:02.479 --> 00:58:04.481
Meta has a backstop in terms
of how it can use compute.

00:58:04.564 --> 00:58:05.815
Yeah, so I think there's 2 things.

00:58:05.899 --> 00:58:09.444
One is if AI works overall, plenty
of other people want all our compute.

00:58:09.652 --> 00:58:09.819
Right?

00:58:09.903 --> 00:58:13.740
So they're willing to pay, like,
I mean, if like the cost is whatever,

00:58:13.823 --> 00:58:16.576
$15 billion a gigawatt,
we could get like a lot more for that.

00:58:17.118 --> 00:58:19.412
So I think that you,
in order for it not to work out,

00:58:19.787 --> 00:58:23.082
it's not just that we have to fail,
AI overall has to fail.

00:58:23.249 --> 00:58:23.458
Yes.

00:58:24.667 --> 00:58:29.047
And then even if that happens,
we still have all these other

00:58:29.130 --> 00:58:30.715
uses to productively use compute.

00:58:30.798 --> 00:58:34.594
So in the grand scheme of things,
we were definitely building out

00:58:35.094 --> 00:58:39.974
this compute for inference ahead
of knowing that the product

00:58:40.058 --> 00:58:41.518
was going to be ready.

00:58:41.935 --> 00:58:45.522
But you were AI-pilled enough that you
said GPUs online and powered

00:58:45.605 --> 00:58:46.564
will be useful to someone.

00:58:46.648 --> 00:58:48.608
Yeah, but also like we believed
in what we were building.

00:58:48.691 --> 00:58:54.072
I mean, it's like, I think,
and you can look at the kind

00:58:54.614 --> 00:58:57.742
of the trajectory where it's like,
all right, first there's ChatGPT

00:58:57.825 --> 00:58:59.244
as the text-based assistant.

00:58:59.869 --> 00:59:02.539
Then, you know, Anthropic
got coding agents to work.

00:59:03.164 --> 00:59:07.126
And no one had built the kind
of personal consumer version of that.

00:59:07.502 --> 00:59:11.047
And it just seemed like this is the thing
that Meta should build in the world.

00:59:11.214 --> 00:59:14.467
And I think that that was just like pretty
clear to you and me and Nat,

00:59:14.551 --> 00:59:15.969
like from the beginning, right?

00:59:16.052 --> 00:59:18.555
That that's like,
that's kind of what we're here to do.

00:59:18.805 --> 00:59:20.473
And so I don't know.

00:59:20.682 --> 00:59:24.394
I mean, there's always like the real
question for me on the Muse launch even

00:59:25.144 --> 00:59:28.189
was you never know when you launch
something how it's going to be received.

00:59:28.690 --> 00:59:28.773
Right?

00:59:28.898 --> 00:59:32.193
So there's always the case where you get
a home run, which in this

00:59:32.277 --> 00:59:33.570
case it was, right?
Yes.

00:59:33.695 --> 00:59:36.239
And it's just like extremely
well received right off the bat.

00:59:36.322 --> 00:59:42.453
But the normal case would be you kind of—
it's fine, and you have to like iterate

00:59:42.537 --> 00:59:45.415
a few times to get product-market
fit before you really grow.

00:59:46.040 --> 00:59:47.959
In some ways, like,
I actually think the product,

00:59:48.209 --> 00:59:51.754
we maybe could have even grown it faster,
but we didn't have all the growth stuff

00:59:51.879 --> 00:59:54.757
really ready to go when we shipped it
because we are like— our median

00:59:54.841 --> 00:59:56.634
expectation was like, all right,
we're probably going to have to like

00:59:56.718 --> 00:59:59.137
iterate on this a bit, and like,
that'll be That'll be fine.

00:59:59.262 --> 01:00:03.266
But I don't know, I think like
if it takes a few iterations,

01:00:03.349 --> 01:00:05.268
it's like not the end of the world.
That's normal.

01:00:05.351 --> 01:00:05.518
Yes.

01:00:05.602 --> 01:00:08.187
Um,
you can have launches where it's like you

01:00:08.271 --> 01:00:10.815
get like, it's kind of catastrophic
and then it takes a while to recover,

01:00:10.898 --> 01:00:11.899
but that's not good.

01:00:12.025 --> 01:00:14.319
But, but it's like,
but I mean, even if it took a few

01:00:14.402 --> 01:00:15.903
iterations, it would have been fine.

01:00:15.987 --> 01:00:19.824
So I don't know, I guess it just seemed
to me like this was going to be— yeah,

01:00:19.907 --> 01:00:22.702
the numbers were big,
but I— it seemed like it was probably—

01:00:22.785 --> 01:00:27.040
Was it bonding for the executive team
to be Holding hands,

01:00:27.123 --> 01:00:28.625
taking this risk together.

01:00:28.708 --> 01:00:31.002
You're talking about Susan taking UXi?
Bonding or bonding moment?

01:00:31.085 --> 01:00:33.588
Is that a bonding moment?
It's like, I mean, it's stressful.

01:00:33.838 --> 01:00:34.881
I think it was—Both.

01:00:35.256 --> 01:00:35.965
Both.
Yeah.

01:00:36.049 --> 01:00:38.968
Being in a stressful situation
together is generally bonding.

01:00:39.052 --> 01:00:45.558
Well, one thing that I think is quite
notable about Meta is the degree to which,

01:00:45.642 --> 01:00:48.686
like, first of all, most of the exec
team has been there quite a long time.

01:00:48.770 --> 01:00:50.229
Like, Nat and I were really newbies.

01:00:50.313 --> 01:00:52.774
And
And,

01:00:53.274 --> 01:00:57.195
you know, most people have been through
crazy situations many

01:00:57.278 --> 01:00:59.113
times in the past as well.
Mm-hmm.

01:00:59.238 --> 01:01:02.909
And I think Meta as a company is like
an extremely resilient company that has

01:01:02.992 --> 01:01:07.622
been through like, you know,
many situations that also at the time

01:01:07.705 --> 01:01:09.332
would've seemed incredibly daunting.

01:01:09.415 --> 01:01:13.336
And so everyone's kind of like
knows the program, so to speak,

01:01:13.419 --> 01:01:18.800
in terms of, you know, this obviously
on paper, and especially when like

01:01:18.883 --> 01:01:22.679
Susan Li, our CFO, is sort of like pulling
me aside in the meetings, like, does,

01:01:22.929 --> 01:01:26.182
you know, does seem like
a particularly scary situation.

01:01:26.265 --> 01:01:28.935
But I think most people were like, yeah,
yeah, no, but we're Meta,

01:01:29.018 --> 01:01:29.769
we're going to get through it.

01:01:29.852 --> 01:01:34.232
Like, I think there was like a real
camaraderie around that and sort of like

01:01:34.315 --> 01:01:40.196
quite a nice energy around that,
which really I did appreciate because I

01:01:40.279 --> 01:01:45.410
think it would be very easy for,
you know, it's the other executives at

01:01:45.535 --> 01:01:49.580
the company who are operating and running—
Who are paying the bills.

01:01:49.664 --> 01:01:51.708
Yeah, one of the greatest
businesses of all time.

01:01:51.791 --> 01:01:51.999
Yes.

01:01:53.167 --> 01:01:58.214
And it'd be very easy for them to be
quite annoyed by this whole endeavor.

01:01:58.297 --> 01:02:00.800
But I think everyone is
also very excited about—

01:02:00.883 --> 01:02:01.342
They are.

01:02:01.426 --> 01:02:04.554
What the opportunities
for the company with AI are.

01:02:04.679 --> 01:02:07.432
And I think,
like, you know, taking a step back,

01:02:07.515 --> 01:02:11.644
I think one
of Mark's jobs as, like,

01:02:11.728 --> 01:02:16.607
the founder and CEO of the company is to
ensure that the company is always

01:02:17.400 --> 01:02:21.904
pushing towards the most ambitious
and expansive version of what it can be.

01:02:22.405 --> 01:02:28.453
And I think that one of the great things
about this entire MSL and AI push is

01:02:28.536 --> 01:02:31.914
that I think this is one of the rawest
expressions of that is

01:02:33.791 --> 01:02:39.338
we knew that AI certainly is a very
important technology and it's one

01:02:39.422 --> 01:02:40.757
of the bigger technologies right now.

01:02:40.840 --> 01:02:45.178
But
if we were able to build it effectively

01:02:45.261 --> 01:02:49.432
and properly integrate it into everything
that we do, as well as like, you know,

01:02:49.599 --> 01:02:52.477
as a full-stack technology company,
like build it into,

01:02:53.227 --> 01:02:55.855
you know, these incredible products
that we know will exist in the future,

01:02:55.938 --> 01:02:58.149
this could be like immensely
expansive for Meta.

01:02:58.524 --> 01:03:00.943
And I think this is true.

01:03:01.027 --> 01:03:04.739
Like, I think Muse is
an incredibly exciting product.

01:03:05.031 --> 01:03:08.785
It has like, you know,
it can go in 1,000 different directions.

01:03:09.368 --> 01:03:13.706
I think it's one of like probably the most
exciting products to just like You know,

01:03:14.123 --> 01:03:17.585
when like late at night and you just
think about all the ways it can go.

01:03:17.668 --> 01:03:22.215
Like, I think Nat, Mark, and I just like,
you know, shoot across messages late

01:03:22.298 --> 01:03:25.009
at night of just like, oh,
we could do this and we could do this and,

01:03:25.092 --> 01:03:27.470
you know, we can make this move
and we can like build this product.

01:03:27.553 --> 01:03:30.014
And I think it's just like,
you know, very—

01:03:30.139 --> 01:03:33.267
It's a very exciting time
in technology right now.

01:03:33.351 --> 01:03:35.269
Yeah, it's very blue ocean in that way.
Yeah.

01:03:35.520 --> 01:03:35.728
Yeah.

01:03:36.646 --> 01:03:38.189
I'm struck by, as we talk about

01:03:39.857 --> 01:03:44.529
Muse and just how it clearly feels like

01:03:44.904 --> 01:03:47.657
the next platform, an agent
operating on your behalf.

01:03:48.157 --> 01:03:51.702
You've talked for a long time about not
wanting to be

01:03:52.870 --> 01:03:56.707
reliant on other platforms and how kind
of the experience of building on

01:03:57.500 --> 01:04:01.170
Android and iOS kind of burned
you on being dependent on other.

01:04:01.254 --> 01:04:04.882
And often when you would talk about, say,
Reality Labs, is the importance of owning.

01:04:05.383 --> 01:04:08.427
the platform of the future
and kind of many years

01:04:08.886 --> 01:04:13.599
working on Reality Labs with the intent
of owning the platform of the future.

01:04:14.183 --> 01:04:18.604
And now
this new modality comes along that feels

01:04:18.688 --> 01:04:21.148
pretty clearly like how you do a lot
of productive work and is

01:04:21.232 --> 01:04:22.358
the platform of the future.

01:04:22.441 --> 01:04:24.235
And now Meta is really well set up.

01:04:24.569 --> 01:04:28.865
Yeah, no, I mean, it's less about wanting
to own a platform for its own sake.

01:04:28.948 --> 01:04:31.033
It's about being able
to build the best things.

01:04:31.409 --> 01:04:36.122
And at some level, you know, if you
look at like other social apps,

01:04:36.205 --> 01:04:39.125
for example,
I think part of the reason why Meta is so

01:04:39.208 --> 01:04:42.086
much better is because we don't
just build the app, right?

01:04:42.169 --> 01:04:44.005
A lot of people like think about it,
we're like this app company.

01:04:44.088 --> 01:04:45.298
We're not an app company, right?

01:04:45.381 --> 01:04:48.801
It's like we're like a deep technology
company and we do this because we

01:04:49.218 --> 01:04:52.305
are world-class at machine learning
and like all the recommendation systems

01:04:52.388 --> 01:04:55.683
are operating at a scale that basically
few others are in the world.

01:04:55.766 --> 01:04:58.477
I think it's basically like Just us
and ByteDance on that,

01:04:58.978 --> 01:05:03.733
and and then like okay we just kind
of go all the way to the metal.

01:05:04.150 --> 01:05:09.113
And to me, being able to also deliver
go the last mile through the device,

01:05:09.780 --> 01:05:14.911
it does come from a while of having these
experiences where we couldn't build

01:05:14.994 --> 01:05:16.746
the experiences that we wanted on phones.

01:05:18.205 --> 01:05:23.210
But I just think for the future,
the the models are going to be by far

01:05:23.294 --> 01:05:25.838
the most important part of delivering
the technology experience.

01:05:25.922 --> 01:05:31.010
And I mean, this is something that I think
we kind of bonded on and agreed on, like,

01:05:31.260 --> 01:05:35.640
very early, is this notion that, like,
in every generation of technology

01:05:35.723 --> 01:05:39.393
companies, there's, like,
no, there's usually not one company that

01:05:39.477 --> 01:05:41.562
kind of is the— is, like, wins everything.

01:05:41.646 --> 01:05:43.439
I don't think that that's how
this is going to go either.

01:05:43.522 --> 01:05:48.402
But with the internet, there were, like,
okay, 5 or 6 very big companies and then

01:05:48.486 --> 01:05:50.780
a whole ecosystem of other
companies that are great.

01:05:51.280 --> 01:05:56.285
The companies that did the best
kind of had the core technology to be

01:05:56.369 --> 01:05:59.330
able to serve people the best there.

01:05:59.789 --> 01:06:03.125
And I think in this next era,
there are going to probably be a small

01:06:03.250 --> 01:06:06.671
handful of companies that have the ability
to build frontier superintelligence.

01:06:07.213 --> 01:06:10.675
And I think that those companies are going
to be the ones that effectively drive

01:06:11.133 --> 01:06:14.345
the agenda and can build most
of the interesting, the most interesting

01:06:15.054 --> 01:06:17.682
experiences for consumers,
for enterprises, hardware,

01:06:17.765 --> 01:06:19.141
these different kinds of things.

01:06:19.225 --> 01:06:23.062
So, and I just look at it as, okay,
like, is it even really a choice, right?

01:06:23.145 --> 01:06:26.816
It's like, yeah, okay, Meta could,
will probably exist into the future

01:06:26.899 --> 01:06:28.401
regardless of whether we do this.

01:06:28.526 --> 01:06:31.696
But if we want to be
kind of an interesting company that is

01:06:31.862 --> 01:06:35.366
like inventing new things that matter
to people in the future,

01:06:35.741 --> 01:06:39.829
I don't see how you can do that if you
don't have the ability to build end-to-end

01:06:39.912 --> 01:06:43.040
your own superintelligence and build
them into the products that people use.

01:06:43.207 --> 01:06:45.668
And I think that this is something
that you just like believed from very

01:06:45.751 --> 01:06:48.838
early on,
Is basically, it's like, all right, there

01:06:48.921 --> 01:06:50.297
are going to be like a handful of these.

01:06:50.381 --> 01:06:54.093
Like, it's like there's a kind of personal
and consumer-oriented version of this.

01:06:54.760 --> 01:06:57.847
And like, this lane is open because
everyone else seems to just be obsessed

01:06:58.305 --> 01:07:01.726
so much with just automating knowledge
work, which, I mean, isn't even that

01:07:01.809 --> 01:07:03.769
charismatic version of the future, right?

01:07:03.853 --> 01:07:06.439
Because it's like, all right, like,
a big part of our belief too is

01:07:06.522 --> 01:07:10.484
that the kind of highest use
of superintelligence is invention

01:07:10.651 --> 01:07:12.194
and creation, not automation.

01:07:12.653 --> 01:07:15.114
And so, I don't know,
it just seemed like there's like a big

01:07:15.197 --> 01:07:17.700
place in there, but you
kind of need to own the stack.

01:07:21.287 --> 01:07:25.458
How is
the Muse-mediated future going to shake

01:07:25.541 --> 01:07:30.046
out with businesses need to— okay,

01:07:30.546 --> 01:07:33.507
businesses variously need to build support

01:07:34.133 --> 01:07:34.341
for

01:07:38.637 --> 01:07:42.683
for Muse, where it might try to computer

01:07:42.767 --> 01:07:45.644
use its way in, but that might
be janky in certain ways.

01:07:45.728 --> 01:07:48.439
So you kind of want everyone to have
first-class support

01:07:48.522 --> 01:07:50.024
for agents talking to things.
Yeah.

01:07:50.149 --> 01:07:54.779
Then the bigger question is they might
actively try to block agents

01:07:54.862 --> 01:07:56.238
from interacting with them.

01:07:56.322 --> 01:07:58.783
And this might be because they have
a misguided sense where, like,

01:07:58.866 --> 01:08:01.744
it's actually in their interest
to support agents, but they don't want to.

01:08:01.827 --> 01:08:04.413
It might actually not be in their
interest to support agents.

01:08:04.538 --> 01:08:08.167
You can imagine where maybe they have
a very thin moat and it all comes from

01:08:09.085 --> 01:08:13.005
a business that only succeeds by consumers
spending time on the site or only succeeds

01:08:13.089 --> 01:08:15.925
by overcharging consumers,
then they should rationally not want

01:08:16.008 --> 01:08:17.635
to allow agents because
the agents are like, why?

01:08:17.718 --> 01:08:19.804
But those are going to get disrupted.
Yeah.

01:08:20.179 --> 01:08:22.139
So
talk to me just through the second-order

01:08:22.223 --> 01:08:24.058
effects of how the economy works.

01:08:24.642 --> 01:08:27.436
Well, I think that it starts off with— I
mean, we trained into the models

01:08:27.520 --> 01:08:29.063
very good browser use, right?

01:08:29.522 --> 01:08:32.900
So that way we want to make it so that no
one No one has to do anything

01:08:32.983 --> 01:08:34.360
to integrate with Muse.

01:08:35.069 --> 01:08:38.364
And then, but then of course,
for— we're kind of just going down

01:08:38.447 --> 01:08:42.618
the list of the most important partners,
and for people who want to work with us,

01:08:43.077 --> 01:08:45.996
we'll just get direct API access, right?

01:08:46.080 --> 01:08:48.541
And then it's like, okay,
then it ends up being way more efficient

01:08:48.624 --> 01:08:50.459
and faster for people to use Muse.

01:08:50.543 --> 01:08:53.420
It's more efficient because now you don't
have an agent crawling your website.

01:08:53.504 --> 01:08:53.963
It's just going.

01:08:54.046 --> 01:08:56.841
So anyone who wants to do that,
we eventually want to get there.

01:08:57.508 --> 01:09:01.262
Um,
and then I think the fullness of time is

01:09:01.428 --> 01:09:04.723
that, you know, just like today
every business has,

01:09:05.391 --> 01:09:10.229
um, a website and social media accounts
and email addresses and all these things,

01:09:10.688 --> 01:09:12.231
every business is going
to have agents too.

01:09:12.314 --> 01:09:12.606
Mm-hmm.

01:09:12.690 --> 01:09:15.151
So, and we're also building
that on the other side of it.

01:09:15.609 --> 01:09:20.322
And, um, and so of course Muse agents will
talk to other types of agents, but they'll

01:09:20.406 --> 01:09:22.116
work really well with our agents, right?

01:09:22.199 --> 01:09:25.035
Because I mean, we'll,
we'll be able to integrate that Just,

01:09:25.161 --> 01:09:27.955
I mean, so Muse using websites
is just a moment in time thing.

01:09:28.164 --> 01:09:29.248
Just to make it easy for everyone.

01:09:29.331 --> 01:09:32.376
You guys want to have it as a backstop
so it's reliable for people using it.

01:09:32.668 --> 01:09:35.212
Yes, and easy for businesses
to get bootstrapped.

01:09:35.504 --> 01:09:40.342
But we have, we have way more businesses
that are calling us asking to integrate

01:09:40.426 --> 01:09:46.015
deeply, and very few really
who have tried to block it.

01:09:46.098 --> 01:09:49.226
And my guess is that the ones that try
to block it, it will end up not being

01:09:49.310 --> 01:09:50.811
the right strategic thing for them.

01:09:50.895 --> 01:09:51.896
But we'll see.

01:09:52.062 --> 01:09:52.646
This will kind of play out over time.

01:09:52.646 --> 01:09:54.815
It saves them a lot of time.
Yeah.

01:09:54.815 --> 01:09:55.941
It saves them a lot of money.

01:09:56.025 --> 01:09:59.236
Like, I think these are, these are, like,
clearly good things for the consumer.

01:09:59.320 --> 01:09:59.528
Yeah.

01:09:59.612 --> 01:10:03.490
So in the long arc,
you'd clearly expect that demand will

01:10:03.574 --> 01:10:08.454
shift from current avenues
more and more towards agentic demand.

01:10:09.330 --> 01:10:12.458
And, you know, there always will be demand
that lives elsewhere,

01:10:12.541 --> 01:10:15.836
just in the same way that, like, there's
still brick-and-mortar demand in commerce.

01:10:15.920 --> 01:10:17.463
But there's a lot of ecommerce demand.

01:10:17.546 --> 01:10:21.383
And kind of in the same way,
like, I think agentic commerce demand

01:10:21.467 --> 01:10:22.968
will grow as a portion of the pie.

01:10:23.052 --> 01:10:25.471
The others will still exist,
but will grow as a portion of the pie.

01:10:25.554 --> 01:10:28.891
And I think there's sort of these
questions for businesses in the long run,

01:10:28.974 --> 01:10:32.228
like,
do they want to participate in that slice

01:10:32.478 --> 01:10:35.064
or that portion of the economy over time?

01:10:35.689 --> 01:10:37.733
And I think it's
an incredibly exciting area.

01:10:37.816 --> 01:10:40.778
I think it's, like, very,
very exciting to imagine what will

01:10:40.861 --> 01:10:42.446
happen in this agentic commerce future.
Yes.

01:10:42.529 --> 01:10:45.157
I actually think Patrick
wrote a great tweeticle.

01:10:47.034 --> 01:10:48.369
Is that what we call it these days?
I don't know.

01:10:48.452 --> 01:10:50.162
Patrick has his own thing
going on with tweets.

01:10:50.246 --> 01:10:51.747
It's like they're buried amongst—

01:10:51.830 --> 01:10:57.795
I noticed that Brother Karamazov was
quite a good literary analysis.

01:10:57.878 --> 01:11:01.465
But yeah, no, I think clearly

01:11:02.049 --> 01:11:04.927
this is a discontinuous event for how

01:11:06.095 --> 01:11:09.056
commerce will happen, broadly speaking,
or just generally speaking, how

01:11:09.139 --> 01:11:12.601
the entire how all the business pipes
will work, maybe even in a broader sense.

01:11:12.851 --> 01:11:16.313
But I think that, you know,
you just go back to, this is clearly

01:11:16.397 --> 01:11:17.815
a lot better for the consumers.

01:11:17.898 --> 01:11:18.983
It's a lot better for the customers.

01:11:19.066 --> 01:11:22.069
So there's a natural gravity
towards that reality.

01:11:22.403 --> 01:11:25.072
One of the exciting things,
I'm curious what you guys see too,

01:11:25.155 --> 01:11:29.618
but I think Muse is one of the first
products where you're actually people are

01:11:29.702 --> 01:11:32.371
fully delegating purchases to the agents.

01:11:32.746 --> 01:11:35.833
And this is like a— this is kind of like
a, you know, a step change, right?

01:11:35.916 --> 01:11:39.670
Like, I think, you know,
we've been talking a lot about agentic

01:11:39.753 --> 01:11:42.631
shopping and like the possibility of it,
but now it's like really happening.

01:11:42.715 --> 01:11:45.050
And a lot of people are trusting
the agents to shop for them.

01:11:45.134 --> 01:11:48.470
And I think the agents are finding them,
you know, in my case, like great teas, or,

01:11:48.846 --> 01:11:52.182
you know, in whomever— whoever's case,
like great products for them to purchase,

01:11:52.516 --> 01:11:53.767
which is quite exciting.

01:11:53.934 --> 01:11:58.272
And so I think that there's There's
clearly an exciting thing happening here.

01:11:58.355 --> 01:12:01.317
I think, I think part of the value prop—
well, one, one part of the value prop

01:12:01.400 --> 01:12:02.943
for the user is just reduced friction.

01:12:03.027 --> 01:12:06.530
Like, hey, I don't feel like click on all
these websites and do all this research

01:12:06.613 --> 01:12:08.115
to try to find the exact products.

01:12:08.240 --> 01:12:10.075
Like, that step is taken care of for me.

01:12:10.159 --> 01:12:13.203
And also, like, criteria can
just be automatically applied.

01:12:13.329 --> 01:12:15.247
Like, hey, I need something by Wednesday.

01:12:15.497 --> 01:12:18.083
So, you know, the agent will only look for
things that could get to me by Wednesday.

01:12:18.167 --> 01:12:19.168
But that's, I mean, keyword search.

01:12:19.251 --> 01:12:20.669
It was insane that we ever relied on it
because that's a very

01:12:20.753 --> 01:12:22.338
reasonable constraint.
You just want to shop by.

01:12:22.421 --> 01:12:23.130
Yeah, exactly.

01:12:24.340 --> 01:12:28.344
And so I think the like old way of like
searching for stuff just feels so

01:12:28.635 --> 01:12:31.013
obviously primitive now,
now that you can do this.

01:12:31.347 --> 01:12:34.725
Then there's this sort of like
back part of the funnel, so to speak,

01:12:34.808 --> 01:12:38.103
but just like the ease of friction
reduction of just, yeah,

01:12:38.228 --> 01:12:41.523
filling out the form, just like
getting it ordered is quite nice.

01:12:42.107 --> 01:12:45.569
And I think the long-term
thing that we're quite excited about

01:12:46.195 --> 01:12:47.863
is greater personalization.

01:12:48.280 --> 01:12:52.201
Ultimately,
I think one of the exciting things about

01:12:52.284 --> 01:12:58.749
Muse is that it has sort of like this
holistic context into you as a person

01:12:58.832 --> 01:13:00.417
and all of the aspects of your life.

01:13:00.501 --> 01:13:04.338
So it sort of like can holistically
understand things about your family or

01:13:04.421 --> 01:13:06.632
things about your coworkers
or things about your friends.

01:13:06.715 --> 01:13:11.345
And then sort of like help you co-optimize
across all of those to get you the exact

01:13:11.428 --> 01:13:16.016
right gift for your, you know,
best friend in the context of your friend

01:13:16.100 --> 01:13:17.267
group based on what it knows
about your friend group.

01:13:17.351 --> 01:13:19.978
Like, I think there's just like a lot
of exciting opportunities

01:13:20.813 --> 01:13:24.650
that are somewhat new and novel
when it comes to like these

01:13:24.733 --> 01:13:25.818
personalizations, these recommendations.

01:13:25.901 --> 01:13:28.404
Because historically,
a lot of these recommendation systems are

01:13:29.238 --> 01:13:31.698
quite siloed and quite isolated.
Mm-hmm.

01:13:32.074 --> 01:13:35.160
And so they're sort of like,
they don't see like a holistic view

01:13:35.702 --> 01:13:37.162
of you as a person.

01:13:37.663 --> 01:13:41.083
And Muse has an opportunity to offer
much greater levels of personalization.

01:13:41.500 --> 01:13:44.044
Yeah,
I think people are already seeing that,

01:13:44.128 --> 01:13:46.463
where if they ask,
like with Muse,

01:13:46.547 --> 01:13:49.883
if you ask it to buy something for you,
quite frequently it'll add in some bit

01:13:49.967 --> 01:13:52.594
of context that it pulled
in from a different conversation.

01:13:52.761 --> 01:13:54.555
You had a good example of this.
Yeah.

01:13:54.847 --> 01:13:59.977
Well, so my 3-year-old
daughter loves baking.

01:14:00.185 --> 01:14:01.145
She loves cooking.

01:14:01.228 --> 01:14:03.355
And so I'm like, all right,
I'll start baking stuff.

01:14:03.480 --> 01:14:08.444
It's like I'm normally into smoking meats,
but I'll do some baking in order

01:14:08.527 --> 01:14:10.696
to To make, to make Aurelia happy.

01:14:11.530 --> 01:14:14.658
And so we've gotten into this
really cute routine where,

01:14:15.951 --> 01:14:19.329
um, you know, we, we,
I've been playing with Muse voice mode,

01:14:19.538 --> 01:14:22.749
which isn't fully rolled out yet,
but we're, we're playing with it.

01:14:23.000 --> 01:14:25.085
And, um, it does this great thing.

01:14:25.169 --> 01:14:26.795
It segments the, the voices.

01:14:26.879 --> 01:14:30.883
So it knows which one is me and it
knows which one is not me based on that.

01:14:31.508 --> 01:14:35.387
And we just like, you know,
throughout the week we're talking about

01:14:35.471 --> 01:14:37.097
what we'll want to bake that weekend.
Mm-hmm.

01:14:37.181 --> 01:14:42.269
And she'll like
give it ideas or like she'll kind of come

01:14:42.352 --> 01:14:48.233
up with crazy things that are not real
recipes, but like bounce it off with Muse.

01:14:48.358 --> 01:14:51.904
And then Muse will just make sure that we,

01:14:52.613 --> 01:14:55.908
every Saturday when it's time to bake,

01:14:56.700 --> 01:15:01.038
I get the ingredients from Instacart
and they kind of show up and they're

01:15:01.121 --> 01:15:04.082
on time, which I mean,
the biggest thing is like if I were just

01:15:04.166 --> 01:15:07.252
starting the activity on Saturday,
you'd have to get everything

01:15:07.336 --> 01:15:08.420
and it would take some time.

01:15:08.504 --> 01:15:11.381
But now everything's ready whenever
we're going to go sit down and do it.

01:15:12.216 --> 01:15:15.052
This weekend it knew that watermelon was
launching, so it threw

01:15:15.135 --> 01:15:16.428
a watermelon in the bag.

01:15:16.512 --> 01:15:18.263
It was very, very sweet.

01:15:18.347 --> 01:15:19.932
It was very heartwarming.

01:15:21.058 --> 01:15:25.312
It deduced that the recipe did not have

01:15:25.395 --> 01:15:29.775
enough— there was not enough, I guess,

01:15:29.858 --> 01:15:33.278
expense in it to get the delivery,
so it needed something else.

01:15:33.362 --> 01:15:34.905
And it was like, you're going to—
And it topped it up with a watermelon.

01:15:34.988 --> 01:15:36.865
It was like, do you want this watermelon?
I was like, yes.

01:15:36.949 --> 01:15:38.784
I'm like, yes, that's very cute.

01:15:38.867 --> 01:15:41.036
Which I think you, uh, did you—
is that the watermelon you ate?

01:15:41.119 --> 01:15:43.247
Yeah, that was the one
I ate on the livestream.

01:15:43.622 --> 01:15:45.123
Okay, but the watermelon
on the livestream was—

01:15:45.249 --> 01:15:47.042
That was just like,
Muse just got it for me.

01:15:47.125 --> 01:15:48.669
That was a Muse—yeah, those are just—

01:15:48.835 --> 01:15:50.546
You just got it for me.

01:15:50.629 --> 01:15:52.422
But I think there's this, um—

01:15:52.506 --> 01:15:56.051
That was a very memeable moment
of that very memeable situation.

01:15:56.134 --> 01:15:58.262
So we have Muse to thank for it.

01:15:58.345 --> 01:16:01.932
So, but here's another interesting thing
about the whole thing is I didn't

01:16:02.015 --> 01:16:04.101
really use Instacart that much before.

01:16:04.184 --> 01:16:08.146
You know, it's like every once in a while,
maybe twice a year, I would like like,

01:16:08.480 --> 01:16:10.524
use Priscilla's account or something.
Yes.

01:16:10.649 --> 01:16:15.904
I went and got an Instacart account
to give Muse, and now I use it like once

01:16:15.988 --> 01:16:19.324
or twice a week because like I'm talking
with Aurelia with Muse,

01:16:19.408 --> 01:16:22.452
and we're coming up with these recipes,
and it's delivering them, and now

01:16:22.536 --> 01:16:24.079
it's like a core part of my workflow.

01:16:24.162 --> 01:16:28.500
So I actually think that the opportunity
here isn't just, okay, it's going to be

01:16:28.584 --> 01:16:30.794
20% more efficient on the conversion rate.

01:16:31.044 --> 01:16:34.172
Like, I am a new Instacart like
subscriber, and I just

01:16:34.256 --> 01:16:35.465
wouldn't have done this before.
Yes.

01:16:35.549 --> 01:16:38.093
So, I do think that there's this question,
and then there's this question,

01:16:38.176 --> 01:16:42.222
which I think is
these sort of interesting— like,

01:16:42.764 --> 01:16:45.058
this is maybe the more interesting
economic question,

01:16:45.684 --> 01:16:48.854
is that Instacart account really
for me, or is it for the Muse?

01:16:48.937 --> 01:16:51.940
Because I think, like, over time,
I think that the question is,

01:16:52.024 --> 01:16:56.069
is the total addressable market for all
of commerce just increasing dramatically?

01:16:56.278 --> 01:16:58.697
Yes.
Because it's not just the people anymore.

01:16:58.822 --> 01:17:01.158
Like, now agents are effectively
going to have accounts.

01:17:01.241 --> 01:17:01.867
Yes.

01:17:01.950 --> 01:17:05.495
And I think that's where this starts to,
like, become a really very exciting

01:17:05.579 --> 01:17:08.540
opportunity where anyone who sits this
out, I think, is going to end

01:17:08.624 --> 01:17:09.791
up feeling sad over time.

01:17:10.000 --> 01:17:12.878
And why did you have to sign
up for an Instacart account?

01:17:13.337 --> 01:17:16.089
Like, we're solving this
at Stripe Projects for developer

01:17:16.173 --> 01:17:18.759
stuff where your coding agent—

01:17:18.842 --> 01:17:20.636
The Stripe integration,
by the way, is great.

01:17:20.719 --> 01:17:23.513
You guys did a great job with us on Muse.

01:17:23.639 --> 01:17:25.474
I think we're very happy.

01:17:25.641 --> 01:17:28.518
We have been very excited about
agent-to-commerce for a long time,

01:17:28.602 --> 01:17:31.146
obviously, because again,
felt to us like clearly how people

01:17:31.229 --> 01:17:33.315
are going to buy things in the future.

01:17:34.608 --> 01:17:38.445
And that was both apparent because no one
needs all that friction in their life

01:17:38.528 --> 01:17:43.283
from the actual buying stage,
and how early product research with AI had

01:17:43.367 --> 01:17:46.453
product-market fit,
even from kind of the earliest AIs.

01:17:47.037 --> 01:17:50.916
It really feels like computer use was
the last big unlock that we needed to make

01:17:50.999 --> 01:17:54.544
it happen, because now you can ask your
muse to buy anything, more or less.

01:17:54.920 --> 01:17:57.214
And it'll just figure it out
with computer use, right?

01:17:57.297 --> 01:17:59.049
And so it's like the ultimate
backwards compatibility layer.

01:17:59.132 --> 01:17:59.549
Yeah, it's pretty—

01:17:59.633 --> 01:18:01.551
this is a set of things
that Nat really obsessed over.

01:18:01.635 --> 01:18:02.135
Yeah, yeah.

01:18:02.344 --> 01:18:05.889
I think, I think there was a lot of,
like, I think a lot of magic of Muse is

01:18:05.972 --> 01:18:08.475
the fact that like a lot
of this stuff just works.

01:18:08.850 --> 01:18:12.437
And so like clearly
if everything was just like a little bit

01:18:12.521 --> 01:18:15.524
less reliable, it would feel like a total
pain in the ass and nobody would use

01:18:15.607 --> 01:18:16.900
it and people just throw away the app.

01:18:16.983 --> 01:18:20.153
And so I think the fact that so much
of this has gotten to the point

01:18:20.237 --> 01:18:24.282
of reliability where You know, it just
works is like a huge part of the magic.

01:18:24.408 --> 01:18:24.825
Yeah.

01:18:24.908 --> 01:18:26.284
What were you saying
Matt was obsessed with?

01:18:26.368 --> 01:18:29.496
Oh, well, I mean, well,
Matt's kind of obsessed with everything.

01:18:29.621 --> 01:18:30.914
Yeah, well, that's
generally a sexy character.

01:18:30.997 --> 01:18:32.374
Yeah, no, it's great, it's great.

01:18:32.457 --> 01:18:35.293
But this is, I mean, but yeah, I mean,
this getting this experience to be good,

01:18:35.377 --> 01:18:38.296
I think is something that he and a core
group of people on that

01:18:38.380 --> 01:18:39.548
team really focused on.

01:18:39.631 --> 01:18:41.133
It's kind of funny.

01:18:41.216 --> 01:18:44.886
I think I had a dinner with Patrick, like,
I want to say it was 2023,

01:18:46.513 --> 01:18:48.473
where I was, he was like,
what do you think we should be doing now?

01:18:48.557 --> 01:18:51.601
I was Oh, you're like,
you're already way behind the 8-ball

01:18:51.685 --> 01:18:52.894
on this agentic commerce stuff.

01:18:52.978 --> 01:18:54.521
Like, you got to figure this stuff out.

01:18:54.604 --> 01:18:57.733
Like, agents are going to be buying things
and like, you're not prepared for it.

01:18:57.816 --> 01:18:59.276
So you were saying this to Patrick
or Patrick saying this to you?

01:18:59.359 --> 01:19:00.444
I was saying this to Patrick.

01:19:00.569 --> 01:19:02.654
Well, because Patrick was very politely
asking, what do you

01:19:02.738 --> 01:19:03.488
think we should be doing?

01:19:03.572 --> 01:19:06.283
And I sort of had this like
very like urgent spiel.

01:19:06.408 --> 01:19:09.286
Yeah, and I think it's very, uh,
it's funny how that was like—

01:19:09.369 --> 01:19:10.245
Well, thank you for your provocation.

01:19:10.328 --> 01:19:14.249
Well, it was directionally correct,
but, uh, but like 3 years early.

01:19:14.833 --> 01:19:15.792
2023, I think.

01:19:15.917 --> 01:19:18.545
And so, um,
no, it's very interesting to look

01:19:18.628 --> 01:19:22.591
at the agentic commerce stats
at Stripe because

01:19:23.049 --> 01:19:25.594
we started developing all this stuff
and we had a whole bunch of announcements

01:19:25.677 --> 01:19:29.723
at Sessions back in May,
uh, and then it only as of September

01:19:29.806 --> 01:19:31.475
basically started going vertical.

01:19:34.311 --> 01:19:37.689
Meta is building increasingly capable
models, including Muse Wonder,

01:19:38.064 --> 01:19:41.401
and their models are available on
OpenRouter, Alongside hundreds of others.

01:19:42.068 --> 01:19:46.072
Stripe has agreed to acquire OpenRouter,
who's the leading model gateway.

01:19:46.782 --> 01:19:50.619
OpenRouter already routes more than 10
trillion tokens a day and handles much

01:19:50.702 --> 01:19:53.163
of the complexity of working
with different models through a single

01:19:53.246 --> 01:19:57.542
API, making it easier to experiment,
find competitive prices, and automatically

01:19:57.626 --> 01:19:59.044
route around provider outages.

01:19:59.628 --> 01:20:01.963
Try it for yourself at openrouter.
ai.

01:20:06.635 --> 01:20:11.973
Hey, could you order a watermelon
to Stripe's office in South San Francisco?

01:20:12.265 --> 01:20:14.476
I'm doing a podcast here.
This would be funny.

01:20:16.686 --> 01:20:17.854
Funny podcast idea.

01:20:18.480 --> 01:20:20.315
I'll check that watermelon delivery now.

01:20:22.359 --> 01:20:24.027
Great.
We'll see how we get on.

01:20:26.571 --> 01:20:27.030
Well, okay.

01:20:27.155 --> 01:20:29.324
This actually raises
an interesting question.

01:20:29.825 --> 01:20:34.454
People have been trying
to make The HAL 9000.

01:20:34.579 --> 01:20:35.372
It's working.

01:20:36.748 --> 01:20:37.582
I really like this.

01:20:37.666 --> 01:20:40.502
I think this is an awesome touch where
it's like clacking away at its laptop.

01:20:40.585 --> 01:20:42.295
I really like that in the app.

01:20:43.421 --> 01:20:46.508
We'll just leave them there
and we'll see what we get.

01:20:46.716 --> 01:20:48.927
Yeah, it's tough to prop up.

01:20:49.052 --> 01:20:50.428
Yeah, there we go.

01:20:52.222 --> 01:20:55.767
People have been trying to make the
speak to a computer AI vision

01:20:55.851 --> 01:20:57.394
happen for a really long time.

01:20:57.978 --> 01:21:03.233
Where Apple launched Siri in 2014,
2015, something like that.

01:21:03.775 --> 01:21:08.280
And obviously there's been Voice Mode and
various AI products for a few years now.

01:21:08.405 --> 01:21:10.198
Why is it so hard?
Like, it's still not the case.

01:21:10.282 --> 01:21:14.160
If you were driving your car and you
wanted to check on the baking or move

01:21:14.244 --> 01:21:16.121
a meeting or something like that,
you actually probably

01:21:16.204 --> 01:21:17.789
wouldn't do it with the voice.

01:21:17.873 --> 01:21:19.833
I mean, maybe almost now
with the latest things.

01:21:19.916 --> 01:21:20.709
Yeah, well, there's a lot.

01:21:20.792 --> 01:21:22.294
I mean, so— But you wouldn't
develop Siri like you're doing.

01:21:22.377 --> 01:21:24.796
Siri right now is actually a good example.
As we're talking over each other.

01:21:24.880 --> 01:21:26.172
And I mean, I don't know,
when you're using different voices—

01:21:26.256 --> 01:21:29.009
So is it just technical limitations?
That's why we haven't done it?

01:21:29.092 --> 01:21:30.844
Well, I think that there's a few things.

01:21:30.927 --> 01:21:32.929
And one is you actually
want it to go do stuff.

01:21:33.013 --> 01:21:35.974
So there's a question of when you speak
with it, even if it understands,

01:21:36.308 --> 01:21:37.559
how's that going to work?

01:21:38.018 --> 01:21:40.937
Then I think that there's a lot of stuff
about the way that people

01:21:41.229 --> 01:21:42.063
have conversations.

01:21:42.230 --> 01:21:46.860
It isn't typically, I will give a prompt,
and now I will wait, and you will speak.

01:21:47.027 --> 01:21:48.778
And then, so it just goes back and forth.

01:21:49.487 --> 01:21:51.656
Um,
how many of your sessions when you're

01:21:51.740 --> 01:21:56.036
talking to an agent in voice mode,
do you basically, oh,

01:21:56.119 --> 01:21:59.164
it starts saying something,
but then like, I don't know, it's like,

01:21:59.247 --> 01:22:02.167
I kind of feel like if I pause for 3
seconds, it's going to think

01:22:02.250 --> 01:22:03.835
I'm done and want to speak.

01:22:03.919 --> 01:22:07.255
And then if it starts speaking,
but then I cut back in, is it going to

01:22:07.339 --> 01:22:09.424
think that it's like a whole new question?

01:22:09.925 --> 01:22:12.844
Um, I think there's a lot of the stuff
about the conversational dynamics

01:22:12.928 --> 01:22:14.095
that you want to get right.

01:22:14.429 --> 01:22:20.560
But then there's this other issue,
which is that For voice overall,

01:22:21.770 --> 01:22:24.814
it's quite high bandwidth for you
to communicate with voice,

01:22:25.523 --> 01:22:30.070
but in terms of consuming information,
having someone speak to you is very

01:22:30.153 --> 01:22:32.072
inefficient compared
to having it be visual.

01:22:32.280 --> 01:22:32.489
Yeah.

01:22:32.572 --> 01:22:35.867
So I think like that's why, you know,
partially when we do these,

01:22:36.868 --> 01:22:39.746
these interfaces, I think it's,
it's kind of working on its task.

01:22:39.829 --> 01:22:40.789
I fired it off now.

01:22:41.373 --> 01:22:45.085
So I think the ideal interface is
probably not just strictly voice-voice.

01:22:45.585 --> 01:22:49.798
Although I think voice
output from you is pretty useful.

01:22:50.298 --> 01:22:54.010
Yeah, I think, I think there very clearly
already is an explosion of voice input.

01:22:54.386 --> 01:22:57.013
Like, I think you even see
this in coding where like—

01:22:57.347 --> 01:22:59.099
Yeah, people are speaking to the—
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:22:59.182 --> 01:23:03.353
Most of the researchers just kind of like
whisper into their keyboard because it is

01:23:03.436 --> 01:23:07.524
like, as Mark's mentioning, it's like
the highest bandwidth way to get your like

01:23:07.607 --> 01:23:10.568
thoughts out, or usually the lowest
friction way to get your thoughts out.

01:23:10.652 --> 01:23:11.403
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:23:11.653 --> 01:23:14.781
I think the most extreme version of this,
by the way, is I think how

01:23:16.199 --> 01:23:18.868
college students now use AI is maybe
the most interesting,

01:23:18.952 --> 01:23:22.288
where they just— I think a very common
modality of how to use

01:23:23.039 --> 01:23:28.920
AI is that they just will
completely word vomit for 10, 15 minutes,

01:23:29.170 --> 01:23:32.799
being full stream of consciousness,
chain of thought of all the things.

01:23:33.049 --> 01:23:35.051
And then have AI clean it up and infer.
Yeah.

01:23:35.135 --> 01:23:40.348
And have AI effectively do all of this
organization of it and pulling things

01:23:40.682 --> 01:23:42.392
together and structuring everything.

01:23:42.475 --> 01:23:43.560
It's quite interesting.

01:23:43.643 --> 01:23:46.771
It's a very interesting,
I think, modality of tech usage.

01:23:47.105 --> 01:23:47.147
Yeah.

01:23:47.230 --> 01:23:49.149
But yeah, I think voice
input has been big.

01:23:49.315 --> 01:23:54.029
I do think agents is like,
that's a big step change, because I think

01:23:54.112 --> 01:23:57.449
the mental model is just very different.

01:23:57.532 --> 01:24:02.120
Like, if it's just like an informational
thing that you're talking to, that like,

01:24:02.537 --> 01:24:04.998
you will clearly get into situations
where it just feels useless.

01:24:05.081 --> 01:24:08.960
Whereas now that it can do things for you,
it's much closer to the experience

01:24:09.044 --> 01:24:10.211
of talking to a human.
Totally.

01:24:10.295 --> 01:24:11.004
Or to a person.

01:24:11.546 --> 01:24:16.760
And so I think there's all these
interesting uncanny valleys in interacting

01:24:16.843 --> 01:24:22.474
with AIs that until they've been able
to take actions have felt kind of strange.

01:24:22.557 --> 01:24:28.146
And now that we have agents,
I think one of the cool things about Muse

01:24:28.229 --> 01:24:29.898
is this confluence of a number of things.

01:24:30.315 --> 01:24:34.736
I think The fact that Muse is embodied
and it like is this little guy

01:24:35.320 --> 01:24:38.990
that like you feel like is working on your
behalf is also only possible

01:24:39.074 --> 01:24:44.162
and a coherent like representation
and embodiment of the product because

01:24:44.662 --> 01:24:48.583
it has persistent memory and it has
the ability to do things for you

01:24:48.666 --> 01:24:51.669
and the ability to take actions and has
like a little bit of a personality.

01:24:51.753 --> 01:24:54.798
Like, is the confluence of all these
things coming together that make

01:24:54.881 --> 01:24:56.341
this like embodiment concept— Yes.

01:24:57.175 --> 01:24:58.384
Even work in the first place.

01:24:59.177 --> 01:25:01.429
Then it turns out, okay,
actually this is the real glasses, right?

01:25:01.513 --> 01:25:05.225
It's like, okay, a lot of people want
it even with just like no

01:25:05.308 --> 01:25:06.851
display or a small display.
Yes.

01:25:07.769 --> 01:25:10.772
The camera and audio is
enough of a useful product.

01:25:11.106 --> 01:25:12.023
Yeah, yeah.

01:25:12.107 --> 01:25:16.694
And I— and then we started shipping
the display glasses that aren't the kind

01:25:16.778 --> 01:25:19.781
of wide field of view full augmented
reality, which, I mean,

01:25:19.864 --> 01:25:25.120
I thought would be awesome too, but even
the small display is pretty powerful.

01:25:25.203 --> 01:25:28.665
So I think you'll start getting some
interesting interactions there that are

01:25:29.457 --> 01:25:32.001
kind of fully multimodal, right?

01:25:32.127 --> 01:25:36.339
So it's voice in and out and It'll be able
to see what you see, and it'll be

01:25:36.422 --> 01:25:38.842
able to kind of paint the pixels.

01:25:39.592 --> 01:25:42.178
And one of the— I mean,
the other exciting thing that

01:25:43.138 --> 01:25:45.431
we've done here is Muse Gadgets,
which is really,

01:25:45.890 --> 01:25:51.938
I think, unleashing the ecosystem to try
cool device formats and form factors

01:25:52.272 --> 01:25:53.398
that could be really exciting.

01:25:53.481 --> 01:25:56.651
Like, since releasing that,
we've gotten actually even a number

01:25:56.734 --> 01:26:00.196
of phone manufacturers,
you know, talk to us about excited You

01:26:00.280 --> 01:26:02.407
know, we're like,
we're just going to integrate Muse Gadgets

01:26:02.490 --> 01:26:04.868
into our phone and see how well it works.
And like, we're really excited about that.

01:26:04.951 --> 01:26:07.996
Like, I think, um,
I think part of what is cool about

01:26:08.079 --> 01:26:12.000
the current moment of technology is that
like, it's very clear

01:26:12.083 --> 01:26:13.251
agents are really exciting.

01:26:13.334 --> 01:26:16.504
It's really clear that like,
this should unlock lots of things.

01:26:16.713 --> 01:26:18.548
Will you guys come back
to the household devices?

01:26:18.631 --> 01:26:21.634
Like, this is obviously a personal device,
but, um, we actually had

01:26:21.718 --> 01:26:25.013
during COVID the, um, Portress—
the Portal.

01:26:25.096 --> 01:26:25.889
Portal.
There we go.

01:26:26.222 --> 01:26:28.474
Yeah, I mean, like,
he's finding the Portal inside.

01:26:28.641 --> 01:26:30.977
One of the gadgets that we just
shipped was the little HomeLink.

01:26:31.060 --> 01:26:34.063
It's like the size of a little,
like, nickel or something,

01:26:34.230 --> 01:26:36.900
but with a USB, right?
Yeah.

01:26:37.066 --> 01:26:41.279
Um, uh, so you can basically connect that,
and then your Muse can connect

01:26:41.362 --> 01:26:42.155
to everything in your home.

01:26:42.238 --> 01:26:47.285
This is actually one of the most fun
things too is just having it connect to

01:26:47.577 --> 01:26:49.579
your Sonos and connect to all your lights.

01:26:49.662 --> 01:26:53.166
I mean, I have it basically
like when it's time for me to start

01:26:53.249 --> 01:26:56.336
winding down in the evening,
I just have it like dim all the lights

01:26:56.544 --> 01:26:59.631
across the house and it's nice.

01:27:00.089 --> 01:27:01.925
How are we going to fund the AI buildout?

01:27:02.050 --> 01:27:05.053
Everyone's worried about, you know,
Google went free cash flow negative.

01:27:05.637 --> 01:27:08.681
You know, you were talking about 1
gigawatt and then 5 gigawatts

01:27:08.765 --> 01:27:09.641
and everything like this.

01:27:10.058 --> 01:27:15.313
Meta's now free cash flow negative yet,
but there's always more CapEx to fund.

01:27:15.688 --> 01:27:18.483
And so what's the answer?
Where is Susan Lee?

01:27:18.566 --> 01:27:19.067
Exactly.

01:27:19.150 --> 01:27:24.447
No,
I mean, look, I think that there are going

01:27:24.530 --> 01:27:26.407
to be a lot of ways
to create value from this.

01:27:26.866 --> 01:27:31.996
So what we're trying to do with Muse
specifically is make it so that it can be

01:27:32.705 --> 01:27:35.041
widely used,
because I think that there are going to be

01:27:35.124 --> 01:27:39.796
some kind of valuable network effects
at scale as people start being able

01:27:39.879 --> 01:27:43.049
to interact with each other's,
and that's going to be great.

01:27:43.132 --> 01:27:45.927
So I think there are all these reasons
why you want it to get to large scale.

01:27:46.010 --> 01:27:47.387
Also, you know, we were
talking about partners.

01:27:47.470 --> 01:27:50.014
Partners are going to want to integrate
more the more people are using it.

01:27:50.098 --> 01:27:52.392
So it will get more valuable
if we can get it to scale.

01:27:53.059 --> 01:27:56.562
The best way to do that is to basically
have a model where it is affordable

01:27:56.980 --> 01:27:58.356
and free for people to use.

01:27:58.940 --> 01:28:02.485
But there are going to be a lot of ways
that I think we can scale revenue.

01:28:03.653 --> 01:28:05.863
Subscriptions is going to be a big one.

01:28:06.072 --> 01:28:09.826
You know, basically,
just because the base version of it is

01:28:09.951 --> 01:28:12.996
free and we want the free version to be
excellent doesn't mean that there are

01:28:13.079 --> 01:28:16.165
things that just naturally aren't going
to be economical to include in that.

01:28:16.499 --> 01:28:20.670
So, you know, if you want the kind of—
you know, we talked about this earlier.

01:28:20.753 --> 01:28:25.258
If you want to start using Muse Wonder
in Muse tomorrow, then, you know,

01:28:25.341 --> 01:28:28.136
the way that you'll do that is
you'll pay for a subscription.

01:28:28.386 --> 01:28:30.221
So I think that that will
be a big business.

01:28:31.055 --> 01:28:33.599
There are a bunch of other things around
commerce that we're quite excited about

01:28:33.683 --> 01:28:37.812
too, where we basically think— we believe
that Muse is actually going to make people

01:28:37.895 --> 01:28:42.191
money and save people money by using it,
and that over time there are lots

01:28:42.275 --> 01:28:46.362
of different ways to effectively take
a small cut of what you are saving or

01:28:46.446 --> 01:28:50.742
making for people, and that will end
up being a very large revenue stream.

01:28:51.284 --> 01:28:52.702
And then there's all
the other things, right?

01:28:52.785 --> 01:28:56.789
So, you think Muse will generally
just cost negative money for people?

01:28:57.040 --> 01:28:58.416
because it helps them out.

01:28:58.499 --> 01:29:00.543
Well, there are already all these sites
that I think are interesting where people

01:29:00.626 --> 01:29:03.963
are like, go look through my emails,
find a bunch of subscriptions I don't

01:29:04.047 --> 01:29:06.257
actually use, cancel them, whatever.

01:29:06.341 --> 01:29:10.970
Um, yeah, so yeah, I mean, that, um,
I don't know if that's the thing that we

01:29:11.054 --> 01:29:13.890
would monetize, but, but like,
but you get, you get, you kind of get the

01:29:13.973 --> 01:29:15.558
point and where it helps you make money.

01:29:15.641 --> 01:29:18.436
I mean, one of the ideas that we kind
of talk about, I mean, this is,

01:29:18.519 --> 01:29:21.439
this is something that I think you've been
kind of obsessed with, is making it like

01:29:21.522 --> 01:29:23.399
a business in a box for small businesses.

01:29:24.400 --> 01:29:26.652
So someone can just kind of— it can help
them administer all these

01:29:26.736 --> 01:29:27.570
parts of what they're doing.

01:29:27.653 --> 01:29:30.865
And you can imagine that being very
valuable and being very profitable,

01:29:30.948 --> 01:29:33.159
both for the business owner and for Meta.

01:29:35.078 --> 01:29:37.413
But I think one of the things that's
interesting

01:29:38.122 --> 01:29:43.586
is, like, OK, if we're building the models
to build the personal agents

01:29:43.669 --> 01:29:47.006
and the business agent,
there is this large surplus of value

01:29:47.090 --> 01:29:49.050
because now the model,
it's very general, right?

01:29:49.133 --> 01:29:50.885
So it doesn't just do those things.

01:29:50.968 --> 01:29:53.012
We'll also have the API
that'll be profitable.

01:29:53.846 --> 01:29:58.184
A big part of why we
want to start the Meta Enterprise Platform

01:29:58.267 --> 01:30:01.687
and have CJ Desai join us to build this is
we have this view, which is like,

01:30:01.771 --> 01:30:03.022
all right, yeah, sure.

01:30:03.106 --> 01:30:05.525
So traditionally Meta hasn't been
an enterprise company,

01:30:05.942 --> 01:30:08.736
but we believe that a huge part
of the challenge is building

01:30:08.903 --> 01:30:10.863
the leading models in the first place.
And they're very general.

01:30:10.947 --> 01:30:16.786
And once we do that, if we make
a significant investment in building

01:30:17.245 --> 01:30:21.499
an enterprise group that can provide
that in the way that enterprises need

01:30:21.582 --> 01:30:24.085
to consume it, then I mean,
I think a lot of enterprises are

01:30:24.168 --> 01:30:25.461
going to want to use Muse Wonder.

01:30:25.545 --> 01:30:27.839
It's going to be an excellent
model and affordable.

01:30:27.922 --> 01:30:31.801
And like, it's so, um,
so I think that that is going to end

01:30:31.884 --> 01:30:33.636
up being a very big business too.

01:30:33.928 --> 01:30:38.474
And all of these things put together,
coupled with the fact that we were already

01:30:38.558 --> 01:30:41.227
starting off as a very profitable business
that can,

01:30:41.727 --> 01:30:45.940
that has good credit and can kind
of underwrite these large investments,

01:30:46.023 --> 01:30:50.236
I think puts us in a pretty good position
as a lab to be able to build out

01:30:50.319 --> 01:30:54.031
the compute that we need to build leading
products, build up the network around

01:30:54.115 --> 01:30:59.620
this, and position the company to really
lead in the area for decades to come.

01:30:59.704 --> 01:31:00.163
Okay.

01:31:00.246 --> 01:31:02.582
So, you think ultimately there'll just be
a lot of revenue from these products,

01:31:02.665 --> 01:31:04.750
and so they'll be self-funding
in fairly short order?

01:31:04.917 --> 01:31:05.168
Yeah.

01:31:05.793 --> 01:31:07.545
I mean, that's a much
shorter answer than mine.

01:31:07.628 --> 01:31:10.256
It's like— But you're saying
people are underestimating—

01:31:10.339 --> 01:31:13.217
The question was, Are we going
to be able to make money from this?

01:31:13.926 --> 01:31:15.011
The answer better be yes.

01:31:16.095 --> 01:31:18.264
I think the history— Yes.

01:31:18.347 --> 01:31:19.640
The answer is yes.

01:31:19.724 --> 01:31:21.976
But the history is very
front-loaded expense, right?

01:31:22.059 --> 01:31:25.104
Well, the history of AI as an industry,
I think, to some degree is like,

01:31:25.480 --> 01:31:28.941
you know, people have always sort of been
hand-wringing about, you know, how are

01:31:29.025 --> 01:31:30.526
these businesses going to make money?

01:31:30.610 --> 01:31:34.405
And I think, you know, every year
people are sort of generally

01:31:34.489 --> 01:31:35.781
surprised at the upside.

01:31:36.324 --> 01:31:39.243
That superintelligence is Is
a useful, incredibly valuable—

01:31:39.368 --> 01:31:40.161
Yeah, exactly.
This is what—

01:31:40.244 --> 01:31:42.079
this is kind of Meta's
whole playbook, right?

01:31:42.163 --> 01:31:44.999
Is we build something,
we get it to a billion people,

01:31:45.208 --> 01:31:49.629
then we scale the revenue while
growing our revenue primarily

01:31:49.712 --> 01:31:53.216
in the meantime by continuing to scale
the things that are already

01:31:53.382 --> 01:31:54.884
kind of at scale and growing.
Yeah.

01:31:55.051 --> 01:31:58.221
I think this is also one of the dynamics,
like, I think,

01:31:59.305 --> 01:32:01.599
you know,
I think Dario's talked about this as well,

01:32:01.724 --> 01:32:04.310
but like, just in general,
because it requires so much advanced

01:32:04.393 --> 01:32:06.646
planning to build these models.

01:32:07.021 --> 01:32:09.857
Like, if you look at the R&D expense
at any given time,

01:32:10.233 --> 01:32:13.778
that is like over— an overlapping R&D
expense of multiple model

01:32:13.861 --> 01:32:15.279
generations at once.
Yes.

01:32:15.655 --> 01:32:19.200
And, you know, these,
they require a long time to work on.

01:32:19.367 --> 01:32:22.245
And then ultimately,
you know, you see like immense payoff

01:32:22.328 --> 01:32:23.579
when, when you ultimately deliver them.

01:32:23.663 --> 01:32:27.291
And this is kind of what we saw
with the Muse Spark family of models.

01:32:27.375 --> 01:32:30.711
Like, you know, again, we started
working on these a long time ago.

01:32:31.045 --> 01:32:34.382
And are seeing like incredible consumer
adoption through the Muse product.

01:32:34.632 --> 01:32:38.052
The other thing is that we use the models
to accelerate our core business, right?

01:32:38.135 --> 01:32:41.138
So I mean, you have this core business
that's hundreds of billions of dollars

01:32:41.222 --> 01:32:42.890
and still growing extremely quickly.

01:32:42.974 --> 01:32:46.894
And now, like, if you can grow it a few
percent faster by using these models

01:32:46.978 --> 01:32:50.982
to accelerate these things, then
that ends up funding a huge amount

01:32:51.065 --> 01:32:52.858
of this too as you're scaling it out.

01:32:53.192 --> 01:32:56.279
The Meta core business is a,
is a like 8th wonder of the world.

01:32:56.529 --> 01:32:58.072
It's extremely good business.
It really is.

01:32:58.155 --> 01:33:03.995
is quite incredible and is growing at
sort of astonishing rates.

01:33:05.079 --> 01:33:09.208
Yeah, you guys really
cooked with that one.

01:33:10.042 --> 01:33:13.170
Yeah, I think you're also getting
at something, Alex, which is like there's

01:33:13.254 --> 01:33:16.882
an interesting accounting artifact where
if you go build a data center,

01:33:17.341 --> 01:33:20.886
you have CapEx and you get to depreciate
that over a useful life of many years.

01:33:20.970 --> 01:33:24.181
And people debate what that useful life
is, but the point is you do get

01:33:24.265 --> 01:33:28.728
to depreciate it, whereas
a lot of the AI costs are also just

01:33:28.811 --> 01:33:31.772
in researcher salaries,
and those salaries are being spent on,

01:33:32.106 --> 01:33:35.776
you know, model training
that also has a useful life of many years,

01:33:36.110 --> 01:33:37.862
but you do not get
to capitalize generally.

01:33:38.070 --> 01:33:41.574
And that's also a financial advantage
for us building out the data centers as

01:33:41.657 --> 01:33:44.994
opposed to a bunch of the other companies
that are paying clouds,

01:33:45.202 --> 01:33:47.872
whereas they're paying the annual rate,
but then at some point

01:33:49.248 --> 01:33:52.793
the GPUs will be fully depreciated,
but we're probably still going to be using

01:33:52.877 --> 01:33:56.797
them for a while because they I mean,
the value of even like an H100 at this

01:33:56.881 --> 01:33:59.133
point is higher than it
was last year, right?

01:33:59.216 --> 01:34:01.677
Just because if compute
continues being constrained.

01:34:02.511 --> 01:34:06.140
So yeah, I think it's— yeah, I think—

01:34:06.974 --> 01:34:08.726
Any advice for us with OpenRadar?

01:34:08.809 --> 01:34:10.227
We have agreed to acquire them.

01:34:10.311 --> 01:34:11.228
It has not closed yet.

01:34:11.228 --> 01:34:17.443
Yeah, I think part of the exciting—
part of what we're starting to see

01:34:17.526 --> 01:34:20.571
in terms of the models,
especially the frontier models that are

01:34:20.655 --> 01:34:24.033
being developed, like Muse Wonder versus,
let's say, a Fable or an Astra,

01:34:24.450 --> 01:34:30.539
is that we actually are starting to focus
in quite different directions

01:34:30.623 --> 01:34:31.415
from the other models.

01:34:31.499 --> 01:34:33.793
And I think there's a lot
of intentionality that goes into the model

01:34:33.876 --> 01:34:36.587
development process that we've discussed
as part of this, you know,

01:34:36.671 --> 01:34:39.924
in this conversation,
that is genuinely different

01:34:40.007 --> 01:34:41.467
from how other models are focusing.

01:34:41.592 --> 01:34:43.803
And so,
you know, when these models kind of get

01:34:43.886 --> 01:34:48.349
collapsed to the same set of benchmarks,
that elides all of the—

01:34:48.432 --> 01:34:49.058
This is a good point.

01:34:49.141 --> 01:34:53.187
The interesting, um, sort of like, uh,
texture that differs

01:34:53.270 --> 01:34:54.271
between the various models.

01:34:54.355 --> 01:34:58.359
And so I think one thing that is
an exciting opportunity for OpenRadar,

01:34:58.442 --> 01:35:02.863
generally speaking, is like, you know,
um, helping to Helping developers

01:35:02.988 --> 01:35:06.409
understand what these sort of subtle
differences are so that they can make

01:35:06.659 --> 01:35:09.870
the most informed decisions about
what models ultimately they select.

01:35:10.287 --> 01:35:12.832
And then also you can imagine the flip
side of that, which is,

01:35:14.500 --> 01:35:16.752
you have all these developers
using these models.

01:35:17.044 --> 01:35:20.548
And so those developers are great people
to ask about what are the interesting

01:35:20.673 --> 01:35:25.928
textual differences between these models
that are more than just tweet length.

01:35:26.011 --> 01:35:30.933
Like, this model's good, this model's bad,
more than just that level of insight.

01:35:31.016 --> 01:35:35.312
And I think this is like
getting to the wine sommelier

01:35:35.980 --> 01:35:39.275
version or form of understanding
the models and their sort of relative

01:35:39.358 --> 01:35:41.819
strengths and weaknesses,
I think would be quite exciting.

01:35:42.069 --> 01:35:45.114
Okay, so based on your experience,
the OpenRadar view that there will be

01:35:45.197 --> 01:35:48.951
a diversity of models in the future and
they're optimized for different tasks and—

01:35:49.118 --> 01:35:50.828
I agree with that.
Exactly.

01:35:50.911 --> 01:35:52.621
That's just your
guys' experience based on—

01:35:53.205 --> 01:35:56.500
Yeah, we are focusing On different things.

01:35:56.584 --> 01:36:01.213
We care a lot more about personal
and professional agents, my observation,

01:36:01.338 --> 01:36:04.925
than the other major labs, which is good.

01:36:05.176 --> 01:36:06.927
There should be
differentiation in the economy.

01:36:07.011 --> 01:36:09.013
This is the point
of capitalism to some extent.

01:36:09.263 --> 01:36:10.723
Yeah.
So you're going to say something?

01:36:10.806 --> 01:36:12.391
No, I just really agree with that point.

01:36:12.475 --> 01:36:16.520
I mean, I think that part of what I love
about you guys working on OpenRouter

01:36:16.604 --> 01:36:20.566
and being able to scale it is
just the more infrastructure that can get

01:36:20.649 --> 01:36:23.527
developed,
that supports a diversity of players

01:36:23.611 --> 01:36:25.279
in the ecosystem, I think is good overall.

01:36:26.238 --> 01:36:30.701
I also, for the same reason, am
quite a fan of some of the recent NVIDIA

01:36:30.785 --> 01:36:33.579
moves to support the open
source ecosystem.

01:36:34.038 --> 01:36:38.125
I don't know, but this just kind of goes
with our whole theory and philosophy

01:36:38.209 --> 01:36:39.960
about, like, what does
a good future look like?

01:36:40.044 --> 01:36:40.127
Mm-hmm.

01:36:40.211 --> 01:36:43.255
We think that a good future
is one where you achieve a,

01:36:44.507 --> 01:36:47.426
like, you basically get
prosperity by empowering people.

01:36:47.635 --> 01:36:49.678
That's kind of the personal
superintelligence thing that we're doing,

01:36:49.762 --> 01:36:51.764
but there's the whole business version.
Yeah.

01:36:52.473 --> 01:36:55.267
But for a lot of the important safety
topics,

01:36:55.851 --> 01:36:59.688
like, our view is that the way to get
there is having a diversity

01:37:00.147 --> 01:37:03.567
and having checks and balances by kind
of having a lot of different things out

01:37:03.651 --> 01:37:06.695
there and accessible to people,
not like just having a couple of good

01:37:06.779 --> 01:37:08.864
models that are only
in a couple companies' hands.

01:37:08.948 --> 01:37:12.535
I think that that would be—
from my perspective, I think that is

01:37:13.369 --> 01:37:19.291
probably a riskier configuration
for the future than lots of people

01:37:19.375 --> 01:37:20.876
having access to different things.

01:37:20.960 --> 01:37:22.419
Do you guys have a theory
as to why that is?

01:37:22.503 --> 01:37:28.175
Where I feel like maybe people predicted
more of a winner-takes-all dynamic in AI,

01:37:28.425 --> 01:37:32.263
or one person clearly— like,
a story that people told back in 2023 is

01:37:32.346 --> 01:37:36.267
that one AI lab will
start to develop an advantage,

01:37:36.600 --> 01:37:39.144
and that will allow them to get recursive
self-improvement going,

01:37:39.436 --> 01:37:42.690
And then they'll just pull further
and further away from everyone else.

01:37:43.065 --> 01:37:47.319
Whereas it has
remained extremely competitive where

01:37:47.611 --> 01:37:49.446
there's kind of multiple
people at the frontier.

01:37:49.530 --> 01:37:53.868
You guys with Wonder today are coming
along with your frontier-class model.

01:37:54.451 --> 01:37:58.747
Open source models have done
an impressive job of keeping up, all told.

01:37:59.331 --> 01:38:03.002
And so we had this story a few years ago
that someone was just going

01:38:03.085 --> 01:38:04.461
to run away with the prize.

01:38:04.753 --> 01:38:05.921
Why hasn't that happened?

01:38:06.922 --> 01:38:11.927
I think there's a lot of underlying
reasons and mechanics that drive this.

01:38:12.428 --> 01:38:14.305
One certainly is just execution.

01:38:14.597 --> 01:38:20.019
I think for any given frontier lab,
there are periods of strong

01:38:20.102 --> 01:38:21.312
execution and weak execution.

01:38:21.395 --> 01:38:24.023
And I think
that is true across all the labs.

01:38:24.106 --> 01:38:26.817
And so that sort of starts
to average itself out over time.

01:38:28.110 --> 01:38:29.153
I think another—Very diplomatic.

01:38:31.196 --> 01:38:32.072
Sorry.
Yeah.

01:38:32.323 --> 01:38:34.867
Is it that some of the Players
in the lead screwed up?

01:38:35.659 --> 01:38:37.620
Well,
I think if all players,

01:38:37.703 --> 01:38:39.872
when they were in the lead,
executed perfectly, they would

01:38:39.955 --> 01:38:41.874
probably be able to stay in the lead.

01:38:42.374 --> 01:38:43.709
So I think that's one factor.

01:38:43.792 --> 01:38:46.712
I think
the power and strength of distillation is

01:38:46.795 --> 01:38:48.964
certainly another factor that's certainly
benefiting the open source

01:38:49.048 --> 01:38:51.258
ecosystem quite a bit.
And I think this is just like a—

01:38:51.592 --> 01:38:55.763
There's an anti-hegemonic force in
the phenomenon of distilling a model.

01:38:56.013 --> 01:38:56.305
Yeah.

01:38:56.555 --> 01:38:59.350
And this is just a quality
of the technology.

01:39:00.643 --> 01:39:04.021
It's not like anyone planned
for distillation to work super duper well,

01:39:04.104 --> 01:39:10.819
but it just turns out that you can
get a lot of capabilities naturally

01:39:10.903 --> 01:39:12.279
through some form of distillation.

01:39:12.613 --> 01:39:15.199
And this is,
broadly speaking, distillation is quite

01:39:15.282 --> 01:39:20.079
a good force for the entire ecosystem
because it means that you can pack very

01:39:20.371 --> 01:39:22.915
strong intelligence capabilities
into smaller and smaller models,

01:39:22.998 --> 01:39:25.459
which enables us to serve
them at much wider scale.

01:39:25.876 --> 01:39:28.921
And that's certainly
one of the very exciting stories is just

01:39:29.004 --> 01:39:33.550
like really small models today are like,
you know, much more powerful than the

01:39:33.634 --> 01:39:35.302
biggest models a year ago or 2 years ago.

01:39:36.804 --> 01:39:41.725
And then I think the last is that there's
just sort of this— I think there's like,

01:39:42.226 --> 01:39:48.273
I actually think a lot of
people in the ecosystem want this future

01:39:48.357 --> 01:39:51.610
where it is not just this like,
you know, definitely.

01:39:51.902 --> 01:39:56.907
One final company in all of capitalism
kind of kind of end outcome.

01:39:56.991 --> 01:39:58.367
Like, I think this is one of the reasons—

01:39:58.450 --> 01:40:01.203
Not just individuals, but companies
putting dollars against things.

01:40:01.704 --> 01:40:02.037
Yeah.

01:40:02.287 --> 01:40:05.708
And I think, like, you know,
this is actually one of the reasons why

01:40:05.791 --> 01:40:10.129
a lot of people were excited to join Meta
and Meta Superintelligence Labs

01:40:10.212 --> 01:40:12.214
in the first place,
was that they were just sort of like,

01:40:12.297 --> 01:40:15.050
you know,
I want to work at a place that is focused

01:40:15.134 --> 01:40:16.969
on giving this technology
to billions of people.

01:40:17.052 --> 01:40:19.471
That is, like,
not the stated goal necessarily

01:40:19.680 --> 01:40:20.806
of all the other frontier labs.

01:40:20.889 --> 01:40:23.434
And so, I think there's, like,
a lot of sort of just, like,

01:40:23.517 --> 01:40:26.895
natural forces where
many people in the industry are sort of,

01:40:26.979 --> 01:40:31.316
like, grappling with, okay,
what is the actual world state

01:40:31.400 --> 01:40:32.943
that I want to live in in the future?

01:40:33.027 --> 01:40:37.239
And there's lots of sort of, like,
decisions that people make at all levels

01:40:37.322 --> 01:40:40.701
in terms of where they work or who they do
business with or what business deals they

01:40:40.784 --> 01:40:45.247
do that sort of, like,
drive towards a more equalized outcome.

01:40:46.040 --> 01:40:51.795
You get into the Some of the MSL startup,
which I also wanted to ask about, which is

01:40:52.838 --> 01:40:55.883
it just seems fundamentally very hard
still to—

01:40:57.051 --> 01:41:01.138
again, we talked about what goes
into developing a frontier model, but also

01:41:01.638 --> 01:41:04.892
from a standing start, like when
was the standing start for MSL?

01:41:05.934 --> 01:41:08.771
Well, we launched Llama 4

01:41:09.271 --> 01:41:12.983
on April 4th, I think it was, 2025.

01:41:13.650 --> 01:41:14.318
Right.

01:41:14.777 --> 01:41:19.239
And then I think, what was it, April 20th

01:41:20.407 --> 01:41:22.493
or 21st was, I think, when I was like,

01:41:22.576 --> 01:41:29.416
hey, when I started calling
people for advice on what to do.

01:41:29.792 --> 01:41:34.379
So, I remember I was in a staff meeting
and I got, like, the WhatsApp

01:41:34.463 --> 01:41:35.923
notification, like, have time for a call?

01:41:36.006 --> 01:41:38.425
I was like, huh, interesting.

01:41:39.009 --> 01:41:41.261
Okay, so MSL is a little over 2 years old.

01:41:41.762 --> 01:41:43.263
No, no, no, 1 year.
1 year.

01:41:43.347 --> 01:41:45.390
Is it '25?
Oh, it's '26.

01:41:45.641 --> 01:41:47.059
Oh, sorry, I'm living in the future.

01:41:47.142 --> 01:41:48.310
Yeah, you are in the future.

01:41:48.393 --> 01:41:49.728
MSL is a little over 1 year old.

01:41:50.562 --> 01:41:55.943
Um, yeah, what are your reflections
a little over a year in on what was

01:41:56.735 --> 01:42:00.114
load-bearing, as the IAs would say,
in how it came together?

01:42:00.489 --> 01:42:04.827
Well, I mean, I think that there was this
whole thing early on which is just like,

01:42:05.410 --> 01:42:07.371
first we had to pull
together this core group.

01:42:07.454 --> 01:42:07.579
Right?

01:42:07.663 --> 01:42:14.461
So, um, so Nat and Alex and I,
we were like talking a bit up front, um,

01:42:15.295 --> 01:42:19.299
and, you know, at first we were,
you know, brainstorming.

01:42:19.716 --> 01:42:23.554
Yeah, like I was talking to Nat about,
could we— is there a way that we can like

01:42:23.804 --> 01:42:27.057
structure this so that way it's like a win
for Alex's shareholders and like

01:42:27.141 --> 01:42:28.559
we can like get him over here?

01:42:29.059 --> 01:42:32.729
And then, so then we were talking about,
uh, like what would the plan be?

01:42:33.230 --> 01:42:37.776
And, and then basically I was just,
I was in this mode where I was just like

01:42:37.860 --> 01:42:40.112
asking a lot of different
people what we should do.

01:42:40.195 --> 01:42:43.782
And like every time that,
that we would talk,

01:42:44.825 --> 01:42:48.537
I'd come away with like 3 more things of
like, oh yeah, this is what we should do.

01:42:48.704 --> 01:42:52.040
It's like very like high signal,
very like a lot of strategic clarity.

01:42:52.666 --> 01:42:56.795
And so we were brainstorming
and working on this for a bit.

01:42:57.212 --> 01:42:59.965
And then Nat and Daniel joined and we
pulled together a few

01:43:00.048 --> 01:43:01.925
of the the initial researchers.

01:43:02.050 --> 01:43:05.512
But like, in the beginning,
it was sort of like the Supernova thing

01:43:05.637 --> 01:43:08.432
where we just kind of like jammed
a bunch of people together.

01:43:08.515 --> 01:43:10.976
And I don't know,
it was like, I don't think either of us

01:43:11.059 --> 01:43:13.020
slept that much for the
first couple of months.

01:43:13.103 --> 01:43:13.187
No.

01:43:13.645 --> 01:43:16.690
I like, don't even,
I kind of have this mental block almost

01:43:16.899 --> 01:43:19.568
where I like, I kind of almost

01:43:20.944 --> 01:43:23.947
cleared my calendar for like a month or 2

01:43:24.031 --> 01:43:28.285
and was just meeting
researchers and talking to them.

01:43:28.452 --> 01:43:32.915
And I remember even before we announced
the deal where you were going to come

01:43:32.998 --> 01:43:35.876
over, we were basically like,
you were recruiting people and people

01:43:35.959 --> 01:43:37.252
were like, what is going on?

01:43:37.336 --> 01:43:38.670
Like, it's like, what is this?

01:43:39.046 --> 01:43:42.925
But, um, but then there was the whole
question, which was a really

01:43:43.008 --> 01:43:45.594
hands-on data labeler.

01:43:46.803 --> 01:43:48.931
But,
but that was, that was this whole question

01:43:49.014 --> 01:43:50.557
too, where it's like, all right, well,
like,

01:43:50.641 --> 01:43:52.809
okay, so if we're not happy
with the trajectory of the current

01:43:52.893 --> 01:43:58.273
program, Like, who were the right set of
people to lead the program going forward?

01:43:58.732 --> 01:44:02.236
And Alex and I had spent a bunch of time
talking over the years.

01:44:02.319 --> 01:44:05.739
I mean, maybe like, whatever, once
every few months or something we'd talk.

01:44:05.822 --> 01:44:09.785
And I was always just very impressed by
how he thought about things strategically.

01:44:10.077 --> 01:44:14.873
And I kind of had this view of like,
all right, yeah, currently he's doing

01:44:15.624 --> 01:44:18.001
this very operational data business.

01:44:19.962 --> 01:44:26.260
But it always felt to me like he was
the sharpest entrepreneur of his

01:44:26.343 --> 01:44:29.930
generation, of the ones who I
had had a chance to meet.

01:44:32.307 --> 01:44:34.935
And like, it always just struck me,
it's like, all right, like,

01:44:35.018 --> 01:44:37.980
this is a guy who, yeah,
he's like running this like kind of very

01:44:38.063 --> 01:44:40.607
grindy operational business, but like—

01:44:40.732 --> 01:44:42.526
Lovely business still, by the way.
What's up?

01:44:42.609 --> 01:44:44.152
We're big investors.

01:44:44.361 --> 01:44:48.782
It's not a— it's a— there's more
Commentary on the difficulty

01:44:48.865 --> 01:44:49.741
of running the business.

01:44:49.825 --> 01:44:52.911
When you run the 8th wonder of the world,
Alex, a lot of businesses look

01:44:52.995 --> 01:44:54.621
like a grindy operational business.

01:44:56.331 --> 01:44:58.166
It's just like my view of things.

01:44:58.292 --> 01:45:01.253
It's like grindy, grindy.

01:45:01.670 --> 01:45:02.921
No, no, no.

01:45:04.840 --> 01:45:08.468
But no, so it's,
so I just kind of had this view.

01:45:08.552 --> 01:45:11.930
I'm like, all right, like,
even though this is sort of

01:45:13.223 --> 01:45:15.976
I think maybe when we got started,
a lot of people were like,

01:45:16.560 --> 01:45:18.061
okay, like, how is this going to work?

01:45:18.145 --> 01:45:22.441
It's like the kind of like the
social media guy is working

01:45:22.524 --> 01:45:23.942
with the like data guy.

01:45:24.192 --> 01:45:27.154
Like, is that like, are we going
to build a frontier lab here or what?

01:45:27.237 --> 01:45:30.157
But, but I mean, but my,
but I mean, my like really strong

01:45:30.240 --> 01:45:33.452
conviction, because I talked to like all
the leaders of all the labs, like all the

01:45:33.535 --> 01:45:35.370
heads of research, like all this stuff.

01:45:35.454 --> 01:45:38.498
And like, I was like, no, Alex is the guy.
Like, he's the fucking guy.

01:45:38.623 --> 01:45:40.417
And like, and we're going to,
and basically,

01:45:40.917 --> 01:45:46.256
like, we're going to basically be able to,
to, to go pull together a team and recruit

01:45:46.715 --> 01:45:48.467
the people that we need to go do this.

01:45:48.633 --> 01:45:54.389
And like, we need this kind of very,
this kind of like strategic clarity

01:45:54.473 --> 01:45:58.018
that he has around the different
ways that we want to approach this,

01:45:58.143 --> 01:46:01.063
the kind of extreme focus on talent
density,

01:46:01.772 --> 01:46:06.360
on kind of like the clarity
of communicating the different vision,

01:46:06.443 --> 01:46:08.779
which we kind of fashioned
together over this period.

01:46:10.614 --> 01:46:13.450
And a bunch of things that I think were

01:46:14.242 --> 01:46:17.954
kind of extreme but have basically worked,

01:46:18.205 --> 01:46:23.293
like having this,
this like very flat culture was something

01:46:23.377 --> 01:46:25.879
that you were kind of really
obsessed with, right?

01:46:25.962 --> 01:46:28.757
It's, I mean, you have like,
was it almost 200 direct reports?

01:46:28.840 --> 01:46:30.926
That's like, we have, it's not,
it doesn't, it's not quite

01:46:31.009 --> 01:46:32.094
that extreme in practice.

01:46:32.219 --> 01:46:35.138
I think that there's like, like team
leads and all, and all this stuff.

01:46:35.222 --> 01:46:36.431
My muse helps me manage that.

01:46:36.515 --> 01:46:41.019
Yeah, well, that's— But like,
but there were these series of things

01:46:41.103 --> 01:46:45.982
that I think quite early we,
you kind of articulated, and I was like,

01:46:46.775 --> 01:46:53.281
okay, this is like kind of extreme,
but it seems like directionally right,

01:46:53.740 --> 01:46:55.075
and like we should go do this.

01:46:55.158 --> 01:46:57.452
And, you know, I went and like talked
to other people who were more like

01:46:57.536 --> 01:47:01.665
the people you'd conventionally think of,
and it's like, it was just very much like,

01:47:01.748 --> 01:47:04.418
I don't know what you'd expect
expect a big company to operate.

01:47:04.501 --> 01:47:06.628
And I was like, no,
we want to do something that's—

01:47:07.087 --> 01:47:11.591
As you describe this, I'll note
4 things that I think even other

01:47:12.300 --> 01:47:15.303
opinionated, effective founder
CEOs would not have done.

01:47:16.138 --> 01:47:20.642
One,
you just called the recent prior effort by

01:47:21.768 --> 01:47:26.189
a bunch of people at the company
a failure and not good enough.

01:47:26.273 --> 01:47:29.025
I mean, you were more diplomatic than
that, but the act of standing up off

01:47:29.109 --> 01:47:32.529
the trajectory— Yeah,
but the act of standing up So, you know,

01:47:32.612 --> 01:47:34.072
people can read between the lines.

01:47:34.322 --> 01:47:36.575
So sometimes, yeah, sometimes
you have to reboot a team.

01:47:36.658 --> 01:47:37.492
That's one.

01:47:37.826 --> 01:47:43.540
Thing 2 is you are willing to pay ungodly
amounts of money for effectively talent.

01:47:43.832 --> 01:47:47.335
Like, you're paying a lot of money
to bring Alex and some other people, but,

01:47:47.419 --> 01:47:50.464
you know, a lot of money
for Alex in particular over.

01:47:52.716 --> 01:47:57.387
Thing 3—
And 49% of this very good grindy business.

01:47:57.471 --> 01:47:59.139
Yeah, exactly.

01:47:59.222 --> 01:48:01.016
But let's keep the story straight.

01:48:01.516 --> 01:48:04.186
So there's that.

01:48:04.686 --> 01:48:08.982
Thing 3 is, I'll note, Alex had never
trained a frontier model before.

01:48:09.357 --> 01:48:13.528
And so you were taking some amount
of a bet on Alex's potential to do

01:48:13.612 --> 01:48:17.324
something where he had done stuff in AI,
but no offense,

01:48:17.407 --> 01:48:20.660
he just had not done the specific
thing that you were being hired to do.

01:48:20.744 --> 01:48:23.538
And then 4th,
you were saying you cleared your calendar

01:48:23.622 --> 01:48:25.081
and just only did this for 2 months.

01:48:25.415 --> 01:48:28.835
I think a lot of CEOs would've had
the business review and would have had

01:48:28.919 --> 01:48:32.714
the board thing and they would have had
the customer stuff and things like that.

01:48:33.131 --> 01:48:37.385
Those 4 seem to me pretty
interesting where I think a lot

01:48:37.469 --> 01:48:40.013
of CEOs would not have done those.
Yeah, that's interesting.

01:48:40.096 --> 01:48:42.641
I definitely think— I mean,
one thing you may not appreciate this

01:48:42.724 --> 01:48:46.394
about yourself, but I think one thing
that is quite special about Marc is

01:48:47.729 --> 01:48:51.358
the degree to which, like,
once he concludes that something is

01:48:51.441 --> 01:48:56.154
the right thing or something makes sense,
he just like fully shifts everything

01:48:56.238 --> 01:48:57.864
to be consistent with that belief set.

01:48:58.615 --> 01:49:04.538
And it is quite like, to your point,
I think like the inertia of most, even

01:49:04.996 --> 01:49:08.250
very successful founders who like are
opinionated and edgy or whatnot,

01:49:08.500 --> 01:49:11.753
would just be to like,
oh, I still have like my whole calendar.

01:49:11.920 --> 01:49:15.924
And like, so I'll just like talk about
doing this thing and hope that somebody

01:49:16.049 --> 01:49:17.842
does it kind of situation.

01:49:18.218 --> 01:49:21.096
Whereas I think,
No, I think when Marc concludes

01:49:21.179 --> 01:49:23.890
that something
is the thing he has to do or makes a lot

01:49:23.974 --> 01:49:29.145
of sense, then he really does, like,
he has, like, maybe very high

01:49:29.229 --> 01:49:30.230
learning rate in that sense.

01:49:30.313 --> 01:49:33.942
Like, he's very willing to, like, pivot
entire things to be consistent with it.

01:49:34.609 --> 01:49:39.948
And I think that, like,
you know, one very underappreciated thing

01:49:40.031 --> 01:49:43.451
is the degree to which so much
of the company had to pivot

01:49:43.535 --> 01:49:44.953
to support MSL as well.

01:49:45.453 --> 01:49:50.208
Like, I think that
while it was a kind of like this more

01:49:50.292 --> 01:49:55.714
startup-y effort within a larger company,
you know, the needs of MSL

01:49:55.797 --> 01:49:56.506
were still quite large.

01:49:56.590 --> 01:49:58.967
Like, we needed a huge amount
of help on infrastructure.

01:49:59.050 --> 01:50:01.136
We needed these large-scale
data centers to be built.

01:50:01.428 --> 01:50:05.056
We needed like lots of support
from many other parts of the company.

01:50:06.141 --> 01:50:09.853
And especially as Muse has been
successful, we've also needed lots of—

01:50:09.978 --> 01:50:10.604
Yeah.

01:50:10.770 --> 01:50:12.355
Deep collaboration with
the rest of the company.

01:50:12.439 --> 01:50:18.528
And this is one thing that I think
kind of only is possible because Mark is

01:50:18.945 --> 01:50:23.241
the founder CEO,
and he can ensure that we have kind

01:50:23.325 --> 01:50:29.539
of like a global optimization towards this
sort of like AI maximalist version of Meta

01:50:30.123 --> 01:50:33.043
versus the natural inertia
of an organization towards

01:50:34.044 --> 01:50:37.130
just local optimization ad infinitum.

01:50:37.797 --> 01:50:41.926
But it's also a lot of how we work
together is it's important to me to be

01:50:42.385 --> 01:50:46.806
in the details of this work so that we can
get everything around the company

01:50:46.890 --> 01:50:48.850
to go do what we need to do.
Right.

01:50:48.975 --> 01:50:51.478
And so it's like, I mean, yeah,
I'll have opinions about this stuff, but

01:50:51.561 --> 01:50:53.938
I'm not like, I'm not the lead researcher.
Right.

01:50:54.064 --> 01:50:58.818
It's like, it's, you know, it's,
but I think that's kind of a lot of,

01:50:58.943 --> 01:51:02.906
I think how we work together well is that,
yeah, it's, I mean, we're,

01:51:02.989 --> 01:51:05.659
we're kind of like,
quite in the details of all of this stuff,

01:51:05.784 --> 01:51:08.662
but running the lab is a big thing.

01:51:08.995 --> 01:51:11.665
Running the rest of the company in order
to support this is like

01:51:11.748 --> 01:51:13.249
the effort is a big thing.

01:51:13.667 --> 01:51:16.795
You almost like, you can't really get this
done, I think, without

01:51:16.878 --> 01:51:18.004
both of those pieces.

01:51:18.713 --> 01:51:22.425
And one thing that I think was
quite important about when we set

01:51:22.509 --> 01:51:27.263
the whole thing up is Meta,
the company, invested a huge amount

01:51:27.347 --> 01:51:31.142
to stand up MSL,
and then we very clearly told everyone

01:51:31.226 --> 01:51:33.144
like, hey, there's no deadlines.

01:51:33.395 --> 01:51:37.524
We are going to take things at the right
pace, which I think on the one hand could

01:51:37.607 --> 01:51:39.776
be like, you know, absolutely terrifying.

01:51:39.859 --> 01:51:42.946
It's like you've just like stood up this
extremely expensive unit and you're

01:51:43.029 --> 01:51:44.114
telling everyone like, hey—

01:51:44.197 --> 01:51:46.408
Yeah,
and you're worried inside a larger company

01:51:46.491 --> 01:51:49.369
that like, okay, 7 years from now
maybe something will ship, right?

01:51:49.452 --> 01:51:50.078
Yeah, totally.

01:51:50.412 --> 01:51:54.916
And so, but I think that like,
first, I think one of the cool things

01:51:54.999 --> 01:51:56.960
about, you know,
focusing on talent density and hiring all

01:51:57.043 --> 01:52:01.089
these very brilliant people who have done
so much already, that's very impressive,

01:52:01.172 --> 01:52:05.176
is, you know, everybody had like a lot of
motivation to ship

01:52:05.635 --> 01:52:09.681
and everybody wanted to sort of like leave
their mark, so to speak, on the field.

01:52:09.764 --> 01:52:13.518
And so everybody was like heavily,
highly self-motivated

01:52:14.018 --> 01:52:15.353
to move as quickly as possible.

01:52:15.854 --> 01:52:20.233
And one of the things that's been funny
and I've had to continue reminding

01:52:20.316 --> 01:52:25.905
the team of is like, you know, it is
a team of a lot of insecure overachievers.

01:52:26.197 --> 01:52:29.617
So like at any one point,
if I ask them like, hey,

01:52:29.701 --> 01:52:32.829
how good do you think— where do
you think we'll be in 3 months?

01:52:33.163 --> 01:52:34.289
I always get the most like—

01:52:34.372 --> 01:52:36.124
Everyone always says it's awful.
It's awful.

01:52:36.207 --> 01:52:36.791
We're screwed.
Yeah.

01:52:36.875 --> 01:52:38.501
These like very pessimistic answers.

01:52:38.585 --> 01:52:42.213
And then I record it and I like 3 months
later I'm like, hey,

01:52:42.756 --> 01:52:43.757
you know, we just shipped this model.

01:52:43.840 --> 01:52:47.677
Let me just show you what all of you said,
where all of you said we

01:52:47.761 --> 01:52:49.679
would be in 3 3 months ago.

01:52:50.180 --> 01:52:51.556
And, uh, and this is—

01:52:51.765 --> 01:52:53.892
And has that caused them to get
any more optimistic or no?

01:52:53.975 --> 01:52:56.060
No, they're still—
they're very self-critical.

01:52:56.311 --> 01:52:59.063
I think it's, it's not,
it's not that they're pessimistic about—

01:53:00.106 --> 01:53:04.277
I think that the average person seems
quite optimistic about MSL and very

01:53:04.360 --> 01:53:06.780
self-critical about their own project.
Yeah.

01:53:06.863 --> 01:53:09.991
Which I think is actually quite a healthy,
a healthy dynamic.

01:53:10.784 --> 01:53:12.952
Um, but, but the trend continues.

01:53:13.036 --> 01:53:16.706
Like, I think, I think, and, and this is,
I mean, in many ways Muse Wonder is like

01:53:16.790 --> 01:53:21.169
this incredible
milestone for everyone because it is a—

01:53:21.294 --> 01:53:23.797
The team was very, very critical.
Yeah.

01:53:23.880 --> 01:53:25.924
That was working on it along the way.

01:53:26.132 --> 01:53:28.843
When did you guys know
that Muse Wanderer was good?

01:53:31.596 --> 01:53:33.223
Um, that's a good question.

01:53:33.389 --> 01:53:37.143
I think that we, uh, so there's
all these intermediate drawings.

01:53:37.227 --> 01:53:39.145
You're peeking in the oven and it's like,
I don't know, it seems

01:53:39.229 --> 01:53:40.146
kind of gooey still.

01:53:40.271 --> 01:53:42.982
And yeah, yeah, going back to it, I mean,
there are all these intermediate

01:53:43.066 --> 01:53:46.444
intermediate signals that you can get
along the way that things are good or bad.

01:53:46.736 --> 01:53:48.863
And what are the intermediate signals?

01:53:49.531 --> 01:53:53.243
So in pre-training, you have a lot
of perplexity-based evaluation.

01:53:53.368 --> 01:53:57.664
So you have a different regime of evals
that can help you in pre-training.

01:53:57.747 --> 01:54:01.835
And then you have the more traditional
benchmarks that can help you towards RL.

01:54:03.253 --> 01:54:06.005
But no,
I mean, this model,

01:54:06.089 --> 01:54:08.842
kind of as you mentioned,
has been cooking for quite a while.

01:54:08.925 --> 01:54:12.637
And You know,
a lot of— I think one of the funniest

01:54:12.720 --> 01:54:18.726
things about AI research is that,
you know, there's a lot of times where

01:54:18.852 --> 01:54:22.397
people just sort of like are kind
of squinting at a bunch of dots and being

01:54:22.480 --> 01:54:27.360
like, yeah, looks like it's good,
or, uh, looks a little bit off.

01:54:27.443 --> 01:54:31.865
Like, one of the funniest things is the
people have protractors,

01:54:33.491 --> 01:54:38.830
like analog protractors that they put
against you know, a set of lines on their

01:54:38.913 --> 01:54:40.874
monitor to like measure an angle.

01:54:41.207 --> 01:54:45.253
And then they,
um, so he's not come for this job yet.

01:54:45.336 --> 01:54:47.505
So I think there's,
I think there's like a huge amount

01:54:47.589 --> 01:54:52.677
of like, uh, like it's very, um, there's
more vibes, um, driving like, okay.

01:54:52.760 --> 01:54:56.014
But when did we have this conversation
where, when did you know it was good?

01:54:56.556 --> 01:55:01.978
Um, well, we had this debate after it
was done pre-training where it was okay.

01:55:03.688 --> 01:55:09.819
How long will it take in RL
before it is better than Muse Spark?

01:55:10.653 --> 01:55:13.448
And I remember the folks
on the pre-training team

01:55:14.073 --> 01:55:16.200
were just trying to set expectations.

01:55:16.326 --> 01:55:18.828
They're like, look, sometimes
this takes a while.

01:55:19.370 --> 01:55:20.663
And then it was immediately.

01:55:21.247 --> 01:55:24.542
And so we were like, okay,
I think we're on a good path.

01:55:25.168 --> 01:55:26.961
That to me was sort of—Yeah.

01:55:27.503 --> 01:55:29.172
I think when when people got— yeah.

01:55:29.255 --> 01:55:30.423
We've been excited.

01:55:30.673 --> 01:55:32.675
I think we've been pretty excited
about it for a few months now.

01:55:32.800 --> 01:55:34.594
Yeah.
But there's a lot to do there.

01:55:35.470 --> 01:55:39.933
But going back to the beginning,
I think one of the things that was very

01:55:41.267 --> 01:55:44.228
interesting and challenging in standing
the thing up was like,

01:55:44.562 --> 01:55:47.941
you really just wanted this like
very high talent density effort.

01:55:49.025 --> 01:55:53.363
And we were just kind of pulling some
of the best people from Meta in and

01:55:53.655 --> 01:55:55.323
recruiting a lot of the best people.

01:55:55.406 --> 01:55:57.742
And I mean, you were asking, it's like,
all right, well, like, okay,

01:55:57.825 --> 01:56:00.995
for people who, like,
if you were asking before, it's like,

01:56:01.079 --> 01:56:04.582
well, Alex hadn't necessarily
trained models like this.

01:56:04.749 --> 01:56:08.294
But to me, one of the big things was like
all of the best people were

01:56:08.378 --> 01:56:09.712
very excited to work with him.

01:56:10.463 --> 01:56:12.298
And I think like,
it's, I mean, you,

01:56:12.382 --> 01:56:16.219
I'm sure see this in all of,
in a lot of the things that you do,

01:56:16.302 --> 01:56:19.806
which is like,
you know, it's like you, most people

01:56:19.889 --> 01:56:22.392
are not kind of one-dimensional, right?

01:56:22.976 --> 01:56:25.103
Yeah, maybe you do a specific thing,

01:56:27.313 --> 01:56:30.692
but your ability to grow and expand is

01:56:30.900 --> 01:56:36.948
kind of largely based on how effectively
can you just do people who are excellent

01:56:37.031 --> 01:56:40.535
in different domains want to come
work with you on different things.

01:56:40.743 --> 01:56:42.495
Yeah, and the great
people have followership.

01:56:42.578 --> 01:56:43.079
Yeah.

01:56:43.413 --> 01:56:46.040
And so I think that there was a little bit
of that,

01:56:47.834 --> 01:56:52.547
but also just It's even for the people
that don't know you yet,

01:56:53.131 --> 01:56:56.884
or like, or aren't, you know,
don't know what you're about.

01:56:56.968 --> 01:57:00.179
Like, are you
like an impressive person and a compelling

01:57:00.263 --> 01:57:02.098
person that people want to come work with?

01:57:02.890 --> 01:57:06.811
Do people feel comfortable that you're
going to create this environment?

01:57:06.978 --> 01:57:10.898
And I think you were like very
thoughtful about this early on.

01:57:10.982 --> 01:57:14.736
We were kind of—
Some of the stuff I think you kind of had

01:57:14.819 --> 01:57:17.780
fully formed as beliefs of how
you thought we should do this.

01:57:18.239 --> 01:57:21.659
But I also remember
when we were having the initial recruiting

01:57:21.743 --> 01:57:24.412
conversations,
we were sort of in real time

01:57:25.079 --> 01:57:28.458
shaping— it's like, okay, well,
this construct that we're talking about

01:57:28.541 --> 01:57:32.170
with this person isn't quite going to
work, but like, they seem good,

01:57:32.253 --> 01:57:33.129
and like, they have this thought.

01:57:33.212 --> 01:57:36.049
And it was just like, like,
really kind of coalescing

01:57:36.507 --> 01:57:38.634
this vision about what the shape
of it should be in real time.

01:57:38.718 --> 01:57:40.470
I mean, I don't know, I mean,
I'm sure you remember that.

01:57:40.553 --> 01:57:44.515
this, where it was like there were some of
the initial concepts of like,

01:57:45.099 --> 01:57:50.396
I mean, I think probably the most extreme
thing was I think you had the concept of

01:57:50.897 --> 01:57:54.150
the talent density
and all and that kind of piece.

01:57:54.692 --> 01:57:58.154
But I think we weren't sure upfront
that it was going to have

01:57:58.237 --> 01:58:00.114
to be a full reboot, right?

01:58:00.281 --> 01:58:04.786
It's like that basically
started when we were out talking to

01:58:05.411 --> 01:58:07.330
a lot of the kind of best people.

01:58:07.538 --> 01:58:11.250
And we realized that, hey,
like, in order to make this as attractive

01:58:11.334 --> 01:58:14.295
as possible, it should feel like, okay,
we're starting this

01:58:14.462 --> 01:58:15.713
thing again from scratch.

01:58:15.797 --> 01:58:18.758
And yeah, we'll pull in a lot of the great
people from the Llama effort

01:58:18.841 --> 01:58:20.343
and FAIR and all of that too.

01:58:21.010 --> 01:58:25.306
But that was a very intentional thing
that we basically figured out and that you

01:58:25.389 --> 01:58:28.893
figured out as we were out talking
to the initial set of people of like

01:58:28.976 --> 01:58:30.603
what was going to make this compelling.

01:58:30.812 --> 01:58:34.482
And it ended up being really smart
and like making a huge difference

01:58:34.565 --> 01:58:37.693
in the culture of the lab and how
it got and how it got started.

01:58:37.819 --> 01:58:40.488
But I don't know,
it's like being able to be that dynamic

01:58:40.613 --> 01:58:43.783
about figuring out, like,
what does the shape of this need to be?

01:58:44.283 --> 01:58:45.576
It's not like that.

01:58:45.785 --> 01:58:47.286
I think that's a very uncommon trait.

01:58:47.453 --> 01:58:49.122
It's a very entrepreneurial trait, right?

01:58:49.205 --> 01:58:52.625
So, when I was thinking about, like,
all right, like, this field is

01:58:52.708 --> 01:58:54.252
going to keep on evolving, right?

01:58:54.335 --> 01:58:56.879
So, what is the thing that's going to be
important for someone who's

01:58:56.963 --> 01:58:58.297
going to run a lab well?

01:58:58.464 --> 01:59:01.968
It's not like,
are they an expert in whatever the latest

01:59:02.051 --> 01:59:06.848
modality is of, like, or kind
of of training technique for a lab.

01:59:07.390 --> 01:59:09.600
It's, is the person a clear

01:59:11.144 --> 01:59:14.438
thinker and able to kind of dynamically

01:59:14.814 --> 01:59:18.776
update and move in, like, as
kind of an entrepreneur would?

01:59:18.860 --> 01:59:21.529
I think this is just like an inherently
very entrepreneurial space.

01:59:21.988 --> 01:59:26.534
Has this experience
changed your view on the value of

01:59:30.204 --> 01:59:34.083
going external,
where both your reboot,

01:59:34.458 --> 01:59:38.045
a bunch of the people you brought in were
external, like Alex and

01:59:38.504 --> 01:59:39.839
Nat and Daniel and folks.

01:59:40.339 --> 01:59:44.343
But then also you're describing
deciding whether you needed a reboot

01:59:44.969 --> 01:59:47.972
based on going to the outside

01:59:49.140 --> 01:59:52.018
intelligence of the aggregate views

01:59:52.518 --> 01:59:55.646
of the researchers in the industry
and getting a sense for the— But just Do

01:59:55.730 --> 02:00:01.319
you feel like you'll carry forward that
approach for future unrelated projects?

02:00:01.402 --> 02:00:04.071
Because obviously the risk of a large
company is that everyone's like,

02:00:04.155 --> 02:00:05.364
oh, we got a great approach.

02:00:05.448 --> 02:00:07.783
Yeah, let me show you our
100-page document on it.

02:00:08.326 --> 02:00:14.874
I mean, obviously you can't—
a lot of what we had to do here was

02:00:15.124 --> 02:00:16.667
rotate a lot of the rest of the company.

02:00:16.751 --> 02:00:19.462
And like for things like
the infrastructure team that now,

02:00:19.712 --> 02:00:22.006
you know, it's like building these
huge clusters to support this.

02:00:22.089 --> 02:00:23.716
I mean, that's obviously a huge thing.

02:00:25.134 --> 02:00:28.262
So huge parts of the company
kind of had to pivot to do this.

02:00:28.429 --> 02:00:32.558
And obviously you can't do too many
of those at the same time, right?

02:00:32.642 --> 02:00:34.769
Only one thing can be the top priority.
Yes.

02:00:35.186 --> 02:00:39.065
But I think on balance,
it has made me somewhat more optimistic.

02:00:39.148 --> 02:00:43.110
I mean, we brought in this guy Kunal Shah,
who, you know, it's like,

02:00:43.194 --> 02:00:45.780
I think he's like one of the top
entrepreneurs in India,

02:00:46.155 --> 02:00:48.991
building this great company CRED,
and And now he's running WhatsApp,

02:00:49.075 --> 02:00:51.494
which I always thought should do more
in commerce,

02:00:51.702 --> 02:00:54.789
and we were sort of underserved there,
and India is the biggest

02:00:54.872 --> 02:00:56.207
country that we're in.

02:00:57.667 --> 02:00:59.794
But, I mean,
that's— it's a bit of a different

02:00:59.877 --> 02:01:02.838
situation,
because we're not, like, pivoting

02:01:02.922 --> 02:01:04.507
the whole company towards WhatsApp.

02:01:04.590 --> 02:01:07.093
We just wanted to bring more
entrepreneurial energy there.

02:01:07.468 --> 02:01:09.679
This was, like, a level 10
out of 10 difficulty thing.

02:01:09.762 --> 02:01:12.139
I don't want to talk about it in the past
tense, as if it's done, because it's still

02:01:12.223 --> 02:01:14.058
a level 10 out of 10 difficulty thing.
Yeah, yeah, sure.

02:01:14.141 --> 02:01:16.894
But I think

02:01:18.104 --> 02:01:21.816
the number of pieces that kind of had

02:01:21.899 --> 02:01:27.571
to work, it was quite all-encompassing.

02:01:27.822 --> 02:01:30.700
And I think that it's both difficult
for the people doing it,

02:01:30.783 --> 02:01:33.703
but I do think that that also puts some
level of strain on

02:01:34.495 --> 02:01:37.039
the rest of the company,
on the financial model of the company.

02:01:37.123 --> 02:01:40.126
I think like you— so I wouldn't
want to conflate those 2 pieces.

02:01:40.543 --> 02:01:40.668
Right?

02:01:40.751 --> 02:01:43.587
It's like I think there's yeah,
it's good to have new blood, and it's good

02:01:43.671 --> 02:01:46.549
to have people who understand the context.

02:01:46.632 --> 02:01:50.636
And you know, you were talking about
people who've been around Meta for a while

02:01:50.720 --> 02:01:52.054
and understand the ups and downs.

02:01:52.138 --> 02:01:54.473
And it's like I don't know if you've
worked at Meta for 10 years,

02:01:54.557 --> 02:01:55.725
you've seen some shit, right?

02:01:55.975 --> 02:02:00.896
It's like it's like we've gone through
a lot of hard times, and and like I think

02:02:00.980 --> 02:02:02.898
that that's kind of important context too.

02:02:03.190 --> 02:02:07.069
But but no, I I think
a lot of this is just.

02:02:08.237 --> 02:02:11.907
trying to,
you know, you're— whenever you're building

02:02:11.991 --> 02:02:16.620
a team, you're— you inherently, you know,
it's like Alex had a theory for what

02:02:16.704 --> 02:02:20.666
the team shape needed to be,
and like, I guess I had a theory for what

02:02:20.750 --> 02:02:23.669
like the shape of the leaders
needed to be for it.

02:02:23.961 --> 02:02:29.175
And, um, and, you know, it's like
a year plus in, it like seems okay.

02:02:29.342 --> 02:02:32.219
Like, it's like, first of all, it was just
good to try something different, right?

02:02:32.303 --> 02:02:32.553
Mm-hmm.

02:02:32.636 --> 02:02:34.180
And then different than what
everyone else was doing.

02:02:34.263 --> 02:02:41.187
Yes.
And yeah, I mean, that's—

02:02:41.270 --> 02:02:45.775
I think there's some other dimensions
here that are worth mentioning too.

02:02:45.858 --> 02:02:49.153
Like, one of the— like,
when deciding we had to do the full

02:02:49.236 --> 02:02:54.200
reboot, like, you know, a lot of it was,
you know, one of the main inputs was

02:02:54.283 --> 02:02:56.744
external, but also one of the inputs—
I don't think I've ever—

02:02:56.827 --> 02:02:59.663
actually, I can't remember if I told you
this story, but one of the first meetings

02:02:59.747 --> 02:03:05.586
I had When I joined, I was in this
meeting with some of the leads

02:03:05.669 --> 02:03:06.712
of the existing effort.

02:03:07.213 --> 02:03:10.299
And I was like, okay,
I think the first thing we need to do is

02:03:10.383 --> 02:03:15.137
train a really good reasoning model
by some timeframe.

02:03:15.262 --> 02:03:18.766
I don't know when, but this is
clearly the most important milestone.

02:03:19.266 --> 02:03:23.145
And one of the key leads in the group

02:03:24.063 --> 02:03:26.732
who no longer works at Meta, but was like,

02:03:27.191 --> 02:03:29.652
oh, we don't need a reasoning model.

02:03:29.819 --> 02:03:32.571
Do any of our consumers
need a reasoning model?

02:03:33.322 --> 02:03:34.698
I feel like we don't need
to do a reasoning model.

02:03:34.782 --> 02:03:36.117
We could just do a normal model.

02:03:36.200 --> 02:03:39.370
And I had this heart-sinking moment.

02:03:39.703 --> 02:03:41.789
Oh, no, I've joined the wrong company.

02:03:42.373 --> 02:03:45.459
I was like, oh, I've got
to take 10 steps back.

02:03:46.043 --> 02:03:49.171
And I think this is
kind of a funny anecdote.

02:03:49.296 --> 02:03:50.840
I remember calling Nat after this meeting.

02:03:50.923 --> 02:03:55.177
I was like, Nat, like, this is
what they just said in the meeting.

02:03:55.344 --> 02:04:00.683
But I think it was specifically
an indicator of something that I think,

02:04:01.100 --> 02:04:04.395
like, Mark, when he talked to people,
like, certainly also started to realize,

02:04:04.478 --> 02:04:08.065
which is like,
we needed leadership in the company to be

02:04:08.149 --> 02:04:11.610
much more AI-pilled and much more
sort of like forward-leaning

02:04:11.694 --> 02:04:15.531
and front-leaning on like where the— how
quickly the technology's moving,

02:04:15.614 --> 02:04:17.908
where it's going to go to,
what the possibilities are.

02:04:18.159 --> 02:04:21.579
And effectively
dramatically more AI-pilled.

02:04:22.079 --> 02:04:27.960
And I think as
Marc started pivoting and shifting

02:04:28.043 --> 02:04:31.964
the whole company towards this direction,
and it was very clear that this is like,

02:04:33.007 --> 02:04:36.343
I think Marc got AI-pilled,
and then everyone saw that he was

02:04:36.427 --> 02:04:39.722
AI-pilled and everyone realized, like,
oh, this is where the company's going.

02:04:39.930 --> 02:04:45.227
Then I think you had a lot of the existing
leaders like very quickly

02:04:45.728 --> 02:04:46.770
pick it up and change.

02:04:46.896 --> 02:04:50.232
And like Adam Mosseri, for example,
who runs Instagram,

02:04:50.566 --> 02:04:56.322
has one of the most like
aesthetic but also like effective kind

02:04:56.405 --> 02:05:01.076
of like personal vibe code and personal
mini app setup of anyone that I've seen.

02:05:01.160 --> 02:05:04.872
Like, I think it's like he's like
really gone quite far in the direction.

02:05:05.331 --> 02:05:05.581
Mm-hmm.

02:05:05.664 --> 02:05:09.168
And I think a lot of the leaders
at Meta who have been at Meta a long

02:05:09.251 --> 02:05:11.670
time are, like, all very adaptive.

02:05:11.754 --> 02:05:14.715
Like, I think, you know, kind
of to the prior point, they've been in—

02:05:14.798 --> 02:05:15.007
Yeah.

02:05:15.090 --> 02:05:18.177
A highly turbulent, like,
period of technology.

02:05:18.385 --> 02:05:22.223
Now, AI is, like, maybe,
you know, 5 or 10 times more turbulent

02:05:22.306 --> 02:05:24.266
and faster moving than those past waves.

02:05:24.391 --> 02:05:28.103
But I think they're, like, they have
adapted to new trends many times.

02:05:28.187 --> 02:05:31.649
And so I think there's, like,
I've been very impressed, actually,

02:05:31.732 --> 02:05:35.778
by seeing how
quickly a lot of Meta leadership

02:05:36.987 --> 02:05:40.282
If I compare the kind of conversations I
would have with them when I first joined

02:05:40.366 --> 02:05:41.659
versus the kind
of conversations I have now—

02:05:41.742 --> 02:05:43.077
They're now super AI-built.
Yeah.

02:05:43.160 --> 02:05:47.623
And it's very impressive, honestly,
for that to happen at the large scale

02:05:47.873 --> 02:05:50.417
of Meta, which is one of the largest
companies in the world.

02:05:51.669 --> 02:05:53.295
Yeah, we got them there pretty quickly.

02:05:53.379 --> 02:05:55.923
It was, yeah, but I mean,
like you're saying, it's like if

02:05:56.257 --> 02:06:00.177
you start coding, then it's helpful.

02:06:01.762 --> 02:06:05.683
But I think a big part of what,
a big part of the culture that we were

02:06:05.766 --> 02:06:11.855
trying to create is also, you know,
when we were talking about how you have

02:06:11.939 --> 02:06:15.568
all these direct reports,
the point of it isn't that

02:06:16.652 --> 02:06:20.364
you have the direct reports,
it's that there aren't, it's the absence

02:06:20.447 --> 02:06:22.741
of non-technical managers, right?

02:06:22.825 --> 02:06:25.160
So,
so I don't think you would say that you're

02:06:25.244 --> 02:06:28.664
like, you know, that you're providing
a ton of managerial support

02:06:28.747 --> 02:06:29.957
to 200 people at once.

02:06:30.040 --> 02:06:31.500
I mean, that's not a feature of the thing.

02:06:31.584 --> 02:06:36.005
Alex, well known for— Career chats and
emotional, how are you doing, checking.

02:06:36.505 --> 02:06:39.842
But I think it's just well known among
